\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: SNB May Start Raising Rates In March -\uc2 BNP Paribas\b0\par\par\uc2 1,457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2010\par 09:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0811 GMT [Dow Jones]--The SNB will probably start raising rates in March, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "While rates look inappropriate given the strength of the economy, the strength of the CHF has delayed the rate hikes," BNP says, referring to SNB's 3-Mos Libor target of 0.25%. BNP notes that SNB President recently sounded more on the hawkish side, talking about the declining deflation risk and pointing out that the overheating property market isn't compatible with loose monetary policy. (anita.greil@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0807 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may fall sharply if Japanese retail investors exit their ballooning JPY short positions, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. It adds, short JPY positions held by Japanese retail investors on the Tokyo Financial Exchange reached record levels Tuesday at Y507 billion, estimating this at 10% of the wider Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  margin trading market. JPY shorts against the USD are now "the most popular trade" among Japanese retail investors, likely due to the yen's historically strong level and possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on further strengthening. However, it says the USD/JPY's recent falls made these retail investors vulnerable. With regulations allowing 50 times leverage, if stops are triggered in thin holiday trading, "the risk of a sudden yen-positive capitulation is very real." Still, the house re-affirms its 1-month and 3-month forecasts for the USD/JPY at 85.00. The pair is at 81.59.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0756 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/SEK is set to trade between 8.97 and 9.02 Thursday as Wednesday's range holds, says SEB. "Upside pressure is likely" after Tuesday's "bullish engulfing candle together with yesterday's downside spike", adds the bank. EUR/SEK trades at 8.9861.(charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0752 GMT [Dow Jones] Hang Seng Bank expects China GDP growth to moderate to 8.5% in 2011 from an expected 10% in 2010 as a result of the recent accelerated pace of monetary tightening, the lingering of the sovereign debt problem in Europe and the fragile U.S. economic recovery. "With inflation and wages rising and signs of a rebound in property market activities emerging recently, more increases in the RRR and interest rates can be expected in coming months," the bank says in a report. It expects the PBOC to raise rates three times in early 2011 with a 25 bp hike each time. It also expects China inflation to hit 4.5% in 2011 from an expected 3.3% this year on higher food prices. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0736 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds are mixed late in holiday-thinned trade, with focus again on Bank Negara notes (about MYR1.5 billion worth transacted thus far), says a dealer with a local bank. "There's really no clear direction in the bond market today with scattered trade in both on the run and off the run paper," says the dealer; he tips the yield curve to bear steepen next year given the bigger pipeline of longer-tenored paper to be auctioned by Bank Negara in 2011, and the likely resumption of policy rate hikes in the second half of 2011. The yield on the May 2013 MGS is down 2 bps at 3.11%, the yield on the August 2015 MGS is up 1 bp at 3.37% and the yield on the November 2019 MGS is down 1 bp at 4.03%.(elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0735 GMT [Dow Jones] January HSI futures are +0.4% at 23,099, roughly in line with the cash market's 0.4% gain. The contract is trading at about 30-point premium-to-cash, indicating positive bias for near-term direction. Volume is modest at 46,984 lots. December HSI futures are less indicative, as the contract is going to expire after market close today. They are down 0.1% at 22,988, in very light volume of 10,898 lots. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0726 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end slightly higher as the liquidity squeeze eases a tad after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injects a net CNY35 billion into the money market this week through its regular open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The money market's tight liquidity will ease further next week when the year-end strong demand for cash ends," says a Shanghai-based trader with a local broker. The Shanghai exchange government bond index ends at 126.29 vs 126.28 last close, and is tipped to hover around the current level tomorrow as most investors are likely to stay on the sidelines in the last session of the year. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0723 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swiss franc looks set to strenghten further against the dollar and the euro, \uc2 UBS\uc2  technical analyst says. USD/CHF has support at 0.9321. If that's breached, the dollar could drop as low as CHF0.9202. The pair currently trades at 0.9428. Outlook for EUR/CHF is also bearish, with the focus on support at 1.2439. If that's breached, euro could drop to CHF1.2283, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. EUR/CHF currently trades at 1.2485. (anita.greil@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0717 GMT [Dow Jones] March bunds open 0.59 higher at 125.36 and the 10-year bund yields 2.956%, down from 3.017% Wednesday afternoon, after a healthy response to an auction of 7-year U.S. Treasurys, which pushed U.S. yields lower Wednesday. Nevertheless, Richard Adcock at \uc2 UBS\uc2  reckons bunds "are vulnerable while they trade below the 125.68 high and as long as this remains the case, the expectation is for limited bounces and further price weakness." (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0710 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Malaysia's exports in November are likely to rise 5.4% on-year vs a 1.3% increase in October, according to the median forecast of 11 economists in a Dow Jones poll. "Trade should remain on an upward trajectory against a backdrop of data suggesting that global demand is regaining traction," says David Cohen, an economist at Action Economics in Singapore. Kuala Lumpur-based Affin Investment Bank economist Alan Tan concurred, adding that demand from major Asian markets like China and the Asean region--which contracted for the first time in October--is expected to have improved in November thanks to highly sought after commodity products and electronics, as well as the recovery in the global economy. The same poll tips November imports to rise 9.8% on-year vs a 12.5% gain in October; Malaysia's November trade surplus is tipped at MYR6.9 billion vs MYR6.8 billion in October. The trade data are scheduled for release at 1001 GMT Monday. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0705 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is down 0.1% at 1522.91 in afternoon trade as investors book profits after recent gains. "The market has been trading sideways for some time now. I still think we might end in the positive territory as today is the last trading day of the year. Traders will try to end on a good note," a local dealer says. He tips the KLCI to trade in a 1520-1530 range for the rest of the session. Malaysia's financial markets will be closed tomorrow for a public holiday. Market breadth is negative with 381 decliners vs 201 gainers. \uc2 IJM Land\uc2  (5215.KU) is down 11.8% at MYR2.86 and \uc2 Malaysian Resources\uc2  (1651.KU) is down 2.9% at MYR1.99 before being suspended from trading on news the planned merger has been called off. Among gainers \uc2 Gamuda Bhd\uc2  (5398.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR3.83 and Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) is up 0.6% at MYR3.43. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0700 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is down 0.1% at 22,953.92, as trading remains lackluster ahead of year-end with volumes modest at HK$32.85 billion, says Mark To, head of research at Wing Fung Financial. He expects the index to be in a 22,900-23,100 range. "I think December HSI futures can close at around 23,000 today (when expired), which is likely to keep the cash market around the same level." Coal companies are outperforming on rising demand for coal during the winter period with China Shenhua (1088.HK) up 0.2% at HK$32.30 and China Coal (1898.HK) up 0.5% at HK$12.08. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com) [ 12-30-10 0311ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101230e6cu0004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
