\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: STI +0.4%; 2011 Target At 3600 - S&P Equity\b0\par\par\uc2 1,909 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2010\par 07:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0659 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's benchmark STI +0.4% at 3171.75, with all but two index components in positive territory, led by Genting Singapore (G13.SG), +1.9% at S$2.16, and Golden Agri (E5H.SG), +1.3% at S$0.785. The STI has traded in a narrow 12-point range so far, beyond its 3174 intraday high, analysts expect 3200 then 3220 as near term resistance. S&P's Equity Research, which has a 2011 STI target of 3600, says next year "if we are to refer to the prior liquidity bubble, chances are decent that the stock market could surprise on the upside." Notes, rising interest rates, the euro-zone debt crisis remain risks but "current market valuations are discounting these issues." Volume today--at 585 million shares--overtakes yesterday's, though 155 million of those are in the normally thinly traded Hotung Investment (H34.SG), +3.7% at S$0.14, which is yet to answer a query from the SGX on the unusually high volume. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0656 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is up 0.6% at 1520.88 in afternoon trade, with financial stocks leading gains. Volumes are thin with only 475 million shares changing hands so far. "Window-dressing activities from fund managers could take the market higher later in the week, but for now it isn't likely to move too much," a local dealer says; he tips a 1515-1530 range. Market breadth is positive with 341 gainers compared with 303 decliners. \uc2 AMMB Holdings\uc2  (1015.KU) is up 1.9% at MYR7.08, CIMB Group (1023.KU) is up 1.4% at MYR8.69 and Public Bank (1295.KU) is up 1.1% at MYR12.94. Among decliners, \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) is down 0.4% at MYR2.82 and \uc2 Telekom Malaysia\uc2  (4863.KU) is down 0.3% at MYR3.50. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0650 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume is down 9.4% at HK$1.18 billion at the interval, with the percentage of the market's total at 5.0%, vs 5.6% at Friday's half-day, suggesting any further downside for the Hang Seng Index (down 0.9% midday) may be limited. A50 (2823.HK) leads with HK$257.08 million in shorts, then HKEx (0388.HK) with HK$103.35 million, China Life (2628.HK) with HK$50.44 million, Ping An (2318.HK) with HK$38.03 and DB X-Trackers (3049.HK) with HK$26.70 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end down 0.2% at 8870.76, erasing an earlier 0.3% gain as China shares fall sharply and most financial shares succumb to profit-taking after recent gains, says Grand Cathay Securities analyst Mars Hsu. He adds, investors are becoming more cautious as the benchmark index approaches 9000, with the trading volume (NT$101.3 billion) remaining thin. "If the volume cannot exceed the 30-day average of NT$136 billion, the benchmark index is likely to test the downside again." He tips the Taiex to find solid support at its 10-day moving-average of 8833 with resistance at 8920. Cathay Financial (2882.TW) closed down 0.8% at NT$48.40 and Fubon Financial (2881.TW) ended down 0.8% at NT$38.35. He adds, some newly-listed tech shares face some selling pressure from their underwriting brokerages who want to dress up their books for the year-end; TPK (3673.TW) closed down 2.4% at NT$680 and MStar Semiconductor (3697.TW) ended down 2.3% at NT$296. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Despite moderate inflation, Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely raise its key policy interest rate 12.5 bps to 1.650% from 1.500%, according 11 out of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones poll; the 12th economist expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep rates on hold. If a third-consecutive rate hike materializes, it would come as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seeks to normalize its lending costs from super-low levels and to help cool the sizzling property market, say economists. \uc2 UBS\uc2  economist Kivin Hsiao says, based on his forecast CPI will rise 1.5%-2.0% next year, "the local real interest rate will likely turn negative next year" if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds off raising the interest rate Thursday, and "that's quite a concern to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." The bank is scheduled to announce its decision 0800 GMT Thursday. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0606 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are little changed, up 0.03% at 1019.79 at the midday break in choppy trade; volume is sluggish as the bulk of investors are on the sidelines for the year-end holidays. "There's no any fresh leads to drive the market, so the (Thai) stock market will likely move sideways throughout the rest of the year," says Anupon Sriard, an analyst with BFIT Securities. Immediate support is tipped at 1018 and then 1010, while resistance is at 1025. Among the most active stocks, \uc2 True Corp\uc2 . (TRUE.TH) jumps 4.6% to THB6.85 on news the Cabinet agreed to cancel state-owned \uc2 CAT Telecom\uc2 's plan to take over the Thai business and assets of Hong Kong's Hutchison Telecommunications International Ltd.; \uc2 True Corp\uc2 . is weighing offering to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . IRPC (IRPC.TH) is up 1.7% at THB5.90, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) is off 1.4% at THB103, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) is off 0.9% at THB2.28 and \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) is up 0.8% at THB24.70. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0601 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional stock markets are mixed; the Nikkei is down 0.5%, the Shanghai Composite is 0.8% lower, the Kospi is up 0.7%, the STI climbs 0.3%, the KLCI gains 0.6%, the HSI is down 0.9%, the Taiex is off 0.2% and the Sensex is flat. Australia and New Zealand markets are shut. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the USD fell to a 3-week low vs the JPY at 82.41 on speculation that the 5-year U.S. Treasury auction later in the global day may meet strong demand. The USD/JPY is at 82.43 vs 82.84 in late NY trade Monday, the EUR/JPY is at 109.08 vs 108.88 and the EUR/USD is at 1.3236 vs 1.3147. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Noda threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as the yen marked 3-week highs against the USD. Japan's November industrial output rose 1.0% on-month seasonally-adjusted, in-line with economists' expectations; the government tips December output to rise 3.4% and January output to increase 3.7%. The nation's November core CPI fell 0.5% on-year vs October's 0.6% and 0.6% expected; November retail sales rose 1.3% on-year vs October's 0.2% fall. November household spending fell 0.4% on-year vs October's 0.4% decline; the November jobless rate is at 5.1% seasonally-adjusted vs October's 5.1%. Spot gold is at $1,391.50/oz, up $7.40 from its New York close Monday while February Nymex crude oil futures are up 12 cents at $91.12/bbl. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW continues to trade marginally lower at 1,147.60 from 1,149.00 late Monday in Seoul as both the Kospi and the EUR hold on to solid gains, supporting the local unit, traders say. The Kospi is up 0.75. A local bank trader pegs the pair's support for the day at 1,146, adding exporters' USD selling appears to be slightly outweighing corporate USD demand. Traders note, offshore players are not providing leads for the KRW today; "offshore players have hardly been showing any movements for a few days now. I've heard that there's been hardly any trading activity in the NDFs market," during New York market hours recently, says another trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0536 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets notes that in 2010, Japanese equities significantly underperformed their counterparts in the U.S., Europe, and the rest of Asia, due largely to the yen's dramatic appreciation, but that in dollar terms, Japanese stocks actually held their own against the other three major regions. The brokerage says while growth stocks have recently turned in relatively strong performances, there is no clear performance trend according to market cap. In a research report, a Daiwa analyst says Japanese growth stocks typically rally when JPY falls vs USD, the yield on 10-year Treasuries is rising, and the U.S. unemployment rate is improving. "Should the U.S. show clearer visibility on a path to economic recovery, Japanese stocks could become the top performer among major markets," the analyst writes. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0530 GMT [Dow Jones] India's interbank call rates are lower as liquidity conditions show some signs of improvement after last week's record borrowing, says a trader in a private-bank. The overnight call rate is at 6.70% from 6.90% last close, while the collateralized borrowing and lending obligation rate is at 6.26% from 6.30% at open. "Most banks are covered for their product needs. Last week we had a (RBI bond) buyback and tomorrow there is another one. Some cash is back in the system," the trader says. She tips the call rate in a 6.70%-6.95% band for the session. Indian lenders on Monday borrowed INR1.34 trillion from the RBI's discount lending window (at 6.25%) vs last week's daily average borrowing in excess of INR1.5 trillion. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0530 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei 225 March futures are down 0.3% at 10,320 on a firmer yen (USD/JPY at 82.51) and weakness in Shanghai shares while volume remains low (less than 13,300 contracts made thus far). "But futures are relatively solid because there are still hopes that shares will rise the (remaining) year-end trading sessions," says Phoenix Securities manager Mamoru Nakajo. But he puts resistance at 10,420, and says the futures index is unlikely to reach 10,500 as many expect. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0521 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares are +1.1% midday at 3666.203 in moderate volume as foreign funds continue to buy select consumer and commodity-related stocks on hopes for strong 4Q earnings, traders say; traders expect the main index to top 3680. "I think the main index could close the year above 3700," says a trader at Erdhika Securities. Foreigners are net buyers of IDR156 billion worth of shares. Among gainers: coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) is +3.4% at IDR3,075, nickel miner Inco (INCO.JK) is +1.6% at IDR4,775, while cigarette maker Garam (GGRM.JK) is +2.8% at IDR40,100. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com) [ 12-28-10 0159ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101228e6cs0005p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
