\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: HK Short Sales Down 9.4% Midday; A50 Tops List\b0\par\par\uc2 1,902 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2010\par 07:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0650 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume is down 9.4% at HK$1.18 billion at the interval, with the percentage of the market's total at 5.0%, vs 5.6% at Friday's half-day, suggesting any further downside for the Hang Seng Index (down 0.9% midday) may be limited. A50 (2823.HK) leads with HK$257.08 million in shorts, then HKEx (0388.HK) with HK$103.35 million, China Life (2628.HK) with HK$50.44 million, Ping An (2318.HK) with HK$38.03 and DB X-Trackers (3049.HK) with HK$26.70 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end down 0.2% at 8870.76, erasing an earlier 0.3% gain as China shares fall sharply and most financial shares succumb to profit-taking after recent gains, says Grand Cathay Securities analyst Mars Hsu. He adds, investors are becoming more cautious as the benchmark index approaches 9000, with the trading volume (NT$101.3 billion) remaining thin. "If the volume cannot exceed the 30-day average of NT$136 billion, the benchmark index is likely to test the downside again." He tips the Taiex to find solid support at its 10-day moving-average of 8833 with resistance at 8920. Cathay Financial (2882.TW) closed down 0.8% at NT$48.40 and Fubon Financial (2881.TW) ended down 0.8% at NT$38.35. He adds, some newly-listed tech shares face some selling pressure from their underwriting brokerages who want to dress up their books for the year-end; TPK (3673.TW) closed down 2.4% at NT$680 and MStar Semiconductor (3697.TW) ended down 2.3% at NT$296. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Despite moderate inflation, Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely raise its key policy interest rate 12.5 bps to 1.650% from 1.500%, according 11 out of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones poll; the 12th economist expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep rates on hold. If a third-consecutive rate hike materializes, it would come as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seeks to normalize its lending costs from super-low levels and to help cool the sizzling property market, say economists. \uc2 UBS\uc2  economist Kivin Hsiao says, based on his forecast CPI will rise 1.5%-2.0% next year, "the local real interest rate will likely turn negative next year" if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds off raising the interest rate Thursday, and "that's quite a concern to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." The bank is scheduled to announce its decision 0800 GMT Thursday. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0606 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are little changed, up 0.03% at 1019.79 at the midday break in choppy trade; volume is sluggish as the bulk of investors are on the sidelines for the year-end holidays. "There's no any fresh leads to drive the market, so the (Thai) stock market will likely move sideways throughout the rest of the year," says Anupon Sriard, an analyst with BFIT Securities. Immediate support is tipped at 1018 and then 1010, while resistance is at 1025. Among the most active stocks, \uc2 True Corp\uc2 . (TRUE.TH) jumps 4.6% to THB6.85 on news the Cabinet agreed to cancel state-owned \uc2 CAT Telecom\uc2 's plan to take over the Thai business and assets of Hong Kong's Hutchison Telecommunications International Ltd.; \uc2 True Corp\uc2 . is weighing offering to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . IRPC (IRPC.TH) is up 1.7% at THB5.90, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) is off 1.4% at THB103, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) is off 0.9% at THB2.28 and \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) is up 0.8% at THB24.70. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0601 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional stock markets are mixed; the Nikkei is down 0.5%, the Shanghai Composite is 0.8% lower, the Kospi is up 0.7%, the STI climbs 0.3%, the KLCI gains 0.6%, the HSI is down 0.9%, the Taiex is off 0.2% and the Sensex is flat. Australia and New Zealand markets are shut. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the USD fell to a 3-week low vs the JPY at 82.41 on speculation that the 5-year U.S. Treasury auction later in the global day may meet strong demand. The USD/JPY is at 82.43 vs 82.84 in late NY trade Monday, the EUR/JPY is at 109.08 vs 108.88 and the EUR/USD is at 1.3236 vs 1.3147. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Noda threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as the yen marked 3-week highs against the USD. Japan's November industrial output rose 1.0% on-month seasonally-adjusted, in-line with economists' expectations; the government tips December output to rise 3.4% and January output to increase 3.7%. The nation's November core CPI fell 0.5% on-year vs October's 0.6% and 0.6% expected; November retail sales rose 1.3% on-year vs October's 0.2% fall. November household spending fell 0.4% on-year vs October's 0.4% decline; the November jobless rate is at 5.1% seasonally-adjusted vs October's 5.1%. Spot gold is at $1,391.50/oz, up $7.40 from its New York close Monday while February Nymex crude oil futures are up 12 cents at $91.12/bbl. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW continues to trade marginally lower at 1,147.60 from 1,149.00 late Monday in Seoul as both the Kospi and the EUR hold on to solid gains, supporting the local unit, traders say. The Kospi is up 0.75. A local bank trader pegs the pair's support for the day at 1,146, adding exporters' USD selling appears to be slightly outweighing corporate USD demand. Traders note, offshore players are not providing leads for the KRW today; "offshore players have hardly been showing any movements for a few days now. I've heard that there's been hardly any trading activity in the NDFs market," during New York market hours recently, says another trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0536 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets notes that in 2010, Japanese equities significantly underperformed their counterparts in the U.S., Europe, and the rest of Asia, due largely to the yen's dramatic appreciation, but that in dollar terms, Japanese stocks actually held their own against the other three major regions. The brokerage says while growth stocks have recently turned in relatively strong performances, there is no clear performance trend according to market cap. In a research report, a Daiwa analyst says Japanese growth stocks typically rally when JPY falls vs USD, the yield on 10-year Treasuries is rising, and the U.S. unemployment rate is improving. "Should the U.S. show clearer visibility on a path to economic recovery, Japanese stocks could become the top performer among major markets," the analyst writes. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0530 GMT [Dow Jones] India's interbank call rates are lower as liquidity conditions show some signs of improvement after last week's record borrowing, says a trader in a private-bank. The overnight call rate is at 6.70% from 6.90% last close, while the collateralized borrowing and lending obligation rate is at 6.26% from 6.30% at open. "Most banks are covered for their product needs. Last week we had a (RBI bond) buyback and tomorrow there is another one. Some cash is back in the system," the trader says. She tips the call rate in a 6.70%-6.95% band for the session. Indian lenders on Monday borrowed INR1.34 trillion from the RBI's discount lending window (at 6.25%) vs last week's daily average borrowing in excess of INR1.5 trillion. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0530 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei 225 March futures are down 0.3% at 10,320 on a firmer yen (USD/JPY at 82.51) and weakness in Shanghai shares while volume remains low (less than 13,300 contracts made thus far). "But futures are relatively solid because there are still hopes that shares will rise the (remaining) year-end trading sessions," says Phoenix Securities manager Mamoru Nakajo. But he puts resistance at 10,420, and says the futures index is unlikely to reach 10,500 as many expect. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0521 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares are +1.1% midday at 3666.203 in moderate volume as foreign funds continue to buy select consumer and commodity-related stocks on hopes for strong 4Q earnings, traders say; traders expect the main index to top 3680. "I think the main index could close the year above 3700," says a trader at Erdhika Securities. Foreigners are net buyers of IDR156 billion worth of shares. Among gainers: coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) is +3.4% at IDR3,075, nickel miner Inco (INCO.JK) is +1.6% at IDR4,775, while cigarette maker Garam (GGRM.JK) is +2.8% at IDR40,100. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0454 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds and bond futures are modestly higher in thin trade, with foreign buying in futures, though relatively light in volume, helping bond sentiment amid a lack of other decisive leads, participants say. The five-year yield is quoted down 5 bps at 4.02%, while the 10-year yield is down 2 bps at 4.49%. March futures are up 10 ticks at 103.66. A bond manager with a securities firm pegs futures to be capped at 103.80, adding, after starting the session strong tracking modest gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight, local bonds haven't moved much amid a lack of momentum on the domestic front. He notes, the yield curve is flattening amid hopes of an increase in the relative proportion of shorter-bonds' issuance compared with longer ones early next year. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0449 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI is +0.3% at 3168.90 midday with 378 million shares traded worth s$320.6 million. Singapore's market may continue to edge up on window-dressing until the new year, when talk of a January rally is likely to take centre stage; "with the STI down 5% from the year's high, fund managers who took an extended holiday (in a very quiet December) should be raring to go when opening their books next week," says Najeeb Jarhom, a technical analyst at AmFraser. He adds, "their strategy could be to push the market up first to reflect the Capricorn effect but traders should also brace for volatility of which January is notorious for." Palm stocks are mostly higher, with \uc2 Golden Agri-Resources\uc2  (E5H.SG) +1.9% at S$0.790 and First Resources (EB5.SG) +2.6% at S$1.57. Keppel (BN4.SG) gains 0.6% at S$10.94 in low volume after announcing new contracts. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com) [ 12-28-10 0150ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101228e6cs0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
