\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares End Mixed; China Property, Financial Plays Fall\b0\par\par\uc2 1,118 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2010\par 11:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stock markets ended mixed Tuesday, with the property and financial sectors weighing on shares in Hong Kong and China, while the strong yen pressured Tokyo's market.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index dropped 1.7%, extending the previous session's 1.9% slide, to end at its lowest closing level in nearly three months, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index shed 0.9%. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average fell 0.6%, while South Korea's Kospi Composite added 0.6%. Markets in Australia and New Zealand were shut for a holiday.\par \par \uc2 Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were 16 points higher in screen trade.\par \par \uc2 China property and financial plays lost ground amid concerns the People's Bank of China will continue to tighten monetary policy after it reiterated Monday the need to prioritize price stability in the nation.\par \par \uc2 Despite the strength of the country's economy, China "faces arduous tasks in managing money, credit and liquidity and in preventing financial risks," the PBOC said in a statement Monday.\par \par \uc2 A 21st Century Business Herald report Tuesday cited \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser Li Daokui as saying China must introduce further tightening measures in 2011, particularly in the first half, to rein in rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 "The PBOC's emphasis again on controlling prices will intensify tightening concerns and are hitting the already-weak market sentiment, especially following the weekend interest rate hike," said Zhang Xin, an analyst at Guotai Junan Securities.\par \par \uc2 On the mainland, \uc2 China Vanke\uc2 , the nation's largest property developer by market share, declined 4.8% and \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  fell 6.2%, while in Hong Kong, China Overseas Land fell 2.3% and \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  retreated 1.6%.\par \par \uc2 Among financial stocks, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2 's Hong Kong shares fell 1.5% and its Shanghai-listed ones shed 1.7%. Among brokerages, Shanghai-listed Citic Securities shed 2.1% and Everbright Secturities lost 3.1%.\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, liquefied natural gas distributor Kunlun Energy fell 3.2% after it agreed to buy a 60% stake in \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  Beijing Gas Pipeline from parent \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  for CNY18.9 billion. Oil producer \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  also retreated 1.6%.\par \par \uc2 Weaker Chinese shares coupled with a stronger yen weighed on Tokyo's market, but the decline was limited by industrial output forecasts provided by the government before the market open that projected a 3.4% rise in output in December and a 3.7% gain in January.\par \par \uc2 "The data will alleviate concerns that the Japanese economy, now at a standstill, will decline further," said Mizuho Investors Securities senior strategist Masatoshi Sato.\par \par \uc2 While few believe the dollar-yen pair will go back to the 80 level, the stronger yen is holding back stock investment sentiment for now, said Yutaka Yoshii, general manager at \uc2 Mito Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Among major exporters, Canon lost 1.7% and \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  dropped 1.1%. Advantest also fell 1.6% as concerns about lingering weakness in the DRAM tester market also weighed.\par \par \uc2 On the upside, \uc2 Mizuho Financial Group\uc2  added 1.3%, supported by president and chief executive officer \uc2 Takashi Tsukamoto\uc2 's comments in a Dow Jones Newswires interview that the company's efforts to meet capital adequacy requirements were progressing and there was no need to issue additional new shares.\par \par \uc2 Seoul's shares gained, boosted by a rebound in technology stocks, with \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  gaining 1.7%, \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  rising 3.7% and LG Display adding 2.4%.\par \par \uc2 Construction plays rallied on hopes for better earnings next year, with \uc2 Daewoo Engineering\uc2  & Construction tacking on 5.0%, GS Engineering & Construction surging 9.6% and \uc2 Daelim Industrial\uc2  jumping 5.0%.\par \par \uc2 "There are likely to be more plant orders from the Middle East," said Han Seok-su, another analyst at Solomon Investment & Securities.\par \par \uc2 The government's recent measures to re-vitalize the domestic housing market, including plans to abolish housing price caps in many areas except for Seoul, are also expected to help builders, said IBK Securities analyst Yoon Jin-il. "When the measures take effect, possibly some time next year, their earnings from new domestic housing projects will improve."\par \par \uc2 Hyundai Elevator soared by a daily limit high of 15% after Schindler Deutschland GmbH, a Germany-based affiliate of Switzerland's \uc2 Schindler Holding AG\uc2 , increased its stake in the Korean elevator company to 35.27% % from 33.40%.\par \par \uc2 Among other regional markets, Taiwan's Taiex shed 0.2%, Philippine shares edged down 0.1%, Malaysia's KLCI tacked on 0.4%, Singapore's Straits Times Index rose 0.8%, Thailand's SET index gained 0.9% and Indonesian shares rose 1.0%.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the dollar touched a three-week low against the yen on expectations an auction of five-year U.S. Treasurys later in the day may meet strong demand, which would likely weigh on yields. The dollar was fetching Y82.10 from Y82.84 in late New York trade Monday, while the euro was at $1.3244, from $1.3147, and Y108.75 from Y108.88.\par \par \uc2 If there is indeed strong demand for the five-year securities, the U.S. dollar could extend its losses, falling as low as Y81.00, said Hideki Amikura, a senior dealer at Nomura Trust and Banking.\par \par \uc2 "Eighty one yen sounds a bit too far, but given the holiday-thinned market, it's actually quite possible," he said.\par \par \uc2 The strength of the yen caught the attention of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister, who threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb its strength.\par \par \uc2 "Over recent days, or the past week or so, (the yen) has been showing one-sided movement," Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said at a news conference after a regular Cabinet meeting. "Our stance is unchanged. We will take decisive steps if there is excessive volatility."\par \par \uc2 But doubts remain over whether Tokyo will carry out its threat anytime soon, partly because the Group of 20 industrial and developing countries have pledged to let market forces determine exchange rates, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,390.70 per troy ounce, up $6.60 from its New York close Monday while February Nymex crude oil futures were up 11 cents at $91.11 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 -Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; markettalk@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 28-12-10 1059GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101228e6cs000fu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
