\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Profiles of Bank of Japan policy board members\b0\par\par\uc2 1,097 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 2010\par 00:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2   (For more stories on the Japanese economy, click [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 21 (Reuters) - Following are profiles of the Bank of Japan's policy board members.\par \par \uc2  MASAAKI SHIRAKAWA, 61, governor\par \par \uc2  A career central banker who took the helm in 2008, Shirakawa led the implementation of unconventional steps including corporate debt buying during the global financial crisis and guided the BOJ out of the programme as recession ended.\par \par \uc2  Shirakawa has repeatedly warned that too narrow a focus on short-term prices can lead to asset bubbles, shrugging off calls by politicians for the BOJ to set a rigid inflation target. He also argues that weak demand is the root cause of deflation, which means flooding markets with cash alone will not work.\par \par \uc2  But he helmed surprise decisions on Oct. 5 to set up a 5 trillion yen ($60 billion) asset buying programme to pump more money into the struggling economy and to peg interest rates virtually at zero until price stability is in sight.\par \par \uc2  He has said expanding the fund would be a strong option if the economy deviated sharply from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's forecast, but said last month he still saw upside and downside risks to the economy as roughly in balance. [ID:nTOE6AS02Y]\par \par \uc2  HIROHIDE YAMAGUCHI, 59, deputy governor\par \par \uc2  A central banker for more than 30 years, Yamaguchi was a BOJ executive director when he was appointed to his current post in October 2008.\par \par \uc2  Yamaguchi, whose views are thought to be close to those of Shirakawa, has mostly toed the BOJ's official line on monetary policy. He is regarded as mainly in charge of communicating the BOJ's view to the government and ruling party lawmakers.\par \par \uc2  Yamaguchi last month warned of looming risks to Japan's economy and very high uncertainty about the U.S. economic outlook, striking a slightly more downbeat tone than Governor Shirakawa. [ID:nTOE6A700Y]\par \par \uc2  KIYOHIKO NISHIMURA, 57, deputy governor\par \par \uc2  A former professor at the University of Tokyo and a statistics expert, Nishimura joined the BOJ board in 2005 and was appointed a deputy governor in March 2008.\par \par \uc2  He has voted with the majority on policy decisions and mostly toed the bank's official line.\par \par \uc2  Nishimura said in October that the yen's rise was a major factor that may drive down the economy through a worsening of business sentiment, while it may also push down consumer prices. [ID:nTOE69J02D]\par \par \uc2  HIDETOSHI KAMEZAKI, 67\par \par \uc2  Kamezaki, a former senior executive vice president of Japan's biggest trading firm, \uc2 Mitsubishi Corp\uc2  , joined the board in April 2007. He had broad overseas experience at the trading house.\par \par \uc2  In July, he expressed a cautious view on Japan's economy, saying it lacked strength and could stall temporarily in the fourth quarter, but adding that the BOJ would not set policy with specific \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels in mind. [ID:nTOE66R02U]\par \par \uc2  In October 2008, he was among four dissenters when the BOJ cut rates to 0.3 percent, calling instead for a cut to 0.25 percent. He has voted with the board and toed the BOJ's official line since then.\par \par \uc2  SEIJI NAKAMURA, 68\par \par \uc2  Nakamura, who joined the board in April 2007, was formerly head of a unit of Japanese ocean freight firm \uc2 Mitsui O.S.K\uc2 . Lines . He worked mostly in the finance and planning sections.\par \par \uc2  Nakamura said last month that downside risks to the Japanese economy outweighed upside risks due to growing uncertainties in the U.S. and European economies, offering a bleaker assessment than Governor Shirakawa. [ID:nTOE6AN060]\par \par \uc2  He dissented when the BOJ cut rates to 0.3 percent in October 2008, favouring instead a cut to 0.25 percent. He has followed the BOJ's official line since then.\par \par \uc2  MIYAKO SUDA, 62\par \par \uc2  A former economics professor, Suda was reappointed for a second term in April 2006 and is the longest-serving member of the current board, with her term set to expire next March.\par \par \uc2  An expert on international finance, she has warned of the drawbacks of keeping policy loose for too long and has stressed the need for structural economic reform to achieve growth.\par \par \uc2  Suda opposed buying government bonds as part of the BOJ's asset buying scheme unveiled in October, and has said that any topping up of the asset purchasing fund should focus on funnelling money into risk assets. [ID:nTOE6B002F]\par \par \uc2  TADAO NODA, 63\par \par \uc2  A banking industry veteran who joined the board in June 2006, Noda was the sole dissenter when the BOJ cut interest rates to 0.1 percent in December 2008, arguing that pushing rates that low could distort market functions.\par \par \uc2  He mostly supported the adoption of unconventional steps taken during the global financial crisis but along with Suda he opposed the BOJ's additional easing on March 17.\par \par \uc2  On Sept. 15, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out its first yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in six years, Noda said the BOJ would not link its policy actions directly to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nTKZ006543]\par \par \uc2  RYUZO MIYAO, 46\par \par \uc2  A lifelong academic and an expert on monetary policy, Miyao joined the board in March. He is known for his research on inflation targeting and some analysts say he doubts the effectiveness of such a policy. [ID:nT259620]\par \par \uc2  Miyao said on Sept. 22 that an increase in the BOJ's buying of Japanese government bonds (JGBs) was one policy option and that assessments of quantitative easing now could be different than in the past. [ID:nTOE68L01P]\par \par \uc2  He said in an interview with the Asahi newspaper in August that if the yen's rise was sustained it could affect the BOJ's main scenario for a moderate recovery in Japan's economy. [ID:nTOE67206X]\par \par \uc2 YOSHIHISA MORIMOTO\uc2 , 66\par \par \uc2  Morimoto, a former executive at \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\uc2  , Asia's biggest power utility, joined the board on July 1, bringing it to its full complement of nine members for the first time in more than two years. He has voted with the majority of the board since then.\par \par \uc2  Morimoto said earlier this month that Japanese bond yields had not risen enough to seriously harm an economy that is set to resume an export-led recovery, suggesting that more monetary easing is not imminent. [ID:nTOE6B802U]\par \par \uc2  He also sounded cautious about the BOJ buying more risk assets, a sign that he may prefer purchases of government bonds to private debt if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to top up its asset buying fund.  ($1=83.94 Yen)  (Reporting by Rie Ishiguro and Leika Kihara)     \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ-PROFILES (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101220e6ck001gm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
