\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  did not conduct  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  in Nov -MOF\b0\par\par\uc2 217 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2010\par 11:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 30 (Reuters) - Japanese authorities did not conduct any \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between Oct. 28 and Nov. 26, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Japan stepped in to sell yen on Sept. 15 for the first time in more than six years after the dollar slid to a 15-year low against the yen, selling a record 2.1249 trillion yen, or $25.3 billion worth of yen, on that day alone.\par \par \uc2 Although the dollar continued to fall later that month and in October, the MOF has said it did not conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on any other day in September and nor did it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a 15-year low of 80.21 yen on Nov. 1, coming within a squeak of its record low of 79.75 yen set in 1995, but has since recovered to about 84.00 yen <JPY=>.\par \par \uc2 Traders say further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this stage is unlikely unless the dollar tumbles steeply against the yen but even then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains controversial after G20 nations agreed in October to avoid competitive devaluations. (Reporting by Stanley White; Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (URGENT)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101130e6bu000k5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
