\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/HKD Off Morning High; But Biased Up - Trader\b0\par\par\uc2 1,642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2010\par 07:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0651 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD off morning high on outright selling by local banks, but retains upward bias on risk aversion, says senior trader at Singapore bank. Tips pair in 7.7530-7.7570 band near-term. "The rebound in the U.S. dollar offshore, as well as the fall in local stocks could trigger some outflows, lending support to the pair," trader says. HSI down 0.8%. Pair last at 7.7554 vs 7.7563 this morning, compared with late 7.7530 yesterday.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may rise to 86.00 in coming weeks, level where pair rose to in wake of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's September \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  senior dealer Tsutomu Soma. Adds, technical levels with "great amount of" stop-loss buying orders at 84.00, 85.00, then 86.00. Tailwinds to USD include falling commodity prices, rising U.S. Treasury yields, lingering euro-zone debt concerns, he says. "There's no doubt that the dollar will remain strong for the time being." USD/JPY last at 83.43. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0613 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Nikkei +0.1%, S&P/ASX down 1.6%, HSI down 0.7%, Kospi down 0.1%, Taiex down 0.7%; Shanghai Composite down 0.3%, SET down 1.3%, NZX-50 down 0.8%; Singapore, Malaysia, Indonesia and India markets shut for holidays. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, USD up vs JPY as concerns over possible slowdown in Chinese economic growth led to fall in commodity prices, which in turn lifted USD. USD/JPY at 83.43 vs 83.28 in late NY trade Tuesday, EUR/USD at 1.3505 vs 1.3490, EUR/JPY at 112.69 vs 112.35. Atlanta Federal Reserve Bank President Lockhart says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely need to complete its $600 billion bond-buying program; but adds, hasn't eliminated possibility that economic growth or improvement in employment could surprise to upside, which could justify change to program. IMF spokesperson says working with Europe on possible rescue package for Ireland. Australia November skilled vacancies index fell 1.0% on-month to 44.5, but +5.9% on-year. Australia September annualized leading economic index +4.6% vs +5.8% in August. Australia 3Q wage index +1.1% on-quarter vs +1.0% expected, +3.5% on-year vs +3.3% expected. South Korea 3Q real household income +3.0% on-year vs +4.9% in 2Q. South Korea 3Q actual consumer spending +2.8% on-year vs +4.7% in 2Q. Spot gold at $1338.50/oz, down 90 cents vs NY close. December Nymex crude oil futures down 4 cents cents at $82.30/bbl.(john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0600 GMT [Dow Jones] MOF's primary dealers meeting Thursday, major investors' meeting Friday worth watching as JGB players increasingly focused on supply/demand dynamics, as monetary policy moves to backburner, says RuiXue Xu, debt markets strategist at RBS. Both meetings focus on JGB issuance plans for next fiscal year. "If the message sent out by the meetings is that additional JGB issuance pressure will not be as strong as the market expects, it will help to bring more buyers back." Expects MOF's auction of Y1.1 trillion of 20-year bonds Thursday "to be absorbed smoothly or at least without difficulty, primarily on steady buying from domestic absolute-yield buyers." While super-long end yields fell Wednesday, they still remain attractive for such buyers after having risen steadily in recent weeks. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0545 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares off 1.3% at 987.77 at midday pause in active trade. Benchmark hit 3-week-intrasession low of 983.88 weighed by weakness in overseas bourses due to mounting concerns over more tightening measures from China, Europe debt issues, still fragile U.S. economy, says Phillip Securities' Assistant VP Teerada Chanyingyong. But adds, SET Index may rebound in afternoon session on bargain-buying after recent sharp falls. Support at 980, then 972; resistance at 998. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) down 1.9% at THB315, Banpu (BANPU.TH) down 2.8% at THB762, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) down 1.1% at THB175.50 while \uc2 Polyplex (Thailand) (\uc2 PTL.TH) +4.8% at THB33, \uc2 True Corporation\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +0.9% at THB5.85. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0539 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +12.0% at HK$2.937 billion midday, as percentage of total market volume at 5.5% tad higher vs 5.3% Tuesday's half-day, indicating near-term view still slightly bearish. Ping An (2318.HK) leads with HK$264.25 million, followed by HKEx (0388.HK) with HK$197.98 million, A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) with HK$194.89 million. Then come China Life (2628.HK) with HK$146.41 million, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) with HK$145.05 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0534 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY higher at 83.42 vs 83.28 in late NY trade Tuesday, partly due to falling commodities prices, says dealer in Tokyo. Commodities, especially gold, often move inversely to USD. Investors paying attention to benchmark CRB index, which fell more than 3.0% Tuesday, to gauge commodities outlook. "If we are going to see sharper declines in commodities prices, we should expect further upside potential for the U.S. dollar," says Yuichiro Harada, a senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . Dealers tip resistance at 84.00, then 84.50. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0526 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW extending gains, taking out 1140 resistance amid concerns over possibility of further tightening measures in China, ongoing euro-zone sovereign debt woes; suspected USD-buying BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  near 1135 earlier, also supporting pair, traders say. Pair now sharply up at 1141.5 vs 1129.5 late yesterday in Seoul. Local bank trader pegs cap at 1145. Further upside from here likely stalled on strong USD-selling interest from exporters, says another trader; adds, market biased towards bidding up USD due to concerns over global financial market conditions, on top of fears over possible government capital control measures, "so a sharp pull-back is unlikely." (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0524 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to leave overnight rates unchanged Thursday; still-benign inflation, concerns higher rates will be magnet for even more speculative capital flows main arguments against tightening at this point. All 10 economists polled by Dow Jones expect Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas to leave rates unchanged Thursday and in December, with possible tightening to happen only next year. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept overnight rates--4.0% for borrowing, 6.0% for lending--at record lows since July last year to allow economy to cope with global financial crisis. "There are hardly any overwhelming justifications for the (Philippine) \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten rates as yet, barring the risk of pipeline inflationary threats," says Radhika Rao, economist at Forecast Singapore. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0522 GMT [Dow Jones] Demand for super long JGBs solid, bucking selling trend in shorter end of curve, amid expectations for MOF's Y1.1 trillion sale of 20-year bonds Thursday to be smooth, says Naomi Hasegawa, senior fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. "I think the auction will go well, as it seems long-end investors are happy to buy on dips." 20-year yield last down 1.0 bp at 1.895%, while 30-year yield down 2.0 bps at 2.030%. In contrast to strength in long-dated maturities, lead December futures down 0.23 at 142.16, as traders, short-term investors sell to lock in profit after rises Tuesday. Benchmark 10-year yield up 1.5 bp at 1.040%.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0516 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi 0.1% lower at 1896.87 in choppy trade though automakers, shipbuilding names rising as institutional investors snap up shares which lagged in recent rally; "Although concerns about renewed sovereign debt woes in Europe and further tightening measures in China are weighing the market, investors still believe the Korean economy will fare well," says Hanhwa Securities analyst Joong-ho Shin. "I think the Kospi will test the key 2,000 level again by the end of the year." \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +2.5% at KRW185,500 as uncertainty surrounding M&A deals removed since \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  consortium failed in bid for Hyundai E&C (000720.SE) (off 5.8%). \uc2 Ssangyong Motor\uc2  (003620.SE) +2.7% at KRW9,850, \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  (009540.SE) +1.2% at KRW375,500 while \uc2 STX Offshore & Shipbuilding\uc2  (067250.SE) +7.2% at KRW22,850. Kospi tipped in 1880-1910 range for rest of session. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)(jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures up 0.2% at 9800 in relatively light trading as cash market briefly breaks into positive territory (before returning to under break-even territory; Nikkei now off 0.1% at 9785.55). Continuing firmness in USD/JPY (now 83.40) generally offsetting concerns about Chinese economy and European debt problems. "As Chinese stocks fall on concerns about tightening policy, closer attention is being paid to Japanese stocks which are relatively undervalued," says Hideki Horikawa, chief analyst at Himawari Securities. But adds futures index risks falling to 9500 level before testing key 10,000 line as liquidity flows out of commodity markets. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 11-17-10 0151ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101117e6bh00030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
