\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Blue-Chip Stocks End Lower; Euro-Zone Concerns Weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 2,540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2010\par 23:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Pakistan markets are closed Tuesday for Iqbal Day.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DJIA      11406.84  -37.24  -0.33% \par Nasdaq     2580.05   +1.07  +0.04% \par S&P 500    1223.25   -2.60  -0.21% \par FTSE 100   5849.96  -25.39  -0.43% \par Xetra DAX  6750.50   -3.70  -0.05% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 30/32 -2/32 0.403% +3.2BP \par 5Y Tsy  100 19/32 -4/32 1.125% +2.9BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 20/32 -3/32 2.552% +1.3BP \par  \par USD/JPY       81.17-19     -0.16% \par Range         81.43-81.01 \par EUR/USD       1.3915-20    -0.76% \par Range         1.4084-1.3888 \par AUD/USD       1.0135-39    -0.10% \par Range         1.0165-1.0082 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   10.25/10.55% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   13.65/13.95% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   13.85/14.15% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,403.20 +$5.50 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,386.60-$1,409.40 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $87.06   +$0.21 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks slipped Monday as reignited concerns over European sovereign debt gave investors reason to pause after last week's climb.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed down 37.24 points, or 0.33%, at 11406.84, snapping a six-day winning streak. Travelers was the measure's worst performer, falling 86 cents, or 1.50%, to $56.58.\par \par \uc2 Verizon Communications\uc2  shed 26 cents, or 0.8%, to $33.17, after \uc2 Verizon Wireless\uc2 , the company's joint venture with \uc2 Vodafone\uc2 , began testing a new price point on an unlimited plan, seen as another sign of pricing pressure in the wireless business.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite edged up 1.07, or 0.04%, to 2580.05. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index shed 2.60, or 0.21% to 1223.25, ending a five-session run of gains.\par \par \uc2 Investors paused Monday as the market digested the Dow's 2.9% climb last week that brought the measure to highs not seen since before the September 2008 collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 . The measure is up about 14% from late August, with much of the rally fueled by expectations for the Republican Party's win of control of the House of Representatives last week, and the Federal Reserve's pledge to inject more money into the economy.\par \par \uc2 "The quantitative easing from our Fed should benefit commodities and commodity-oriented sectors," said Tom Samuelson, chief investment officer at Advanced Equities Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 But investors are now wondering whether the market may have overdone the impacts of the election and stimulus move, dubbed by Wall Street as QE2. Meanwhile, concerns about the debt situation in Ireland, Portugal and Spain resurfaced.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended higher Monday, led by oil producers and property companies, after U.S. shares rose to a two-year high Friday.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely consolidate around its current level in the next few sessions to digest its 5.1% rise last week. However, they also said the Chinese market will post more gains in the coming three to six months due to strong demand backed by ample liquidity.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could launch more measures to curb inflation and control hot money inflows, but this wouldn't tighten the flush liquidity in the near term," \uc2 China International Capital Corp\uc2 . said in a report.\par \par \uc2 "More investors are turning to the stock market as the government's property tightening measures are pushing them out of real estate investments," said Amy Lin, an analyst at Capital Securities.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended higher for a sixth straight session Monday to close at a more than 29-month high, led by shipping companies, but \uc2 HSBC\uc2  weighed on the broader index after posting lackluster third-quarter results Friday.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said local shares will likely maintain their upward momentum this week given the high market volume, though profit-taking pressure is growing as the index has risen 8.1% in the past six sessions.\par \par \uc2 Francis Lun, general manager of Fulbright Securities, said the index is likely to surpass 25,000 this week given the ample liquidity in the market.\par \par \uc2 However, Belle Liang, a director at Core Pacific-Yamaichi Research, was more cautious in her outlook. "In the near term, we will see some more consolidation in equities after the market fully digests the second round of quantitative easing, and we suggest short-term investors accumulate on market corrections," she said.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose Monday, with the Nikkei Stock Average closing at a three-month high as better-than-expected U.S. jobs data helped strengthen the dollar, while strong business results from \uc2 JX Holdings\uc2  and others continued to support share prices.\par \par \uc2 "A U.S. economic recovery represents the biggest trigger for a weaker yen (against the dollar)" and is more effective than monetary easing measures by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  or government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, said Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities.\par \par \uc2 Yano added that while the Nikkei index may meet resistance at the 9750 level, a pickup in U.S. consumer spending during the year-end Christmas season underscoring a broader economic recovery could push the index to the 10,000 mark.\par \par \uc2 Crude prices settled higher in U.S. trading Monday but retreated from an intraday high of $87.49, with gains capped by a stronger dollar.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures topped $1,400 for the first time amid uncertainty on the outcome of Group of 20 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  talks and renewed worries about sovereign debt in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed mixed on the London Metal Exchange Monday, with copper and tin the only markets to record gains as a stronger U.S. dollar weighed on prices.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro declined against the dollar and yen Monday as investors renewed their scrutiny of the euro-zone periphery nations, especially Ireland.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many investors pared aggressive bets in favor of the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  due to heightened concerns about debt problems in Ireland, Portugal and Spain, analysts said. The cost of insuring against sovereign debt defaults for those nations went up overnight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro fell 0.8% Monday against the dollar--regaining some earlier losses in afternoon New York trading--and 0.9% against the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone concerns were revived as the market moved from speculating about--and reacting to--the Fed's quantitative easing plan, which was unveiled last Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit default swaps are derivatives that function like an insurance contract for a debt default. If a borrower defaults, the CDS seller compensates the buyer of the debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CDS costs on Irish, Portuguese and Spanish sovereign debt were all higher early Monday. The annual cost of insuring EUR10 million of Irish debt for five years rose by EUR17,000 to EUR606,000, while the cost for Portuguese debt increased by EUR11,000 to EUR460,000 and the cost for Spain climbed by EUR12,000 to EUR260,000. CDS risk--and the underlying fiscal uncertainty--of the nations in the euro zone's geographic periphery contrasts with the economic strength of those nations at the euro zone's core, such as Germany and France.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Outside of Greece, fear of an Irish bond default is the greatest in the euro zone--and that could harm the nation in the long term, said Flavio Giust, senior vice president and manager of institutional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at \f28 \uc2 \f2 KeyBank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Cleveland.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The ability to borrow will still be there but it will be at a much higher price," when Ireland returns to the bond market next year, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Monday afternoon in New York, the euro was at $1.3921 from $1.4029 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y81.17 from Y81.35, while the euro was at Y112.99 from Y114.15. The U.K. pound was at $1.6135 from $1.6199. The dollar was at CHF0.9660 from CHF0.9628.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 77.0035 from 76.606.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The cost of insuring Greek sovereign debt actually fell Monday after the ruling Socialists won local elections in a majority of Greece's 13 electoral regions. The victory provided some confidence in the existing Greek government, which is pushing austerity measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beyond widening CDS, the Irish concern was amplified with uncertain passage of a proposed 2011 budget, which calls for another EUR6 billion in spending cuts and new taxes, said analysts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A government majority is needed to pass that new budget through Parliament in December," and that majority is razor-thin, causing concern in the market, said Jane Foley, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London. "Ireland has had to swallow a huge pill of austerity already and this will be a continuation of deep cuts, and require a fair amount of pain for the Irish people."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A broader retreat from riskier assets was seen Monday, following weeks of dollar selling based on the expected Federal Reserve stimulus that aims to flood the U.S. economy with dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The overall unsettled market conditions have negatively impacted the commodity and emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as well," said Nick Bennenbroek, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The New Zealand dollar was particularly hard-hit trading off by almost 1% versus the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors were likely not impressed by a new survey showing New Zealand's October average property value rose by 1.1% year-over-year, demonstrating slow growth, said analysts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury prices fell Monday, pressured by a heavy slate of new bond supply from both the U.S. government and the private sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1908 GMT, the benchmark 10-year note was 3/32 lower to yield 2.552%. Bond prices move inversely to their prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The three-year note was 4/32 lower to yield 0.548% and the two-year note was 2/32 lower to yield 0.403%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The resulting higher yields boosted demand for a $32 billion sale of three-year notes. The Treasury sold the notes at a second-lowest yield ever of 0.575%, allowing the U.S. government to borrow at historically low interest rates to fund a budget shortfall. The record low yield was set at last month's sale, at 0.569%. [ 08-11-10 2207GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury Department will sell $24 billion in 10-year notes Tuesday and $16 billion in 30-year bonds Wednesday as part of this week's $72 billion in new debt auctions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Supply is the key focus of the week. But I think the 10-year auction will go OK as the Fed is friendly to the bond market," said Mary Ann Hurley, vice president of trading in Seattle at \f28 \uc2 \f2 D.A. Davidson & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "will be buying as much as will be issued for the next six months and probably next year," which will help keep bond yields low, said Tom Tucci, head of U.S. government bond trading in New York at RBC Capital Markets LLC.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended lower for a fifth straight session amid ongoing concerns over inflation. China will release October CPI data Thursday, with some analysts expecting CPI to grow more than 4.0%, above September's 3.6% increase due to higher food prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are now focusing on the PBOC's 1-year bill sale Tuesday in its regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a Shanghai-based trader with a local brokerage said. "If the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to leave the bill yield unchanged tomorrow (Tuesday), that will help support government bond prices in the secondary market."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds finished lower Monday as U.S. treasuries weakened following strong October payrolls data, while gains in Tokyo stocks prompted a shift out of safe-haven investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While surprisingly strong economic indicators could dent bond demand going forward, analysts say the underlying JGB-buying trend seen in recent trading is likely to continue as the Bank of Japan guides interest rates lower through additional steps, including purchases of more JGBs and other securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBS Securities chief strategist Akito Fukunaga said that while U.S. Treasury auctions scheduled for this week aren't likely to hurt investor sentiment, China's economic indicators due Thursday do warrant close scrutiny.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "What we should watch out for most as a potential risk for the current market trend is China CPI," Fukunaga said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The possibility of China's consumer prices having risen sharply in October isn't high, but an unexpectedly strong showing could alarm investors, who generally expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to next hike rates in the first quarter of 2011, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Obama Jumps Into G20 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Surplus Spat \par Chrysler Reports 3Q Loss Of $84M \par No Sign Money Put Before Safety In BP Spill Probe \par A $10 Billion Bond Boom In High-grade Market \par EU-wide Austerity May Spur Double-dip Recession \par Warsh Skeptical Of Fed's Bond Purchases \par Banks Ease Loan Terms, But Credit Demand Falls \par Qantas Finds Oil Leaks In A380 Engines \par Rolls-Royce Engine Problem Unrelated To \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boeing\f28 \uc2 \f2  Program \par =Clothier China Xiniya's US IPO Set To Raise Less Than Expected \par WSJ: Seoul Signals Some Openness On US Trade Deal \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2145  New Zealand  Oct Electronic Card Transactions \par 2350  Japan        Oct Money Stock, Broadly-defined Liquidity \par 2350  Japan        Oct Bank Lending \par 2350  Japan        Sep Balance of Payments \par 2350  Japan        Oct International Transactions in Securities \par 2350  Japan        Oct Provisional Trade Statistics for 1st 20 days of \par                    Month \par 0001  UK           Oct BRC-KPMG Retail Sales Monitor \par 0001  UK           Oct RICS UK Housing Market Survey \par 0030  Australia    Oct NAB Business Survey \par 0300  S Korea      Oct PPI \par 0500  Japan        Oct Economy Watchers Survey \par 0500  EU           Oct European Monster Employment Index \par 0600