\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Long USD/JPY 2M,Short 1Y Vol Spread- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEW\b0\par\par\uc2 1,726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2010\par 08:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] Go long 2-month USD/JPY volatility swap for higher realized, says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus senior analyst Russell Floyd; or spread long 2-month vol against short 1-year vol to exploit extremely steep USD/JPY volatility curve. Adds, USD/JPY implied vols down sharply at front-end, while realized vols starting to rebound off lows. Also notes 2-month volatility risk-discount very large relative to historical norms. See =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTION VIEWS: Value Emerging With Long Dollar/Yen Gamma. (murugesan.suppayyan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0714 GMT [Dow Jones] A further strengthening of the Swiss franc represents one of the main threats to Swiss economic growth, says the KOF economic institute. "The Swiss export industry has benefited from the boom in Asia and a normalization in the mature markets, but one of the main dangers going forward is the strong franc," according to KOF head Jan-Egbert Sturm. Still "the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this year have helped stabilize the market, and exporters have learned to live with a strong franc," he points out. EUR/CHF at 1.3488. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0704 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF begins the week on the defensive after last week failing to breach its 200-day moving average, according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  technical analysts. "The sell off has seen the short-term uptrend eroded and the market starts this week on the defensive," says Karen Anne Jones. "Near term attention has reverted to the 55-day moving average at 1.3291," she notes. EUR/CHF at 1.3459. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0704 GMT [Dow Jones] Hawks in Poland's Monetary Policy Council, or MPC, have gained majority to start policy tightening in November, \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says. That's unless this month's economic data releases disappoint or the zloty strengthens markedly, the bank adds. BNPP sees a 25 basis point hike as more probable than a 50 basis point move, to be followed by another 25 bp move in December or January. "We remain of the view that monetary tightening now is unnecessary and would consider it a policy mistake," BNPP says. "Similar to rate hikes in 2004, monetary tightening should be reversed as soon as the second half 2011." (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0659 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds lower, trading volumes sag as tight cash conditions in banking system hinder buying sentiment, says trader with state-run bank. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.60 after rising as high as 99.02, 98.78 last close. Trader says, market worried over attainability of government's 6%-6.5% inflation estimate by end-December, fuelling fears \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to hike key rates at next month's policy review despite signaling a pause last week. "There is an apprehension that the Fed's QE2 may keep India from taming inflation as more cash floats in the system, giving rise to inflationary pressures," trader says. RBI has said deficit liquidity is preferable for effective monetary policy transmission. Tips 2020 bond in 98.50-98.75 band in session. Indian banks borrowed INR1.18 trillion vs INR842.30 billion Thursday. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0651 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI falls tad more vs midday, last down 0.3% at 24,800.59, on mild profit-taking after 7.7% run-up last week; volume moderates to HK$63.051 billion, suggesting profit-taking pressure not heavy. With market liquidity remaining ample after QE2, HSI valuation still at reasonable level, any consolidation likely brief "and we believe the Hang Seng Index would find support at between 24,200 and 24,500," says \uc2 South China Securities\uc2 . \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) off 2.0% at HK$86.40 after tepid 3Q business update, but China Merchants (0144.HK) soars 4.7% to HIK$32.20 on news of stake buy in Nigerian container-terminal operator; other outperformers include recent laggard, \uc2 China Resources Enterprise\uc2  (0291.HK), +2.9% at HK$34.10. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0650 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.6% at 1520.01 as market sentiment remains positive on "a confluence of better news," says dealer; adds stronger-than-expected U.S. jobs data Friday, expectations of more foreign funds inflow, government winning 2 key by-elections last week lifting market. Says CPO futures at 27-month high, UEM Land's (5148.KU) plans to take over Sunrise (6165.KU) attracting buying interest in plantation, property sectors. OSK analyst Shin Kao Jack says keeping bullish bias near-term; 1524.69 (all-time high) next resistance. Market breadth positive with 517 advancers vs 278 decliners; Sunrise +23% at MYR3.11, UEM Land +6.6% at MYR2.41, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2  (1961.KU) +2.4% at MYR6.00. Decliner include glove maker Supermax (7106.KU) down 1.3% at MYR4.49 after weak 3Q results. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] Interest in Singapore stocks remains keen. STI +1.1% at 3275.87, expected to end below 3300. Among smaller caps, FTSE ST Mid Cap Index +1.5%, FTSE ST Small Cap Index +0.5%. "As a consequence of the money printing by the Fed, the Singapore market looks set to see even better times in the coming months. Given that blue chips will be the hunting ground for foreign funds, the STI should outperform the (FTSE ST) Small Cap Index in the near term," says DMG research head Terence Wong. Overall participation level active, with current volume almost matching whole of Thursday's 2.03 billion shares. Most heavily-traded stocks comprising good mix of small, mid, large caps, including Top Global (519.SG), flat at S$0.015, Genting Singapore (G13.SG), +1.3% at S$2.30, \uc2 Golden Agri-Resources\uc2  (E5H.SG), +5.7% at S$0.74, Noble Group (N21.SG), +4.9% at S$2.13, Rowsley (A50.SG), +3.6% at S$0.145. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0640 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end down 0.2% at 8430.58; losses in chip, PC makers wiped out gains in petrochemical, construction stocks. Concord Securities analyst Allan Lin says lingering concern over strengthening TWD will continue to weigh on tech stocks, investors increasingly switching to non-tech stocks; "tech stocks will not likely reverse recent losses in the near-term if raw material prices and the Taiwan dollar continue to rise." Tips Taiex in 8400-8500 range this week, although could test 8600 by end-November if Asia stocks continue to surge. TSMC (2330.TW) off 1.4% at NT$63.30, Mediatek (2454.TW) off 2.8% at NT$385, Acer (2353.TW) off 2.9% at NT$89.30. Petrochemicals strong on positive product price outlook: Formosa Chemicals (1326.TW) +1.6% at NT$94.80, Formosa Plastics (1301.TW) +1.7% at NT$94.5. Construction shares up as strong TWD to boost asset values; \uc2 Highwealth Construction\uc2  (2542.TW) +4.7% at NT$62.6. Automaker Yulon Motor (2201.TW) +5.3% at NT$61.80. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0634 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD higher as a Swiss bank and major U.K. bank buy, says senior trader at Singapore bank. Tips pair in 7.7500-7.7550 band this week. "Weaker local stocks slowed some capital inflows. I've spotted a Swiss bank buying about US$100 million at the 7.7520 level, and another major U.K. bank buying about US$100 million at that level as well," says trader. HSI last down 0.1%. Pair last at 7.7525 vs 7.7518 this morning, compared with 7.7524 Friday.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0626 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 31% at HK$3.077 billion at interval, 5.4% of market total vs 6.1% Friday's half-day, suggests any further downside in HSI likely limited after 0.1% fall midday. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) leads with HK$363.70 million, then China Life (2628.HK) with HK$243.22 million, A50 (2823.HK) with HK$215.59 million, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$173.99 million and Bank of China (3988.HK) with HK$131.95 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0624 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mixed; Nikkei +1.1%, S&P/ASX down 0.5%, NZX-50 down 0.1%, HSI down 0.1%, Shanghai Composite +0.5%, STI +1.1%, Kospi +0.2%, Taiex down 0.2%, Philippine shares down 1.2%, Sensex down 0.5%, SET +0.4%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR sharply lower vs JPY, USD on worries over debt problems buffeting peripheral euro-zone nations. EUR/USD at 1.3962 vs 1.4034 in late NY trade Friday, EUR/JPY at 113.39 vs 114.15, USD/JPY at 81.22 vs 81.34. Korean regulators may tighten existing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives regulations depending on results of scheduled probe into banks' activities, a senior government official said, as Seoul grows increasingly wary of systemic threat posed by rapid foreign capital flows. Official data on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed Friday authorities waded into market only once in July-September period, lowering expectations for fresh attempts to drive JPY down, even as it remains near record high vs USD; Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda says strong JPY has "advantages," urging domestic companies to use it buy foreign firms and assets; BOJ Deputy Gov. Hirohide Yamaguchi reiterates Monday expansion of newly introduced Y5 trillion asset-buying program is one policy option if nation's economy deteriorates significantly. Australian October job advertisements +0.6% on-month, +34.6% on-year seasonally adjusted, ANZ says. New Zealand July-September core operating deficit at NZ$3.71 billion vs NZ$1.47 billion deficit forecast in Treasury's May budget. Spot gold at $1,390.70/oz, down $3.40 vs NY close. December Nymex crude oil futures up 17 cents at $87.02/bbl, after touching 25-month high in Asian trading. (leslie.shaffer@dowjones.com) [ 11-08-10 0216ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101108e6b800042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
