\par\par \b \uc2 PREVIEW: Japan 3Q GDP Growth Seen Up, But Risk Of Recession Ahead\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Monahan   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,299 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2010\par 05:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's economic expansion likely accelerated in the third quarter of 2010 as shoppers scrambled to buy cars before government subsidies ran out, air conditioners to stay cool in a scorching summer, and cigarettes before a new tobacco tax kicked in.\par \par \uc2 But without these crutches, a slowdown in the current quarter already appears underway. Further efforts to prop up spending may not be enough to halt this trend, fueling speculation that the country's economy could fall back into recession as exports and business investment slow due to the strong yen, falling prices and flagging overseas demand.\par \par \uc2 Adding to doubts the nation's economy will be able to avoid stumbling, the government of Prime Minister Naoto Kan faces an uphill battle in receiving approval from a split parliament for a Y4.85 trillion extra budget to fund new stimulus. Even if passed, the spending would pale in comparison to previous economic support packages.\par \par \uc2 This increasingly uncertain outlook means policy makers will have little time to cheer even if the pace of growth picked up in the July-September period, as expected. Data due Nov. 15 will likely show the country's gross domestic product grew 2.5% on an annualized basis in the quarter, up from 1.5% growth in April-June, according to the median forecast of economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Personal spending supported by one-off factors, particularly government purchasing incentives to buy fuel-efficient cars, likely led growth in the period. The reliance on stimulus highlights the mounting risk that the economy may falter as these programs wind down.\par \par \uc2 Economists say that risk extends as far as the economy possibly falling back into recession, making the government's balancing act even harder as it tries to keep the recovery on track while cutting the country's massive debt.\par \par \uc2 Kan has pledged to cut the public debt, already approaching twice the size of Japan's GDP, and the current extra budget is the first in more than a decade to be funded without deficit-financing government bonds. But analysts say the focus on fiscal restraint means less scope for the kind of programs that fueled growth in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 The higher growth rate "is not sustainable," said Yoshiki Shinke, a senior economist at Dai-Ichi Life Research Institute. "It came in large part as temporary factors supported personal consumption."\par \par \uc2 Other economists echoed this view. Highlighting the risk of a return to recession as consumption wanes in coming months, GDP may contract 0.2% on quarter in the October-December quarter, or 0.7% in annualized terms, said Kyohei Morita, chief economist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in Japan.\par \par \uc2 In the July-September period, private consumption rose 0.9% on quarter, economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires expect on average. Such a rise would be welcome, but will likely prove short-lived. Consumption was flat in the previous quarter, and will likely fall off in the October-December quarter, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Consumers rushed to buy cars before government subsidies expired at the end of September. Other one-off factors also buoyed consumption, which accounts for nearly 60% of Japan's GDP. Smokers stocked up on cigarettes before the introduction of a new tobacco tax in October. A sizzling summer, the hottest on record, also helped by pushing up sales of air-conditioners and beverages.\par \par \uc2 Government tax breaks for home buyers and low interest rates on mortgages likely led to gains in the housing market, economists say. Housing investment grew 1.0% on quarter, after falling 1.3% in the previous period, economists forecast.\par \par \uc2 An expansion in Japan's GDP in the July-September period would mean the economy has grown each quarter over the past year. Brisk shipments of cars, electronics and construction material to China and other high-growth Asian countries have helped Japan's export-driven economy bounce back from the hit it took during the global financial crisis in late 2008 and early 2009.\par \par \uc2 But exports have been slowing in recent months, as overseas markets soften due to the waning effect of stimulus measures.\par \par \uc2 The strong yen has also taken a toll, by making Japanese goods less competitive overseas and diminishing revenue sent back to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in more than six years in mid-September in a failed attempt to tame the soaring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has since neared a record-high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The government last month downgraded its assessment of the country's economy for the first time since February 2009, citing the strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lower demand elsewhere in Asia that is cooling production in Japanese factories.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic Party of Japan-led government says its proposed stimulus steps, such as supporting growing business areas and technologies, would push up real gross domestic product by 0.6 percentage point. But the extra budget funding the measures could be blocked by the opposition Liberal Democratic Party, which has withheld support as it demands sworn parliamentary testimony from the scandal-tainted DPJ party powerbroker Ichiro Ozawa--political bickering that analysts say the economy could well do without.\par \par \uc2 Slowing orders are prompting businesses to revise spending plans. Economists said they expect capital expenditure, which accounts for 16% of GDP, to rise only 0.5% on-quarter in July-September, slowing from a 1.5% gain in the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 Deflation is further clouding the outlook for investment and the broader economy, by pushing up real borrowing costs and prompting consumers to delay purchases as they wait for prices to fall further.\par \par \uc2 Economists said they expect the GDP deflator, the broadest measure of price trends, to come in at minus 1.6% during the July-September quarter, near the minus 1.7% it logged in the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 The nation's seemingly unending problem with deflation may prompt the government, which has made fighting it a top priority, to pressure the Bank of Japan to ease monetary policy further. In October the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its key policy interest rate to a 0.0%-0.1% range, from 0.1%, and launched a new program to buy Y5 trillion in assets including government bonds. It also forecast that prices will finally begin rising in the fiscal year starting April 2011. But economists say such forecasts look too optimistic, after core consumer prices fell 1.1% on year in September for the 19th straight month.\par \par \uc2 "They seem to think the economy is going to bounce back quickly from its current lull, but that's not clear," said Takeshi Minami, chief economist in Tokyo at Norinchukin Research.\par \par \uc2 July-September Real GDP Forecast:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                                                   On Quarter        Annualized \par    Daiwa Insitute of Research                     0.5%              1.9% \par    Japan Research Institute                       0.6%              2.5% \par    Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities       0.8%              3.3% \par    Dai-Ichi Life Research Institute               0.9%              3.6% \par    Mizuho Research Institute                      0.7%              2.7% \par    Mizuho Securities Research & Consulting        0.7%              2.8% \par    Mitsubishi Research Institute                  0.4%              1.7% \par    Nikko Cordial Securities                       0.6%              2.5% \par    \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2                                0.6%              2.5% \par    \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  MUFG                            0.7%              2.8% \par    MEDIAN                                         0.6%              2.5% \par    Previous (April-June)                          0.4%              1.5% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Andrew Monahan, Dow Jones Newswires; 81-3-6269-2783; andrew.monahan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 08-11-10 0425GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 70693   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101108e6b80009w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
