\par\par \b \uc2 Geithner Push On Economic Imbalances Hits Resistance At APEC\b0\par\par\uc2 By P. R. Venkat and Ian Talley  \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 2,430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2010\par 06:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 KYOTO, Japan -(Dow Jones)- U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner's agenda to cajole countries to rein in their current account imbalances ran into headwinds this weekend as a raft of emerging Asian countries complained Washington's monetary policy is flooding their economies with too much cash.\par \par \uc2 Asian finance ministers said the Federal Reserve's easing has prompted a surge in capital flows into their countries as investors look for higher yields, creating potential asset bubbles and stoking inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 The U.S., meanwhile, is attempting to calm roiled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and the escalating risk of a trade war by getting countries to sign up to limit their trade surpluses and deficits. The U.S.-driven strategy is designed to counter growing protectionism and is a less-controversial way of leveraging a faster pace of yuan appreciation by China, a sensitive issue for Beijing.\par \par \uc2 Meeting with members of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations on the sidelines of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation finance ministers' meeting, Geithner stressed "the centrality of U.S. growth to a strong global economy" and called for members to "limit excessive current account imbalances."\par \par \uc2 But the Fed's easing policy--including the announcement last Wednesday to renew asset purchases to help stimulate the U.S. economy--is giving many developing nations headaches.\par \par \uc2 Philippines Finance Secretary Cesar Purisima said Asean nations raised their concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the impact of monetary easing with Geithner at the meeting.\par \par \uc2 Thai Finance Minister Korn Chatikavanij said the resulting influx of cash threatened to harm his economy. "The money has to find a home, and the chances are it will seek a home in our markets and our economies," Korn said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Geithner sees the APEC meeting as a stepping stone to the Group of 20 industrialized and developing nations leader's meeting in Seoul, South Korea, next week.\par \par \uc2 But some Asean members have opposed Washington's push to quantify the goals for reducing imbalances.\par \par \uc2 "Asean has concerns that specific targeting of the current account could lead to measures being employed that may be detrimental to the principal of free trade," group members said in a draft position letter reviewed by Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Although Asean members are unlikely to derail the current account effort--G-20 leaders are expected to include a commitment to restraining surpluses and deficits in the final communique--ministers' complaints are a foreshadow of what the U.S. should expect in Seoul next week.\par \par \uc2 So far, Geithner's appeared to be willing to sacrifice the criticism if it means it can successfully nudge China to lower its surplus and Treasury defends the Fed's decision as necessary for sustaining U.S., and therefore global growth.\par \par \uc2 But the U.S. appeared to give some ground. Current account policies, Geithner told Asean members at the breakfast, should be implemented "with flexibility to respect individual country circumstances." The language suggested that the U.S. was taking into consideration Asean's views that any policy would be sensitive to countries having "differing economic structures."\par \par \uc2 Among Asean members, Singapore and Thailand have especially high current-account surpluses.\par \par \uc2 -By P.R. Venkat, and Ian Talley, Dow Jones Newswires, 202-631-5794; venkat.pr@dowjones.com, ian.talley@dowjones.com [ 11-06-10 0146ET ]\par \par \uc2 KYOTO, Japan -(Dow Jones)- U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner Saturday said there was broad consensus to develop a policy framework to avoid excessive current account imbalances threatening to derail a global economic recovery, but adding flesh to the bones of such an agreement would take time.\par \par \uc2 Geithner, speaking at the end of a Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation meeting, also defended Washington's easing monetary policy from criticism from emerging countries feeling the heat from subsequent massive cash flows into their economies, saying the U.S. recovery was essential to global growth.\par \par \uc2 Washington had wanted to hammer out more detail at the leaders summit of the Group of 20 industrialized and developing nations next week in Seoul, South Korea, on a range of trajectories for deficit and surplus reductions as a way to calm roiled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and avert a global trade war.\par \par \uc2 -By Ian Talley, Dow Jones Newswires, 202-8262-9285; ian.talley@dowjones.com [ 11-06-10 0353ET ]\par \par \uc2 (Adds Treasury Secretary comments.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Ian Talley \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KYOTO, Japan -(Dow Jones)- U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner Saturday said there was broad international consensus to develop a policy framework to avoid excessive current account imbalances threatening to derail the global recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But adding flesh to the bones of such an agreement shouldn't be expected from leaders at the Group of 20 industrialized and developing nations summit next week in Seoul, South Korea.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Washington had wanted to hammer out more detail at the G-20 summit on a range of trajectories for deficit and surplus reductions as a way to calm roiled \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, avert a global trade war, and leverage a faster pace of yuan-appreciation from China.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Geithner flew into Japan early Saturday morning to gather support on the current account agenda among members of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation meeting. APEC represent half of the world's output and the lion's share of surplus economies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although U.S. Treasury officials said last week the U.S. planned to push countries for "concrete guidelines" that would provide the basis for account commitments and monitoring by the International Monetary Fund, Geithner said that specific targets shouldn't be expected at the leaders' summit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's not something you can reduce easily to a single number" Geithner told reporters after the APEC conference. "It's not desirable, necessary and it's not likely at this stage," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Washington ultimately envisions fleshing out a policy framework that would provide the G-20 with an "early warning indicator, a signal of incipient problems and imbalances that could be a problem for future growth and stability," the Secretary said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But that will take time, Geithner said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although finance ministers and central bankers at the G-20 summit are likely to work on drafting such a framework through the IMF-administered Mutual Assessment Process, countries have so far been reluctant to move ahead with specific current account goals beyond a general backing of the concept.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Geithner said there are already indications of what the guidelines could look like in time, however. "If you looked at what countries say and what their policies will produce...the broad policy intentions of major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  provide an outlook that brings those external imbalances down to a level that looks to us to be relatively sustainable over time," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China, for example, has said it plans to reduce its surplus down to below 4% within five years from a 6%. The IMF-produced MAP report at the Toronto G-20 summit in June said Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would have to appreciate at least 10% for current accounts to fall to under 4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given the primary role that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy plays in current accounts, Geithner reiterated that China needed to gradually appreciate the yuan to a more market-determined level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Under a barrage of criticism from emerging countries feeling the heat from subsequent massive cash flows into their economies, the Treasury Secretary also defended Washington's easing monetary policy, saying the U.S. recovery was essential to global growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He called the capital flows "a very positive thing" for emerging countries: "There are risks and challenges with that, but most countries would trade those challenges for any of the challenges that we've faced over the last three years."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. has come under pressure from a variety of quarters, including Germany, particularly after announcing another round of Federal Reserve asset buying. "What the U.S. accuses China of doing, the U.S.A. is doing by different means," Germany's finance minister Wolfgang Schaeuble said of the action, known as quantitative easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Geithner said that a strong dollar was in the U.S.'s interest and Washington "won't ever use our dollar as a tool for economic advantage."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ian Talley, Dow Jones Newswires, 202-631-5794; ian.talley@dowjones.com [ 11-06-10 0548ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KYOTO, Japan -(Dow Jones)- New Zealand Finance Minister Bill English said Saturday that potential damage to exports from the rising local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is a concern, but that market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is not a long-lasting solution to tackle the issue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The member nation of APEC is one of many economies in the region facing challenges from rising \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  due to weakening of the dollar as a result of Federal Reserve's quantitative easing measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's clear from discussions here that we are not the only country with those concerns. Pretty well everyone outside of the U.S. and China has the same concern," he said in a briefing with reporters at the end of a meeting of APEC finance officials.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Faced with a rising yen against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been among countries that have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market, with the sale of an estimated Y2 trillion on Sept. 15.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, English said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is not a lasting measure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We have spoken with quite a number of policy-makers and financial markets people in the last few days, and no one thinks that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is the right solution," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said the best countermeasure is a "long-lasting" policy, such as negotiating free-trade agreements. Japan has recently become more aggressive in this area after years of refusing to open up its highly protected agricultural sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 English noted that in New Zealand, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has control over \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issues, and English avoided ruling out the tool as a policy option.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In extreme circumstances," he said, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can be "appropriate."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Mochizuki, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2785; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com [ 11-06-10 0638ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Ian Talley and P. R. Venkat \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KYOTO, Japan -(Dow Jones)- U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner's efforts to cajole countries to rein in their current account imbalances ran into some headwinds this weekend as a raft of emerging Asian countries complained that Washington's monetary policy is flooding their economies with too much cash.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian finance ministers said the large-scale easing by the Federal Reserve has brought a surge of capital flows into their countries as investors look for higher yields, creating potential asset bubbles and stoking inflationary pressures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S., meanwhile, is attempting to calm roiled \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and the escalating risk of a trade war by getting countries to sign up to limit their trade surpluses and deficits.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The reception at the meeting of finance ministers of the Asian-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum may offer a foreshadowing of what he can expect at the more critical upcoming Group of 20 industrialized and developing nations meeting in Seoul, South Korea, next week at which the U.S. is hoping to get a deal signed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S.-driven strategy is designed to counter growing protectionism and is a less-controversial way of leveraging China to allow a far faster pace of yuan appreciation, a sensitive issue for Beijing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meeting with members of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations on the sidelines of the APEC meeting, Geithner stressed that strong U.S. growth is critical to the global economy, an argument not likely lost on countries that largely depend on the U.S. consumer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury Secretary said that the capital flows being seen are in many respects "very positive" for emerging economies. "There are risks and challenges with that, but most countries would trade those challenges for any of the challenges that we've faced over the last three years."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the Fed's easing policy--including the announcement last Wednesday to renew asset purchases to help stimulate the U.S. economy--is giving many developing nations headaches.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philippines Finance Secretary Cesar Purisima said Asean nations raised their fears over \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the impact of monetary easing with Geithner at the APEC meeting. Thai Finance Minister Korn Chatikavanij acknowledged his country runs a hefty surplus, but he said the resulting influx of cash threatened to harm his economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The money has to find a home, and the chances are it will seek a home in our markets and our economies," Korn said in an interview. "But the speed and the manner in which large economies are forcing adjustments is a concern," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While there were complaints voiced at the conference, no countries actually stepped forward to request that the U.S. stop its easing policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, they have openly opposed Washington's push to quantify the goals for reducing imbalances.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Asean has concerns that specific targeting of the current account could lead to measures being employed that may be detrimental to the principal of free trade," group members said in a draft position letter reviewed by Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asean members are unlikely however, to derail the current account effort, since the G-20 leaders are expected to include a commitment to restraining surpluses and deficits in the final communique.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So far, Geithner has appeared to be willing to sacrifice the criticism if it means he can successfully nudge China to lower its surplus. Treasury officials defend the Fed's decision as necessary for sustaining U.S., and therefore global growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But at the same time, the U.S. appeared to give some ground.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury officials said last week the U.S. planned to push countries for "concrete guidelines" that would provide the basis for account commitments and monitoring by the International Monetary Fund, while Geithner took a somewhat softer line, saying after the APEC meeting that specific targets shouldn't be expected at the leaders' summit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's not something you can reduce easily to a single number," Geithner told reporters. "It's not desirable, necessary and it's not likely at this stage," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also, Geithner told Asean members that current account policies should be implemented "with flexibility to respect individual country circumstances." The language suggested that the U.S. took into consideration Asean's views that any policy would be sensitive to countries having "differing economic structures."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among Asean members, Singapore and Thailand have especially high current-account surpluses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By P.R. Venkat, and Ian Talley, Dow Jones Newswires, 202-631-5794; venkat.pr@dowjones.com, ian.talley@dowjones.com [ 11-06-10 0727ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020101106e6b60000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
