\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 :Dollar Falls Following Fed QE Decision;Mkt Awaits Trichet -3-\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew J. Johnson Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2010\par 14:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Investors fled the dollar Thursday, sending it sharply lower in the wake of the Federal Reserve's announced bond buying plan that will keep U.S. Treasury yields low indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 Investors continued a trend of moving toward the euro and the sterling, comforted by the lack of stimulative moves by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of England, increasing the yield differential in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the greenback.\par \par \uc2 The euro continued its trend as the main benefiiary of dollar-shedding with the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shooting past the $1.42 mark, and to its highest levels since January.\par \par \uc2 The sterling also rose to highs last seen this past January.\par \par \uc2 Investors were nonethless looking for details on withdrawing from uncoventional stimulus measures at ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet's 9:30 a.m. EDT press conference following its meeting.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell much more slightly against the yen as investors remain on guard on possibilities of a Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up a spiraling dollar.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fell back to trading levels last seen in December 2009.\par \par \uc2 Early Thursday morning, the euro was at $1.4225, according to EBS via CQG, from $1.4122 from late Wednesday. The dollar was at Y80.92 from Y81.13, while the euro was at Y115.12 from Y114.59. The U.K. pound was at $1.6226 from $1.6095. The dollar was at CHF0.9680 from CHF0.9707.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index was at 75.897 from 76.396.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's policy-setting Open Market Committee decided Wednesday to buy $600 billion of U.S. Treasurys over the next eight months. While the result was roughly in line with market expectations, investors were much more concerned about the open-ended nature of the Fed's language on possibly resuming with more QE if the first round isn't deemed sufficient, said analysts.\par \par \uc2 'The Fed's announcement that the volume and speed of the purchasing programme will be adjusted if necessary to reach the two targets of price stability and maximum employment might soon turn out to be a bottomless pit,' said Claire Tappenden, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 'Without specifying how much progress toward reaching those objectives, it is not clear what terminates the policy,' said \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  analysts in New York.\par \par \uc2 The euro's upward momentum could slow on any hints the ECB will extend its own full allotment scheme next month based on concerns over Ireland's struggle to adopt a new austerity budget.\par \par \uc2 The cost of credit default swaps written on Irish sovereign debt rose to a record high for the fourth consecutive day Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Ireland's five-year sovereign CDS were 12 basis points wider at 570 basis points, according to data provider to Markit.\par \par \uc2 The yield spread between Irish 10-year government bonds and the benchmark German 10-year bund also hit a fresh high, rising over 13 basis points to reach 517 basis points shortly before 0940 GMT.\par \par \uc2 The Australian and New Zealand dollars, closely linked to the pace of commodity-driven Asian-led global growth, continued to gain sharply against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The euro didn't get much help from the latest purchasing managers' index for euro-zone service industries, which came in at 53.3 in October, only a slight tick up from the initial 53.2 estimate and still well down from 54.1 in September.\par \par \uc2 Analysts suggest the BOJ will leave its policy unchanged for now and concentrate on completing plans for previously announced asset purchases. -By Andrew J. Johnson, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-3092; andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Mark Brown and Nicholas Hastings in London contributed to this article.)\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2010 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020101105e6b400eyy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
