\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Fed Move Whacks Dollar Lower Across The Board\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2010\par 12:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The dollar slumped Thursday following the Federal Reserve's decision to provide another $600 billion of quantitative easing between now and the middle of next year.\par \par \uc2 The slide took the dollar's trade-weighted index down to a new 2010 low of 75.794, with both the euro and the pound rising to nine-month highs against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Contributing to the negative reaction, the additional QE was slightly larger than anticipated. The Fed's suggestion that it hasn't ruled out further easing also drove the dollar down.\par \par \uc2 According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy team at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 , it is fear of this 'bottomless pit' if U.S. economic data doesn't improve that is undermining support for the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The euro was the main beneficiary, especially as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to leave its policy unchanged at its meeting Thursday.\par \par \uc2 At one stage the euro rallied to $1.4265, a level last seen in January. However, its upward momentum could slow on any hints the ECB will extend its own full allotment scheme next month and on concerns over Ireland's struggle to adopt a new austerity budget.\par \par \uc2 The yield spreads on Irish debt and the cost of insuring Irish loans through credit default swaps both rose to new record highs early Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The euro didn't get much help from the latest purchasing managers' index for euro-zone service industries, which came in at 53.3 in October, only a slight tick up from the initial 53.2 estimate and still well down from 54.1 in September.\par \par \uc2 See how the euro powered ahead against the dollar:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4883"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4883}}}\par \par \uc2 The pound was also pushed to new nine-month high against the dollar, reaching $1.6206, encouraged by market speculation that the Bank of England won't follow the Fed with its own quantitative easing program.\par \par \uc2 Expectations of a U.K. QEII receded this week as U.K. economic data proved more resilient than expected.\par \par \uc2 Although the Fed's announcement lifted global risk appetite and helped the Nikkei Index in Japan to rise nearly 2.2%, the dollar also fell against the yen.\par \par \uc2 However, losses were curbed by fears that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would launch a new round of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen from getting stronger. Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda kept these fears alive by repeating warnings that the ministry will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if need be.\par \par \uc2 Investors were also nervous about pushing the yen too far ahead of the Bank of Japan's own policy meeting that ends early Friday. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  brought the timing of this meeting forward in case it needed to respond to the Fed's move.\par \par \uc2 However, analysts suggest that the BOJ will leave its policy unchanged for now and concentrate on completing plans for previously announced asset purchases. By midmorning, the euro was up at $1.4250 from $1.4122 late on Wednesday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The pound was also up at $1.6186 from $1.6095 while the dollar slipped to Y80.96 from Y81.13.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was also down at CHF0.9664 from CHF0.9707 while the euro rallied to Y115.40 from Y114.59. By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2010 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020101105e6b400d1i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
