\par\par \b \uc2 Euro Hits Intra-Day High As ECB's Trichet Speaks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew J. Johnson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 731 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2010\par 14:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Investors fled the dollar Thursday, sending it sharply lower in the wake of the Federal Reserve's announced bond buying plan that will keep U.S. Treasury yields low indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 Investors continued a trend of moving toward the euro and sterling, comforted by the lack of stimulative moves by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of England, increasing the yield differential in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the greenback.\par \par \uc2 The euro continued its trend as the main benefiary of dollar-shedding with the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shooting past the $1.42 mark, and to its highest levels since January.\par \par \uc2 The sterling also rose to highs last seen this past January.\par \par \uc2 After the ECB and BOE announced no changes in rates at the start of New York trading, the euro and sterling picked up some fresh steam, adding to the dollar's losses.\par \par \uc2 While ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet addressed the media following its meeting, the euro hit its high for the day of $1.4276. Sterling also hit its daily high of $1.6293.\par \par \uc2 Trichet said the ECB's existing unconventional stimulus measures would be "temporary in nature."\par \par \uc2 The dollar also fell on the back of mixed new U.S. economic data early Thursday with jobless claims for the week ending Oct. 30 emerging higher than expected at 457,000. A third-quarter non-farm productivity data point was more encouraging by rising at a better-than-expected annnual 1.9% rate.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the U.S. data, the dollar fell to a daily low against the yen of Y80.58.\par \par \uc2 Investors though refrained from testing a post-World War II low against the yen as they remained on guard on possibilities of a Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up a spiraling dollar.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fell back to trading levels last seen in December 2009.\par \par \uc2 Thursday morning, the euro was at $1.4267, according to EBS via CQG, from $1.4122 from late Wednesday. The dollar was at Y80.72 from Y81.13, while the euro was at Y115.13 from Y114.59. The U.K. pound was at $1.6285 from $1.6095. The dollar was at CHF0.9661 from CHF0.9707.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index was at 75.716 from 76.396.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's policy-setting Open Market Committee decided Wednesday to buy $600 billion of U.S. Treasurys over the next eight months. While the result was roughly in line with market expectations, investors were much more concerned about the open-ended nature of the Fed's language on possibly resuming with more QE if the first round isn't deemed sufficient, said analysts.\par \par \uc2 "The Fed's announcement that the volume and speed of the purchasing program will be adjusted if necessary to reach the two targets of price stability and maximum employment might soon turn out to be a bottomless pit," said Claire Tappenden, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 "Without specifying how much progress toward reaching those objectives, it is not clear what terminates the policy," said \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  analysts in New York.\par \par \uc2 The Australian and New Zealand dollars, closely linked to the pace of commodity-driven Asian-led global growth, continued to gain sharply against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Analysts suggest the BOJ will leave its policy unchanged for now and concentrate on completing plans for previously announced asset purchases.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Canada Morning \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Canadian dollar is trading very close to parity with the U.S. dollar Thursday, extending post-FOMC gains after sliding sharply on Ottawa's decision to block BHP Billiton's $39 billion takeover bid of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Potash Corp of Saskatchewan Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar was at C$1.0015 Thursday morning, according to CQG, from C$1.0079 late Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar spiked to C$1.0123 on news of the blocking of the merger "but retreated sharply as a deluge of USD sellers felt QE2 trumped the politically driven decision," said Brad Schruder at BMO Capital Markets in Toronto.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Andrew J. Johnson, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-3092; andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Karen Johnson in Toronto contributed to this article.) [ 11-04-10 0950ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101104e6b40009l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
