\par\par \b \uc2 HIGHLIGHTS-Key quotes from Japan policymakers on yen, economy\b0\par\par\uc2 811 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2010\par 06:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the Japanese economy, click [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 4 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan begins a two-day rate review on Thursday, a day after the Federal Reserve's decision to buy $600 billion more in government bonds to boost the U.S. economy. [ID:nFEDAHEAD]\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  brought forward its meeting from mid-November to speed up the launch of its 5 trillion yen ($62 billion) asset buying scheme.\par \par \uc2 It is expected to hold off on easing monetary policy further unless the Fed's quantitative easing pushes the dollar well beneath its record low against the yen. [ID:nTOE6A205I]\par \par \uc2 Following are recent key quotes by BOJ and government officials on monetary policy and the economy.\par \par \uc2 PRIME MINISTER NAOTO KAN, Nov 4, in parliament\par \par \uc2 "We had hoped Japan's economy would achieve self-sustained growth backed by consumption and investment. But unfortunately the recovery has not gone that far and downside risks for the economy are increasing."\par \par \uc2 JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER YOSHIHIKO NODA, Nov 4, in parliament\par \par \uc2 "Japan is experiencing deflation and economic conditions are still severe. Under such circumstances, excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility will have negative economic and financial effects ...\par \par \uc2 "We will continue to watch \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with great interest and will take decisive action when necessary, including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ...\par \par \uc2 "We hope the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to support the economy on the monetary policy front."\par \par \uc2 BOJ GOVERNOR MASAAKI SHIRAKAWA, Nov 4, in seminar\par \par \uc2 "There is debate globally at present about correcting current account imbalances that are unsustainable. The lack of flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, which are one of the means of adjusting the imbalances, in emerging economies may delay needed adjustments ...\par \par \uc2 "Japan's economy is gradually recovering following a rapid pickup in spring last year. But due to slowing growth in exports and production, the economy's improvement is weakening ... The pace of recovery in global economic recovery has somewhat slowed since the middle of this year, but the recovery trend will likely be maintained ...\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ's asset buying and its taking on of risk is expected to prompt market players to invest more aggressively and spur more fund flows to markets, thereby narrowing risk premiums. We intend to launch the asset buying as early as possible."\par \par \uc2 CHIEF CABINET SECRETARY YOSHITO SENGOKU, Nov 4, to reporters.\par \par \uc2 "The Fed's decision was based on the U.S. unemployment rate and inflation trends.\par \par \uc2 "As for what happens from here on, we need to deepen debate on monetary policy coordination at G20 and G7. It's difficult to analyse the impact the Fed's decision will have on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The BOJ has already eased policy, so I'm sure they will watch several indicators closely when conducting policy."\par \par \uc2 FINANCE MINISTER YOSHIHIKO NODA, Nov 2, news conference\par \par \uc2 "The G20 confirmed that excessive volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates would hurt the economy, and countries with reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar, the yen and the euro, will cooperate as appropriate while closely watching market moves."\par \par \uc2 ECONOMICS MINISTER BANRI KAIEDA, Nov 2, news conference\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Japan brought forward its meeting because it wanted to get approval from the finance ministry soon for asset purchases.\par \par \uc2 "The results of the Federal Reserve's meeting will be out early on Nov. 4 in Japan, so depending on that, I think the BOJ will make its own decision on monetary policy ... I am closely watching the outcome of the Fed's meeting."\par \par \uc2 BOJ GOVERNOR MASAAKI SHIRAKAWA, Oct 28, news conference\par \par \uc2 "In order to start buying ETFs and REITs early, we decided to change the date of the next policy meeting to Nov. 4-5 from Nov. 15-16 so that we can debate and decide on the basic guidelines.\par \par \uc2 "Of course we will hold debate as usual on monetary policy."\par \par \uc2 BOJ BOARD MEMBER KIYOHIKO NISHIMURA, Oct 20, speech to business leaders in Hiroshima\par \par \uc2 "The fact that the recent yen appreciation is causing a deterioration in business sentiment is a big factor in putting downward pressure on economic activity ...\par \par \uc2 "There is a risk that the yen's appreciation will lower consumer prices not only through worsening economic activity but also through changes in import prices."\par \par \uc2 SHIRAKAWA, Oct 21, in parliament\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ is very carefully watching the impact of the yen's rise on the economy and based on this stance we strengthened our powerful easing further (at the Oct. 4-5 board meeting) ...\par \par \uc2 "Our policy of committing to low interest rates (until price stability is in sight) will prove most effective when the economy starts to improve fully." ($1=80.99 Yen) (Compiling by Tetsushi Kajimoto, Leika Kihara, Rie Ishiguro; Editing by Edmund Klamann and Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/QUOTES (HIGHLIGHTS)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|O|OIL|UKI|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101104e6b40001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
