\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Signs May Aid  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Stocks-Daiwa\b0\par\par\uc2 1,524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2010\par 01:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets notes in light of USD's climb as much as Y1 yesterday vs JPY at start of TSE trading (triggering speculation of another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ), if history serves as guide, bottoms and tops in Japanese stocks have often materialized around the start and end of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (to buy dollar and sell yen). "We view that history as a negative share price catalyst, if the absence of BOJ activity in October indicates the recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ended in September. Although equally hard to tell at this juncture, signs of a sustained \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could provide support to the broader market." Adds, fears that the absence of further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could rekindle concerns over the dearth of powerful measures to combat strong yen. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA expected to keep policy interest rate on hold at 4.5% in rate decision due at 0330 GMT, but AUD could still gain if \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's statement points to a rate hike at next meeting in December. AUD/JPY may rise to 80.30 in this case, says Kenichiro Ikezawa, senior dealer at \uc2 Daiwa SB Investments\uc2 ; "the market's main scenario is no rate hikes this year. If they suggest they may raise the rate at their next meeting, players will likely increase bets on the Australian dollar." In poll of 18 economists by Dow Jones, 12 forecast RBA will keep rates steady, rest tip 25bp hike. AUD/JPY last 79.66. Says if AUD/JPY tests 80.30, EUR/JPY may rise to 112.50 as investors' appetite for riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  grows; EUR/JPY last 112.12. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped lower on remittance flows, rising EUR in early Asian trade; pair may slide to 42.80 initial support after settling at 43.01 late Friday (market shut yesterday for All Saints' Day holiday), with next support at 42.60. Resistance pegged at 43.20. "The (pair) is on its way to breaching the 43 level. This is the start of the (strong) remittance season" due to tuition fee payments for a new school term and ahead of Christmas, says \uc2 Marcelo Ayes\uc2 , senior vice president at \uc2 Rizal Commercial Banking Corp\uc2 . Note, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week raised BOP surplus, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves targets for 2010 due to strong capital inflows. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD trades in very tight range in offshore trade, likely to continue to do so ahead of FOMC meeting results. Trader expects 1.2890-1.2950 range to hold. "The market wants to sell the (Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) rally but U.S. dollar weakness is still the main story...it will probably be pretty quiet ahead of the Fed," trader at foreign bank says. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been rising steadily since about mid-year, as investors look for more attractive returns from Asian assets; with several Asian central banks already suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' rise recently, some expect that the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally may soon end, or at least slow. Pair last 1.2918.(samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely in 30.50-30.70 range vs yesterday's 30.44-30.78 trading band, trader at local bank says; pair at 30.695 late yesterday. "Foreign funds are still coming in (to Taiwan), but they seem to be more on the cautious side ahead of the Fed meeting" this week; says overnight USD strength vs EUR, JPY is a rebound, not a turnaround, with USD very likely to remain in downtrend in near term as U.S. economy struggles. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may open higher on expectations Fed will announce a bond-purchasing program after FOMC meeting, which starts later today, ends tomorrow. Shanghai Composite Index faces resistance at psychologically-important 3100 level, after ending +2.5% at 3054.02 yesterday. "Expectations of a new round of quantitative easing from the Federal Reserve means that global monetary flows will remain loose, which will lend support to inflation-related stocks like metals, coal and agriculture," says Shenyin Wanguo Securities analyst Qian Qimin. Shenzhen Composite Index ends +2.9% at 1341.84. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] Some BOJ policy board members expressed concern about side-effects of interest rate cuts at October 4-5 meeting, according to minutes released earlier, suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be unwilling to make any further policy rate cuts in near future; at that meeting, BOJ board cut policy target rate to range of 0.0%-0.1% from 0.1% previously. BOJ will hold another policy-setting meeting this Thursday, Friday; since timing comes just after FOMC meeting, some market players speculating that BOJ may take further action--such as expanding new Y5 trillion worth asset-buying fund--if potential Fed move causes another spike in JPY. USD/JPY last around 80.56.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi flat at 1914.06, as investors watch for FOMC's policy decision Wednesday, when quantitative easing measures widely expected; "The market's consensus on the size of the Fed's measures has been lowered, to around $500 billion. The market will cheer if the result comes in above that expectation, but if it is below that, some shocks (for stocks) are expected," says \uc2 Daishin Securities\uc2 ' Park Jung-seop. Adds, Kospi could drop below 1900 if Fed disappoints. Analysts think Kospi will slowly rise to 1950 in November on improving economic fundamentals, still-ample liquidity, though Park adds momentum could weaken toward end-month as most major events centered in November's first half. Techs mixed with \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 0.4% at KRW744,000, \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) +0.8% at KRW97,700. Shipbuilders, chemical stocks up on brighter business outlook; \uc2 Hyundai Heavy\uc2  (009540.SE) +3.4% at KRW392,500, Daewoo Shipbuilding (042660.SE) +4.6% at KRW31,100. Kospi tipped in 1910-1925 range today. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0036 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW flat at 1116.7 vs 1116.6 late yesterday in Seoul after coming off earlier 1119 high as EUR/USD staging modest rebound in early Asian hours, traders say. Local bank trader tips pair in 1110-1123 range for day. Says, "players can't help but be cautious ahead of the U.S. mid-term elections and the FOMC meeting. But eventually, a broadly weaker dollar due to likely further quantitative easing and South Korea's strong economic fundamentals will lead the dollar/won lower." Traders expect pair to closely track EUR/USD moves, with key USD/KRW support tipped at 1110. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR tad higher at 3.0940 in thin volume vs 3.0900 late Monday; local dealer says pair likely to edge higher after stronger U.S. manufacturing data overnight. "There are going to be some buyers (of USD) at this level but we don't think it'll be aggressive," says dealer; adds, some market players on sidelines, eyeing FOMC meeting for direction. Tips 3.0900-3.1100 range for rest of day. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares tipped to maintain positive tone, though in cautious trade after Wall Street's big intraday pullback yesterday ahead of FOMC meeting, also after STI's 1.6% rise to 3,192.18 yesterday. "Of course, generally people should be cautious because of the FOMC meeting. So far this morning, I haven't seen any real catalysts for the market -- my best guess is that it will continue to hold ground, maybe with a slight upward bias for the day, but there's not really any major news," says trader at local brokerage. Cosco Corp. (F83.SG) may see some interest after winning over US$87 million of contracts for 3 dry bulkers, also Sound Global (E6E.SG) after it gets Jiangsu sewage project. Trader adds banks likely underpinned after UOB (U11.SG), OCBC (O39.SG) results didn't disappoint, while usual suspects like Genting Singapore (G13.SG) will continue to take volume. Index near 3200 psychological level, beyond that, 3220 recent peak may cap. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0030 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ 3Q labor cost index shows wage growth picking up, says ASB economist Jane Turner; "the lift in wage inflation reflects the improvement in labor market conditions as the economy exits recession." Stats NZ says salary and ordinary time wages in the private sector +0.6% on-quarter, +1.6% on-year. Turner adds stronger-than-expected wage growth indicates inflation pressures could potentially build quickly over next year; still, "the softness in recent activity data reduces the urgency for the RBNZ to normalise policy settings in the near term." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 11-01-10 2059ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   