\par\par \b \uc2 INDIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Sensex May Rise To 20,190\b0\par\par\uc2 2,006 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2010\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Philippines markets closed for All Saints Day.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA                11118.49  +4.54    +0.04% \par Nasdaq              2507.41   +0.04    +0.00% \par S&P 500             1183.26   -0.52    -0.04% \par FTSE 100            5675.16   -2.73    -0.05% \par Nikkei Stock        9186.04   -16.41   -0.18% \par Hang Seng           23501.88  +405.56  +1.76% \par Kospi               28721.33  +318.74  +1.12% \par SGX Nifty           6103.00   +63.00   +1.04% \par Nov. contract \par  \par USD/JPY             80.32-34     -0.14% \par Range               80.39-80.30 \par EUR/USD             1.3962-64    +0.24% \par Range               1.3965-1.3945 \par  \par CBOT Wheat Dec.     $7.172 per bushel \par Comex Gold          $1,357.60   +$15.10 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,335.20-$1,359.70 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $81.43      -$0.75 \par  \par  \par CHARTING THE SENSEX \f28 \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bombay Stock Exchange\uc2 's 30-share Sensitive Index could rise to 20,190, according to charts.\par \par \uc2 Friday's sharp recovery toward the close of trading is a positive sign. A move past minor resistance at 20,190 could lead to a test of major resistance at 20,260.\par \par \uc2 But the Sensex has to rise above the Oct. 25 high of 20,453 to impart momentum to the uptrend. A fall below minor support at 19,760 would indicate a reversal of the short-term uptrend.\par \par \uc2 The National Stock Exchange's 50-share Nifty could test resistance at 6,055.\par \par \uc2 A move past major resistance at 6,072 would lend momentum to the short-term uptrend. But a drop below minor support at 5,945 would trigger a fall to major support at 5,930.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. blue-chip stocks edged higher Friday while the Standard & Poor's 500 index fell slightly in a quiet end to a strong October as investors bided their time before the coming week's Federal Reserve meeting and midterm elections.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Microsoft\f28 \uc2 \f2  was among the Dow's best performers with a 1.5% rise in its stock. The software giant's fiscal first-quarter profit climbed 51%, benefiting from a continued strong response to the Windows 7 operating system and Office 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chevron\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Merck helped limit the Dow's gain. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chevron\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 2.2% after the oil major's third-quarter earnings and revenue missed analysts' expectations. Merck dropped 1.7% as the company's earnings excluding items topped Wall Street estimates, but revenue fell short of forecasts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday, which marks the 81st anniversary of the Crash of 1929 known as "Black Tuesday," was the final trading day of what has been a strong October for stocks. The Dow climbed 3.06% during the month and the S&P 500 rose 3.69% for October, the Dow's best October since 2006 and the S&P 500's best October since 2003.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This month's advance has come on growing expectations that the coming week the Federal Reserve will announce more stimulus, and the midterm federal elections will lead to a Republican takeover of the House of Representatives.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets were mostly higher Monday, but the Tokyo market was weighed down by concerns over the outlook for earnings and a strong yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei Share Average was down while Australia's S&P/ASX 200, South Korea's Kospi Composite and New Zealand's NZX-50 were all up. Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were 57 points higher in screen trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sentiment was supported by the China's official purchasing managers' index for manufacturing for October. China Federation of Logistics and Purchasing's PMI rose to 54.7 in October, from September's 53.8, indicating momentum in the Chinese economy remained strong and supporting last month's surprise decision by the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to hike interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some investors were cautious ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy meeting Tuesday and Wednesday. There was some uncertainty over the scope of the quantitative easing measures that the Fed is widely-expected to unveil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo, exporters remained under selling pressure, with Canon off 0.6% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toyota Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 1.1%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honda Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  lost 3.4% despite reporting solid second-quarter earnings as the auto maker cut its fiscal second-half net profit outlook to Y91.6 billion, from its previous forecast of Y105 billion due to a strong yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares fell 2.2% as news that the electronics giant had returned a net profit in the July-September was offset by concerns over pressure on earnings at its television division.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Seoul, Samsung Engineering rose 6.1%, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hyundai Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  advanced 4.7% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kia Motors\f28 \uc2 \f2  jumped 7.0%.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's sharp fall against the yen in early trade made for volatile trade in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  majors. After touching a 15-year low, the dollar shot up more than one yen and has since pulled back a little to trade at Y80.64, compared with Y80.40 in late New York trade Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was fetching $1.3970, from $1.3947 late Friday in New York, and was at Y112.23 against the yen, from Y111.99.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier, the dollar came under renewed pressure as Friday's U.S. data, including third-quarter gross domestic product and October consumer confidence, highlighted the sluggish state of growth in the world's biggest economy. The data reinforced widely-held expectations in the market that the Fed will launch a second round of quantitative easing at its meeting this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices were helped higher Friday as the downbeat economic data and rising uncertainty about the coming week's FOMC meeting saw investors re-enter the market. Spot gold was at $1361.30 per troy ounce, up $1.50 from its New York close Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange pared early losses after the dollar slipped on weaker-than-expected U.S. GDP data, but still finished down on the day and on the week, consolidating at lower levels following a week of choppy trading across broader financial markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil futures lost ground Friday in U.S. trading after a report showed only modest U.S. economic growth in the third quarter, capping a week where prices stayed in a narrow range.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December Nymex crude oil futures were up 37 cents at $81.80 per barrel on Globex.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CALENDAR OF EVENTS Nov. 1 \par 0930 IST New Delhi:   India's prime minister, oil minister, junior oil \par                       minister, federal oil secretary; energy/oil \par                       ministers of Angola, Sudan, Uganda, Sri Lanka, \par                       Ghana; Oil and Natural Gas chairman, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indian Oil\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par                       chairman at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrotech\f28 \uc2 \f2  conference \par 1045 IST New Delhi:   Finance minister at event \par 1230 IST New Delhi:   Oil minister, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bharat Petroleum\f28 \uc2 \f2  managing director, \par                       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hindustan Petroleum\f28 \uc2 \f2  managing director, Reliance \par                       Industry head of refinery business at event \par 1415 IST Mumbai:      \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman, GE India chief \par                       executive at event \par 1500 IST Mumbai:      \f28 \uc2 \f2 JSW Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman at 2Q earnings press meeting \par 1600 IST Mumbai:      L&T Infra chief executive, Mercedes-Benz India \par                       chief executive, Yes Bank chief economist at event \par 1700 IST Mumbai:      \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  to release half-yearly \par                       review of macroeconomic, monetary developments \par N/A:                  Auto makers and cement companies to announce \par                       October sales data \par EARNINGS:             Gammon Infrastructure, GTL Infrastructure, PVR, \par                       Punj Lloyd, Emami, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dishman Pharma\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hindustan Motors\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \par                       Lupin, GMDC, Havells India, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Century Textiles\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par 1230 GMT US:          September personal income and outlays \par 1345 GMT US:          October Dow Jones economic sentiment indicator \par 1400 GMT US:          October online help wanted index \par 1400 GMT US:          October ISM manufacturing report on business \par 1400 GMT US:          September construction spending - construction put \par                       in place new construction \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES - Dow Jones/WSJ \par =UPDATE: CHINA CFLP OCTOBER PMI RISES; SIGNALS INFLATION PRESSURE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's manufacturing activity expanded at a faster pace in October compared with September, an official gauge showed Monday, and input prices rose sharply, which will likely raise concerns about further government tightening measures to combat inflationary risks. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =DATA SNAP:KOREA OCT CPI UP 4.1% ON YEAR; FASTEST IN 20 MONTHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's annual inflation unexpectedly jumped to a 20-month high in October and remained on the upper end of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2% to 4% inflation target band for a second straight month. (published 2320 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =DATA SNAP: S KOREA EXPORTS UP 29.9% ON YEAR IN OCT; $6.9B SURPLUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's exports increased more than anticipated in October from a year earlier, helping the country record another robust trade surplus of $6.914 billion in the month and giving comfort just days after September's production data raised concerns of a faster-than-expected slowdown of the local economy. (published 0004 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OFFICIAL: NO COMMENT ON IF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENED\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MKTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen's moves have been "excessive" recently, a Japanese government official said Monday, but he declined to comment on whether Tokyo authorities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market earlier in the day to knock the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lower. (published 0033 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MEWAH INTERNATIONAL SEEKS TO PRICE SHARES AT S$1.25-S$1.55 IN IPO - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mewah International Inc. expects to price its shares between S$1.25 and S$1.55 in its proposed initial public offering to raise about S$400 million as the palm oil processor seeks to tap investor appetite for commodity stocks, a person involved in the share sale said Monday. (published 0142 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA MODERN DAIRY STARTS PRE-MARKETING HK IPO MONDAY - TERM SHEET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-31-10 2300ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Modern Dairy Holdings Ltd. starts pre-marketing for its Hong Kong initial public offering on Monday ahead of a listing on the Hong Kong stock exchange Nov. 26, according to a term sheet seen by Dow Jones Newswires. (published 0124 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =WSJ UPDATE: ICBC BUYS SMALL US BROKERAGE FIRM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Industrial & Commercial Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ltd. (1398.HK, 601398.SH, IDCBY), China's largest lender, is venturing into the broker-dealer business in the U.S., one of the most telling new signs of the rise of Chinese financial institutions in the world economy. (published 2200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S PACIFIC EQUITY PARTNERS MULLING EXIT OF HOYTS CINEMA CHAIN -SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian private equity firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pacific Equity Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2  is considering an exit of The Hoyts Group through either a trade sale or initial public offering, a person familiar with the matter said Monday. (published 2308 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TEN NETWORK PREPARED TO DISCUSS CHAIRMAN'S TRANSITION WITH PACKER, MURDOCH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ten Network Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Monday it offered directorships to James Packer and Lachlan Murdoch and will also consider appointing a new chairman after Packer's Consolidated Press Holdings took a 17.88% stake in Ten earlier this month. (published 2214 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MITR PHOL TO BUY 19.9% STAKE IN MARYBOROUGH SUGAR FROM \f28 \uc2 \f2 GUINNESS PEAT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand-based \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitr Phol Sugar Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . will buy up to a 19.9% interest in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Maryborough Sugar Factory Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guinness Peat Group PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which holds a 24.47% stake in MSF, Maryborough said Monday. (published 2307 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc