\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY May Fall To 78.50 This Week - Citi\b0\par\par\uc2 1,749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2010\par 08:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0714 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall to 78.50 this week, below record-low of 79.75, as falls in U.S. Treasury yields, Japan exporter sales weigh on USD, says Osamu Takashima, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist, Citibank Japan. Says that Japan exporters sales may ease somewhat after passing of month-end but says firms still behind in their hedging (i.e. using options contracts) and likely to continue to sell pair in spot. IMM report on trading positions shows net JPY non-commercial long positions vs USD have declined for 3 weeks, indicating that USD/JPY fall largely due to sales by real demand players, including Japan exporters, not by speculators. Takashima also expects volatile market with U.S. mid-term election, FOMC, BOJ policy meeting, U.S. non-farm payroll all coming up. Pair last 80.45. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0709 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD down at 30.450 vs 30.782 Friday, off early low at 30.441, lowest since July 2008, on hot money inflow to local stock market (Taiex ends +1.1% ), says local bank trader. Foreign investors buy net NT$16.65 billion Taiwan shares today. Adds, pair may test 30.4200 Tuesday as "foreign funds are looking to park money in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of U.S. Fed's FOMC meeting and elections due later this week."(lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0708 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +2.0% at 23,561.24, supported by gains in China markets with Shanghai Composite +2.2% after strong China PMI data; trading volume brisk at HK$57.54 billion. "Fund flows will continue to dictate the market's direction near term, with particular attention paid to the U.S. Federal Reserve's statement after the two-day Federal Open Market Committee meeting," says Taifook. Notes there are questions over whether expected quantitative easing from Fed will prove effective in stimulating economy, also highlights risk that flood of liquidity from Fed may fuel asset inflation in Asian countries, particularly in China. Taifook continues to believe market uptrend will remain intact after pre-FOMC volatility. Among blue chips post-results, \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK) flat at HK$11.02, while \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) +2.7% at HK$7.59. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0707 GMT [Dow Jones] India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may lift its key lending and borrowing rates by 25 basis points each at Tuesday's policy review as inflationary concerns will play a dominant role in policy decision, says ICICI Bank in research note. "Despite this move, real interest rates will continue to be negative. However, these are expected to normalise on their own as inflation would start to ease in a few months," house says. Adds, repurchase rate or key lending rate (currently at 6.00%) will continue to remain operative rate for overnight lending as RBI likely to "maintain the liquidity in deficit as it improves the efficacy of the monetary transmission mechanism". (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0707 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF extends gains above 1.37 early Monday, and is unlikely to be restrained by the solid economic data coming out of Switzerland, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "The big new impulses for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are likely to emerge from the forthcoming FOMC decision on further quantitative easing," according to economist Reto Huenerwadel. "Fundamentally there are good reasons to look for CHF positive news from the Swiss real economy, but whether solid Swiss economic indicators will push the CHF decidedly up from current levels appears questionable to say the least," he points out. EUR/CHF at 1.3737. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0704 GMT [Dow Jones] Switzerland's decision to opt for a new bond issue November 10 is probably aimed at boosting liquidity at the ultra-long end of the franc yield curve, and also to fund itself at historically low yields, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "In the light of the solid fiscal situation of the Swiss government, in August the Finance Ministry said they're on track for another surplus for '10, the sale can be seen as a more active management of financial markets by the Swiss Treasury," he notes. In addition "there are no signs of a tighter \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  monetary policy whatsoever," he says. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0658 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.2% at 1508.50 in afternoon session, after touching fresh 33-month high of 1511.24 in early trade. "We may see some profit-taking pressure later in the day, as people will probably retreat to the sidelines and wait for the outcome of the U.S. Fed's FOMC meeting later in the week," says dealer. Tips support at 1505, then 1500 on downside, with new high acting as resistance. Gainers led by plantation, finance stocks including CIMB Group (1023.KU) +0.8% at MYR8.36, Kulim (2003.KU) +4.3% at MYR10.12, \uc2 IJM Plantations\uc2  (2216.KU) +1.8% at MYR2.85. Decliners include \uc2 KFC Holdings\uc2  (3492.KU) down 1.4% at MYR3.57, \uc2 Malaysian Resources\uc2  (1651.KU) down 1.4% at MYR2.05.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD touches 1-week high above 1.4000, rising briefly to high of 1.4012, with short-term investors buying risk-sensitive common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as China share prices rise, dealers say. After data earlier reflect strength in gauges of manufacturing activity in China, with official PMI rising to 54.7 in October vs September's 53.8, Shanghai Composite last +2.1%. Dealers say EUR/USD may now test initial resistance at 1.4030. Last at 1.3990. Meanwhile, traders say USD weighed by continued speculation that FOMC will announce further easing steps at end of its two-day meeting Wednesday. USD Index falls to intraday low at 76.733, last 76.854.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly higher; Nikkei down 0.5%, S&P/ASX +0.8%, HSI +2.1%, Shanghai Composite +2.1%, STI +1.5%, NZX-50 off 0.2%, Kospi +1.7%, Taiex +1.1%, Sensex +1.7%, SET +1.0%, KLCI +0.2%, Philippine market shut for a holiday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, after touching 15-year low, USD spiked up more than Y1 vs JPY in early Asia trade, sparking talk of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but pair quickly lost half its gains, attention shifted to talk of trading by major fund or possible misfire in jittery market. USD/JPY pulled back, last at 80.43 vs 80.40 in late NY trade Friday. EUR/USD at 1.3990 vs 1.3947; EUR/JPY at 112.55 vs 111.99. China's October CFLP PMI at 54.7 vs 53.8 in September, October \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI at 54.8 vs 52.9 September; PMI reading above 50 indicates expansion. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Taiwan October PMI at 48.6 vs 49.0 in September. Korea October CPI +4.1% on-year vs +3.6% in September, +3.4% expected; October exports +29.9% on-year vs +22.1% expected, imports +22.4% on-year vs +22.1% expected; boosts trade surplus to $6.914 billion vs $4.37 billion surplus expected; October \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI fat 46.75 seasonally-adjusted vs 48.81 in September. Australia 3Q house prices +0.1% vs 2Q; nation's October TD-MI Inflation Gauge +0.3%, industry group's October Performance of Manufacturing Index +2.1 points on-month at 49.4. Thailand October CPI +2.8% on-year vs +3.0% in September, +3.1% expected. Indonesia October CPI +5.67% on-year vs +5.80% in September, +5.81% expected; September trade surplus at $2.55 billion vs $1.49 billion surplus in August, $998.5 million surplus expected. Spot gold at $1363.70/oz, up $3.90 from NY close Friday; silver hits new 30-year high at $25.05/oz, up 30 cents since NY close. December Nymex crude oil futures up 42 cents at $81.85/bbl on Globex.(wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0637 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 9.3% at HK$2.485 billion midday, 4.5% of total market volume vs 5.5% at Friday's half-day when excluding AIA's (1299.HK) trading impact, suggesting bears deterred by HSI's 1.9% rebound. A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) tops list with HK$251.23 million, followed by China Life (2628.HK) with HK$168.01 million, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$109.97 million. Cnooc (0883.HK), \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$106.53 million, HK$99.32 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 HSBC\uc2  warns investors not to get too comfortable with expectation China to continue gradual appreciation of CNY vs USD, saying USD/CNY will most likely in 2-way moves going forward. "Indeed, some authorities we met firmly emphasized the 'flexibility' and 'reference to a basket' aspects of the CNY regime, as the PBOC has also described," says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . Adds, while CNY gradually appreciated vs USD in six weeks leading to G-20, during which period USD generally weak, CNY depreciated vs broad basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in same period. "This just shows the difficulty of concluding that the price action in USD-CNY in the past few months signified a politically driven shift in CNY policy," says house. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0623 GMT [Dow Jones] Sell pricey EUR/USD 1-year volatility via long 1-year 1.2455/1.5225 double no-touch for 12% of payout or, for spot bulls, via long 1-year 1.3850 call with 1.2455/1.5225 double knockouts for just 0.25% EUR, says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus senior analyst Russell Floyd; 1-year implied vol now highest since June, among widest risk premiums among 23 pairs tracked by CommentaryPlus models--likely overpricing spot reaction to US mid-term elections, FOMC, Floyd says. See =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEWS: Short Euro/Dollar Vega Via DNT Or DKO Call (russell.floyd@dowjones.com) [ 11-01-10 0314ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101101e6b10004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
