\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/USD Touches 1-Week High Above 1.4000\b0\par\par\uc2 1,793 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2010\par 07:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD touches 1-week high above 1.4000, rising briefly to high of 1.4012, with short-term investors buying risk-sensitive common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as China share prices rise, dealers say. After data earlier reflect strength in gauges of manufacturing activity in China, with official PMI rising to 54.7 in October vs September's 53.8, Shanghai Composite last +2.1%. Dealers say EUR/USD may now test initial resistance at 1.4030. Last at 1.3990. Meanwhile, traders say USD weighed by continued speculation that FOMC will announce further easing steps at end of its two-day meeting Wednesday. USD Index falls to intraday low at 76.733, last 76.854.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly higher; Nikkei down 0.5%, S&P/ASX +0.8%, HSI +2.1%, Shanghai Composite +2.1%, STI +1.5%, NZX-50 off 0.2%, Kospi +1.7%, Taiex +1.1%, Sensex +1.7%, SET +1.0%, KLCI +0.2%, Philippine market shut for a holiday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, after touching 15-year low, USD spiked up more than Y1 vs JPY in early Asia trade, sparking talk of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but pair quickly lost half its gains, attention shifted to talk of trading by major fund or possible misfire in jittery market. USD/JPY pulled back, last at 80.43 vs 80.40 in late NY trade Friday. EUR/USD at 1.3990 vs 1.3947; EUR/JPY at 112.55 vs 111.99. China's October CFLP PMI at 54.7 vs 53.8 in September, October \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI at 54.8 vs 52.9 September; PMI reading above 50 indicates expansion. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Taiwan October PMI at 48.6 vs 49.0 in September. Korea October CPI +4.1% on-year vs +3.6% in September, +3.4% expected; October exports +29.9% on-year vs +22.1% expected, imports +22.4% on-year vs +22.1% expected; boosts trade surplus to $6.914 billion vs $4.37 billion surplus expected; October \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI fat 46.75 seasonally-adjusted vs 48.81 in September. Australia 3Q house prices +0.1% vs 2Q; nation's October TD-MI Inflation Gauge +0.3%, industry group's October Performance of Manufacturing Index +2.1 points on-month at 49.4. Thailand October CPI +2.8% on-year vs +3.0% in September, +3.1% expected. Indonesia October CPI +5.67% on-year vs +5.80% in September, +5.81% expected; September trade surplus at $2.55 billion vs $1.49 billion surplus in August, $998.5 million surplus expected. Spot gold at $1363.70/oz, up $3.90 from NY close Friday; silver hits new 30-year high at $25.05/oz, up 30 cents since NY close. December Nymex crude oil futures up 42 cents at $81.85/bbl on Globex.(wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0637 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 9.3% at HK$2.485 billion midday, 4.5% of total market volume vs 5.5% at Friday's half-day when excluding AIA's (1299.HK) trading impact, suggesting bears deterred by HSI's 1.9% rebound. A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) tops list with HK$251.23 million, followed by China Life (2628.HK) with HK$168.01 million, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$109.97 million. Cnooc (0883.HK), \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$106.53 million, HK$99.32 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 HSBC\uc2  warns investors not to get too comfortable with expectation China to continue gradual appreciation of CNY vs USD, saying USD/CNY will most likely in 2-way moves going forward. "Indeed, some authorities we met firmly emphasized the 'flexibility' and 'reference to a basket' aspects of the CNY regime, as the PBOC has also described," says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . Adds, while CNY gradually appreciated vs USD in six weeks leading to G-20, during which period USD generally weak, CNY depreciated vs broad basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in same period. "This just shows the difficulty of concluding that the price action in USD-CNY in the past few months signified a politically driven shift in CNY policy," says house. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0623 GMT [Dow Jones] Sell pricey EUR/USD 1-year volatility via long 1-year 1.2455/1.5225 double no-touch for 12% of payout or, for spot bulls, via long 1-year 1.3850 call with 1.2455/1.5225 double knockouts for just 0.25% EUR, says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus senior analyst Russell Floyd; 1-year implied vol now highest since June, among widest risk premiums among 23 pairs tracked by CommentaryPlus models--likely overpricing spot reaction to US mid-term elections, FOMC, Floyd says. See =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEWS: Short Euro/Dollar Vega Via DNT Or DKO Call (russell.floyd@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0615 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA likely to keep cash rate on hold at 4.50% as expectations of hike still low after hike-camp took hit last week following weaker-than-expected 3Q inflation data; 30-day interbank futures now pricing in just 25% chance of hike, down from above 50% week ago. In a survey of 18 economists by Dow Jones, 12 forecast RBA will keep rates steady Tuesday, rest tip 25 bp hike. "They have the luxury to wait and why wouldn't they, given the only downside risks to the outlook they describe are to the global outlook not the domestic one and--just one day after the RBA's meeting--the Fed will enlighten us on how they are going to mitigate (or not) those global risks," said \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 ' Strategist Annette Beacher. Still, those tipping hike note inflation remains at high-end of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2%-3% target band. RBA decision due at 0330 GMT tomorrow. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW down late at 1,116.6 vs 1,125.3 late Friday in Seoul, remains under selling pressure amid broadly weaker USD in Asian trade. But further downside limited with market growing wary over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders say. "The pair has stalled around the 1,116 level," with offshore players rather quiet after selling USD in morning trade, says local bank trader. Foreign bank trader tips pair in 1,100-1,130 range for rest of week. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0607 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +1.1% at 8379.75, 29-month high, driven by hot money inflows and strength in heavy electronics stocks on strong 4Q guidance, says KGI Securities trader Randy Chang. Tips Taiex may test 8450 Tuesday, key support at 8300. "Abundant liquidity will continue to lend support to the local stock market. HTC (2498.TW) is likely to rise further Tuesday given its strong 4Q guidance and share buybacks." Electronics stocks led the Taiex's rally, HTC +7.0%, hit limit up at NT$739.00, Acer (2353.TW) +4.5% at NT$92.90 and TSMC (2330.TW) +1.1% at NT$63.50. But \uc2 Chimei Innolux\uc2  (3481.TW) ends down 4.5% after reporting wider-than-expected 3Q net loss of NT$3.64 billion amid falling LCD panel prices, industry-wide inventory surpluses. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0602 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds off highs after federal finance secretary says high inflation still a concern, while noting liquidity easing measures not signal of monetary policy stance; 8.13% 2022 bond at 100.37 vs 100.45 pre-comments, 100.30 at last close. "The statements, coming a day ahead of the monetary policy, show that the government will not be comfortable with high prices," says dealer with private bank. Besides, upbeat reading on health of economy from \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI may give RBI more comfort in hiking rates. Tips 12-year paper to trade in 100.10-100.50 range in session. RBI widely expected to raise rates at rate setting meeting Tuesday. Seasonally adjusted \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI, prepared by Markit, rose to 57.2 in October from 55.1 in September. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0556 GMT [Dow Jones] Presence of two of OPEC's most senior ministers at Singapore energy event--Saudi's Naimi and Qatar's al-Attiyah--giving little for markets to chew on; their comments so far uninteresting, repetition of well-worn lines about current price range being at acceptable levels; more of note was remark Sunday from Angola's oil minister speaking in Delhi, saying his country should get higher quota, may indicate OPEC faces discord on individual output at next meeting. (simon.hall@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0549 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +1.0% at 994.02 midday pause; moderate trade. PTT group shares, other big cap stocks buoyant. "Investors again hope that the size of the U.S.'s potential quantitative measures will be significant, so they are on a flurry of share buying," says analyst with BFIT Securities Aphisit Limsupanark. Focus on details of quantitative easing measures widely expected to be unveiled in FOMC's meeting later this week. Resistance tipped at tough 1000-mark, support at 988-990 area. Among most active, PTT Aromatics & Refining (PTTAR.TH) +5.0% at THB31.25, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +2.6% at THB175.50, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +1.4% at THB143, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +1.0% at THB782, PTT (PTT.TH) +0.7% at THB305. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares down 0.3% midday at 3624.772 in moderate volume as some investors take profit on several blue chips after the companies reported weaker-than-expected earnings; still inflows expected to limit declines, dealers say. "Investors are looking more to earnings than CPI data, but expect the better-than-expected CPI data to prompt more foreign buys and limit the index's fall," dealer says; tips 3618 support. Bellweather Telkom (TLKM.JK) down 6.6% at IDR8,500 after reporting weak 9M10 earnings. Coal miner Bukit Asam (PTBA.JK) down 1.8% at IDR19,300, also on below-view earnings. Food producer \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) down 2.9% at IDR5,050 after short rally last week.(andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com) [ 11-01-10 0254ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101101e6b100042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
