\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: U.S. Corp Taxes Eyed For Japan Stock Impact-Daiwa\b0\par\par\uc2 1,621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2010\par 02:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets notes Nikkei Stock Average has slipped from lower end of the tight range developed over past 7 trading days, indicating risk of further downside short-term. But says market could find support from Topix (approaching September 1 year-to-date intraday low), as well as TSE 1st section advance-decline ratio (approaching key support at 70% level). Adds, although continues to harbor high expectations for capacity growth (employment and capex), reports of a potential (U.S. corporate) tax break could cause businesses to postpone planned capital investments to 2011. Still, however, says a lower corporate tax bill could trigger surge in hiring and capital spending in the U.S. "Such developments would provide a significant tailwind to the Japanese economy, possibly setting the stage for a sharp improvement in Japanese equities, especially if the yen were to weaken against the dollar," writes analyst in report. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0143 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open up, after China's official PMI rises to 54.7 in October vs September's 53.8, indicating continued growth in manufacturing activity. Shanghai Composite Index +1.0% at 3008.37, faces immediate resistance at 3050 level. "There are still some concerns of inflationary pressures, but China's economy is on a sound footing and the fourth-quarter economic activity is likely to be stable," says Zheshang Securities analyst Zhang Yanbin. Among actives, \uc2 China Construction Bank Corp\uc2 . (601939.SH) +1.2% at CNY5.10, \uc2 China Shenhua Energy\uc2  (601088.SH) +0.4% at CNY28.70. Shenzhen Composite Index +1.0% at 1317.07. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] The strong reading on China's official October PMI, up at 54.7 from 53.8 in September, "suggests that domestic demand is quite robust," says Bank of America-Merrill Lynch economist Lu Ting. Overall output increased even as new export orders fell slightly, Lu says, suggesting that domestic orders saw a big boost. Lu says the strong reading gives policymakers "more room" to allow yuan appreciation; notes still small chance of rate hike this year. (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB slips to 29.88-29.89 vs 29.97 late Friday in tandem with broadly softer USD vs regionals. "Despite speculation over suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in the morning, the baht remains stronger against the U.S. dollar as investors are paying more attention to potential quantitative easing measures," says Bangkok-based dealer. Market usually expects Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to follow in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's footsteps in terms of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Focus on FOMC meeting later this week with Fed widely expected to unveil further quantitative easing measures to stimulate U.S. economy. Also, investors awaiting Thailand's October CPI due around 0700 GMT. Pair tipped to trade in 29.85-29.92 range. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.2% at 1509.49 led by gains in finance, property and plantation plays on expectations of decent 3Q earnings. Dealer says lower-liners attracting more interest; "there's some selective bargain-buying there...but trading in blue-chips is a bit quieter with some players consolidating gains," says dealer. Tips 1515 resistance, 1505 immediate support, then 1500. Market breadth positive with 189 advancers vs 77 decliners; plantation plays Kulim (2003.KU) +1.4% at MYR9.84, \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) +0.9% at MYR19.98, Sunrise (6165.KU) +3.5% at MYR2.36, RHB Cap (1066.KU) +1.0% at MYR8.11. Decliners MISC (3816.KU) down 0.9% at MYR8.70; Axiata (6888.KU) down 0.4% at MYR4.47. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] China official PMI rises to 54.7 in October from 53.8 in September, showing manufacturing activity continues to pick up strength, but high input price reading is likely to raise concerns over inflationary pressures and possible future tightening. Input prices subindex rises to 69.9 in October from 65.3 in September. China Federation of Logistics and Purchasing analyst Zhang Liqun says high input price reading "indicates that companies' cost pressures are building." Adds it's "not appropriate to be overly optimistic" on outlook for China economy. (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot silver spiked to 30-year $25.02/oz high, spot palladium hit 9-year high of $650.75/oz in early trade but both metals have now fallen back; spot silver at $24.83/oz, up 8 cents, spot palladium at $645/oz, down $1. Palladium rallied 3.5% Friday with pundits talking about potential for further gains on dwindling of suspicious supply from Russia; "a slowdown in palladium supply from potentially exhausted Russian state stocks creates a more constructive fundamental base longer term," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Silver, which rallied 3% Friday, harder to explain especially given industrial metals like copper fell Friday. Some market participants believe ongoing U.S. CFTC investigation into alleged manipulation of Comex silver futures is forcing some large players to cover outstanding short positions, keeping market well bid.(james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may continue rangebound trade, not only today, but until U.S. Fed meeting results unveiled early HK Thursday morning, when size, scope, implementation method of much-awaited QE2 will be unveiled. While most argue QE2 expectation being factored in, "it could still support market sentiment," says Citic Securities; adds, "the coming QE2 no matter how large the size is will be another boost for capital inflow to China." Also says benchmark index likely to trade around 23,000 for session. \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK) set to fall after below-view 3Q results, whereas \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK), China Shenhua's (1088.HK) 3Q results both solid, although mostly being priced-in. HSI ended Friday down 0.5% at 23,096.32, off 1.8% for week, extending 1.0% loss previous week, which ended 7 consecutive weeks of gains started Sept. 1. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to extend consolidation for now after settling down 75 cents Friday at $81.43/bbl, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart mixed as MACD bearish; but stochastics neutral, while 5-day & 15-day moving averages meandering sideways. Resistance at $82.12 (Friday's high); breach would target $82.64 (Thursday's high), then $82.88 (Tuesday's high), $83.28-$83.33 band (Oct. 25 high-Oct. 15 high) and $84.12 (Oct. 14 high). Support at $80.56-$80.52 band (Friday's low-Wednesday's low); breach would tilt near-term outlook negative, targeting $80.09-$80.00 band (Oct. 21 low-psychological level), then $79.25 (Oct. 19 reaction low) and $78.01 (200-day moving average). December crude last up 42 cents at $81.85/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea exports rise more than anticipated in October, helping economy record another robust trade surplus of $6.914 billion (September trade surplus at $4.53 billion), and lending some comfort after recent concerns over a faster-than-expected economic slowdown in wake of September production data. In October, exports up 29.9% on year at $44.12 billion, exceeding 22.1% rise forecast by economists in Dow Jones poll, while imports +22.4% at $37.20 billion, also tad faster than +22.1% expected, show preliminary data from commerce ministry; economists had forecast a $4.37 billion surplus. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0110 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures tad down on data showing October consumer price index +4.1% on-year earlier, far faster than +3.4% forecast and accelerating vs +3.6% in preceding month. But fall not so steep as market focuses on core inflation readings, which strip out energy and food costs, +1.9% on-year and flat on-month, which implies high headline inflation could be temporary, participants say; 3-year yield +1 bp at 3.26%, December futures down 2 ticks at 112.55. Also, many investors sidelined ahead of FOMC meeting later this week in anticipation of extra stimulus from U.S., says bond manager at local securities firm. Notes, bargain-hunting for longer-end bonds kicking in following steep losses Friday, providing support. Tips futures in 112.40-112.90 range for day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,358.60/oz, down $1.20 since NY close and off intraday high of $1,365.70, with follow-up buying fading, after Friday's NY session saw 1.2% rally in gold despite fairly stable \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and a lack of obvious cues. "On the physical market, Indian buyers were bargain-hunting as scrap sellers were holding back for higher prices," says MKS Finance. Indian festival of Deepavali reaches its climax this week which likely to keep physical market firm, despite near record prices. MKS adds, stops were hit on Comex Friday after heavy buying came in after uninspiring U.S. GDP data. "(Gold) is now trading in a more healthy market after having undergone a correction followed by a consolidation phase. We still expect the metal to remain rangebound until the outcome of the FOMC meeting is released," says Switzerland based MKS. Fed meeting statement to be released late Wednesday. (james.campbell@dowjones.com) [ 10-31-10 2145ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101101e6b100015\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
