\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Talk Of Fund-Buys,System Error On USD/JPY -Dealers\b0\par\par\uc2 1,410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2010\par 02:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY spike of more than one yen to 81.55 came amid talk of buying by global macro funds, says trader at European brokerage. Casting further doubt on speculation that move may have been driven by Japanese government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , other dealers cite rumors that sharp move may have been caused by technical error with electronic trading system. Pair quickly fell back, last at 80.59.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely down, weighed by foreign investors' selling, fall in USD/KRW, says local bank trader. "The pace of the pair's fall could be fast (today)"; notes \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eyed to only mildly slow pair's drop last week. Tips test of 30.600 support today vs 30.782 late Friday. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to continue to consolidate Monday, as investors stay on sidelines ahead of U.S. midterm elections Tuesday, Fed meeting Wednesday. Taiwan International Securities analyst Andrew Teng tips Taiex in 8200-8350 range today, says market lacks momentum; volume shrank to around NT$106 billion Friday vs Thursday's NT$128 billion. "The benchmark index is unlikely to challenge 8395 resistance as there are lots of uncertainties...concerning U.S. monetary policy." Says airline stocks may rise in near term, after Songshan-Haneda direct flights started yesterday, months-long Taipei Flora Expo to open this weekend may draw in more tourists. Adds, investors may take profit in tech stocks such as TSMC (2330.TW), which reported upbeat results. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely lower, tracking USD weakness Friday, with USD/CNY fixing tipped around 6.6880 vs Friday's 6.6908, says Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank. Adds, USD/CNY may trade in 6.6700-6.6900 band vs 6.6708 last close. "The dollar fell further Friday, but not by as much as it did on Thursday, so we expect the PBOC to set central parity just a tad lower," trader says. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0043 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bond yield curve expected to flatten in Asia session, in line with Treasury yields Friday. Trader expects yields down 2-3 bps in Asia; market participants have now revised up expectations of scope of expected Federal Reserve quantitative easing action this week following U.S. GDP, inflation numbers last week. Friday, 2-year yield ended flat at 0.44%, 10-year yield ended 3 bps lower at 1.98%.(samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0042 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea October CPI, which at 20-month high of +4.1%, raises chances of a BOK rate hike to 2.5% in November, say economists. "Our current house view is (a rate) freeze until the end of the year, but the BOK's monetary policy board will definitely care more about it" now and a rate hike can't be ruled out, says IBK Securities economist Yoon Chang-yong. HI Investment & Securities economist Park Sang-hyun, who forecasts 25-bp hike in November as annual inflation well above midpoint of BOK inflation target band of 2%-4% for 2 straight months now, says inflation to take center of attention in November BOK meeting; notes timing for a hike may not be good for growth in Korean economy, but inflation may stay high - between around 3%-3.5% in November, December - "so the rate should be raised once before the year is out." Park forecasts BOK tightening to resume late 1Q after November hike. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last +0.2% at 1885.70 as foreigners' net selling, cautious sentiment keeping main index from rising sharply; "some investors appear concerned that the Fed's meeting this week may not take enough quantitative easing measures to satisfy market demands," says Lee Kyoung-min at \uc2 Woori Investment & Securities\uc2 ; tips Kospi to hover around 1890 today. Foreigners net seller of stocks worth KRW54.3 billion. Depending on outcome of FOMC meeting this week, China PMI data today, Kospi may test 1900 resistance this week. Top gainers include transportation, engineering stocks; "strong 4Q earnings forecasts are boosting auto plays and expectations of increased plant orders are helping Samsung Engineering." \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +4.7% at KRW178,000, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) +7.0% at KRW48,050, Hankook Tire Manufacturing (000240.SE) +3.1% at KRW30,150, Samsung Engineering (028050.SE) +6.1% at KRW190,500. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR lower at 3.0970 vs 3.1100 Friday; local dealer says pair down on USD weakness but in thin volume; "players won't want to take aggressive positions ahead of the FOMC meeting," dealer says. Tips market to remain quiet today; pair likely in 3.0900-3.1100 range. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0035 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.3% at 9177.83 after opening lower, briefly breaking into positive territory, then falling again. USD/JPY jumped at stock market open to 81.55 from 80.40 (now 80.63); traders speculate global macro funds (investing in wide range of assets, including \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and equities) bought USD and sold JPY. "The equities market is reacting as if there has been no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says trader at European brokerage, adding that foreign funds' buying USD likely snapped up Japan stocks temporarily as well; says such rallies in stock market likely be short-lived. "Investors are se