\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Have Flat End To Strong October; Eyes On FOMC\b0\par\par\uc2 2,932 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2010\par 23:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Philippines markets closed for All Saints Day.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11118.49  +4.54  +0.04% \par Nasdaq     2507.41  +0.04  +0.00% \par S&P 500    1183.26  -0.52  -0.04% \par FTSE 100   5675.16  -2.73  -0.05% \par Xetra DAX  6601.37  +6.09  +0.09% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100 2/32  +1/32 0.344% -2.3BP \par 5Y Tsy 100 11/32 +10/32 1.176% -6.4BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 4/32 +15/32 2.610% -5.7BP \par  \par USD/JPY       80.32-34     -0.14% \par Range         80.39-80.30 \par EUR/USD       1.3962-64    +0.24% \par Range         1.3965-1.3945 \par AUD/USD       0.9851-54    +0.37% \par Range         0.9853-0.9844 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   11.45/11.75% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   13.68/14.03% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   15.05/15.35% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,357.60 +$15.10 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,335.20-$1,359.70 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $81.43   -$0.75 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. blue-chip stocks edged higher Friday while the Standard & Poor's 500 index fell slightly in a quiet end to a strong October as investors bided their time before the coming week's Federal Reserve meeting and midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed up 4.54 points, or 0.04%, to 11118.49.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  was among the Dow's best performers with a 1.5% rise in its stock. The software giant's fiscal first-quarter profit climbed 51%, benefiting from a continued strong response to the Windows 7 operating system and Office 2010. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  was also one of the Dow's best performers this month, up 8.9% over the period.\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2  and Merck helped limit the Dow's gain. \uc2 Chevron\uc2  fell 2.2% after the oil major's third-quarter earnings and revenue missed analysts' expectations. Merck dropped 1.7% as the company's earnings excluding items topped Wall Street estimates, but revenue fell short of forecasts.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite rose 0.04 point to 2507.41. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index edged down 0.52, or 0.04%, to 1183.26. The health-care sector led to the downside for the S&P 500 as Merck weighed.\par \par \uc2 Friday, which marks the 81st anniversary of the Crash of 1929 known as "Black Tuesday," was the final trading day of what has been a strong October for stocks. The Dow climbed 3.06% during the month and the S&P 500 rose 3.69% for October, the Dow's best October since 2006 and the S&P 500's best October since 2003.\par \par \uc2 This month's advance has come on growing expectations that the coming week the Federal Reserve will announce more stimulus, and the midterm federal elections will lead to a Republican takeover of the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 "We've got a huge week next week," said Jim Paulsen, chief investment strategist at \uc2 Wells Capital Management\uc2 . "Today's market may just be the perfect reflection of that quandary--how big a position do you want to take leading into that?"\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended lower for the fourth consecutive session Friday because of profit-taking in property firms and brokerages ahead of the Federal Reserve's policy-setting meeting.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely consolidate around the 3000 key level for a few more sessions as the market needs to digest sharp gains accumulated earlier this month. The Shanghai index rose 15% from Oct. 8 to Oct. 25.\par \par \uc2 "Investors aren't sure whether the U.S. dollar has much more downside, so they are trying to lock in profits in property companies and securities firms," said Li Nian, an analyst at Shenyin Wanguo Securities.\par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve will discuss a plan to buy government bonds on a large scale at the Nov. 2-3 Federal Open Market Committee meeting. Investors are concerned the dollar will strengthen if the Fed plans a bond-buying program that is less than a few hundred billion dollars over several months.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell on Friday to a seven-week low as disappointing economic data and yen strengthening more than offset generally good corporate earnings and raised outlooks.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are focusing on companies that are raising their outlooks," said Naoki Fujiwara, fund manager at Shinkin Asset Management, adding that the trend for corporate earnings is that most manufacturers seem to be keeping their full-year outlooks after posting robust results for the fiscal first half.\par \par \uc2 Investors were also cautious ahead of the week's U.S. Federal Open Market Committee meeting.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended lower Friday after several blue-chip firms reported weaker-than-expected quarterly results, but market volume got a boost from the strong investor interest in \uc2 AIA Group\uc2 , which surged in its much-anticipated debut.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect the index to consolidate more in the coming few sessions, but further declines will provide opportunities to take positions for a likely year-end rally.\par \par \uc2 "(At today's intraday low), the Hang Seng Index has corrected about 1,000 points from the peak of the latest rally at 23,866 (hit Oct. 14), which probably is not enough. But another 500-point fall should present a chance to get in," said Francis Lun, general manager at Fulbright Securities.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures lost ground Friday in U.S. trading after a report showed only modest U.S. economic growth in the third quarter, capping a week where prices stayed in a narrow range.\par \par \uc2 Gold prices were helped higher Friday as the downbeat economic data and rising uncertainty about the coming week's FOMC meeting saw investors re-enter the market.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange pared early losses after the dollar slipped on weaker-than-expected U.S. GDP data, but still finished down on the day and on the week, consolidating at lower levels following a week of choppy trading across broader financial markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell to a fresh 15-year low against the yen Friday as the specter of another round of Federal Reserve asset purchases loomed, while investors also cast cautious eyes toward a possible terrorism plot.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar dropped below Y80.41, dipping as low as Y80.37, its lowest level since 1995, as investors continued to pare their positions in the dollar ahead of the coming week's expected decision by the Fed to possibly flood the market with more liquidity, which would likely weigh on the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Confirmation by President Barack Obama of a possible terrorism plot against the U.S. might also have pushed investors into the perceived safety of the yen, said Brian Kim, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Stamford, Conn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You could maybe say that it gave it a little bit of a push," Kim said of Obama's speech and the dollar's fall to a fresh 15-year low against the yen. "I don't think it's been a major driver, but any terrorism alerts make a little risk aversion" kick in, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the risk-positive euro actually gained ground against the dollar during Obama's remarks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , also dropped to an intraday low late Friday, though its decline was modest, losing about 0.3% on the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Friday in New York, the euro was at $1.3947 from $1.3940 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y80.40 from Y81.02, while the euro was at Y111.99 from Y112.86. The U.K. pound was at $1.6039 from $1.5936. The dollar was at CHF0.9824 from CHF0.9832.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index was at 77.047 from 77.294.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar also fell against the euro, which has benefited in recent weeks from the speculation swirling around the Fed's possible re-ignition of what is known as quantitative easing, or the purchase of Treasurys to push down longer-term interest rates to kick-start a sluggish U.S. economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors returned to the recent trend of filing out of the dollar in the wake of Friday's U.S. data, which largely disappointed. Those results added weight to expectations that more Fed stimulus waits in the wings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar had earlier Friday been on a weakening trend following what was mostly disappointing U.S. data, including a weak reading on consumer sentiment and third-quarter gross-domestic-product data that slightly missed expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "All of this data just really reinforces the existing themes already established," said Lena Komileva, head of G7 market economics at Tullett Prebon in London. "The U.S. is growing--just not fast enough."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are fixed on Wednesday's decision by the policymaking Federal Market Open Committee on whether or not to initiate another round of asset purchases; if the dollar weakens further on the heels of fresh easing, it could trigger steeper losses against the yen, with the dollar's all-time low of Y79.75 in sight, Kim said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That would put investors on high alert for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by Japanese officials concerned about yen strength. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  massively in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in mid-September to prop up the sagging dollar against the yen, whose strength weighs on exporters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan Thursday brought forward its next policy board meeting to Nov. 4-5 from Nov. 15-16. The new timing comes just after the FOMC's, with analysts suspecting the BOJ could be on guard to respond to any dollar weakness.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has for the first time allowed cross-border investment to be settled in yuan, expanding a trial program that had originally targeted trade settlement only, the official Xinhua news agency reported Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The trial program has started in the western Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous region, Xinhua said, without giving details on how the mechanism works.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys rallied Friday as the latest bout of disappointing U.S. data boosted optimism that the Federal Reserve will print more dollars to support the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market recouped some losses for the week and the month. Still, Treasurys were a laggard in October, beaten by U.S. stocks; high-yield, high-risk corporate debt; and government bonds that offer inflation protection to investors. [ 31-10-10 2208GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's data showed the U.S. economy grew at an annual rate of 2% in the third quarter, slightly below the 2.1% forecast by economists. A major inflation gauge slipped further below the Fed's desired 1.7% to 2% range. The Reuters/University of Michigan consumer confidence survey unexpectedly dropped this month to 67.7.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The reports came before U.S. midterm elections Tuesday and a Fed meeting Tuesday and Wednesday. More than a year after the recession ended, stubbornly high unemployment could hurt Democrats in Congress.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The near 10% jobless rate is likely to be a key factor in getting the Fed to resume government bond purchases, known as quantitative easing, in a bid to push down long-term borrowing costs for consumers and businesses. The potential second round of quantitative easing is referred to as QE2.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Two percent GDP is not what the Fed wants to see," said David Coard, head of fixed-income trading in New York at Williams Capital Group. "The QE2 is the key driver for the markets."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nick Brophy, head of North America rates trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  Global Markets in New York, said: "Our flows have turned bullish as real money investors, domestic and international, have started to put money to work [in the Treasury market] and take advantage of the increase in bond yields we had over the last few weeks."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The five-year and seven-year notes were the best performers, fueled by speculation that they will be the favorite for the Fed's bond-buying program. Their yields are linked to home refinances.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 30-year bond received support from a report from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The firm said the long bond will be included in the Fed's bond-buying plan and will outperform the benchmark 10-year note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 2000 GMT, the benchmark 10-year note was up 15/32 to yield 2.610%. The 30-year bond was 1 1/32 higher to yield 3.994%. Bond prices move inversely to yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year note's yield, the benchmark for consumer and corporate borrowings, has increased nearly 30 basis points from the recent trough earlier this month, as many investors cut bets on expectations of a dramatic-sized Fed bond-purchase program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds gained slightly Friday on safe-haven plays, but further upside is likely limited due to concerns over inflation, a Guotai Junan Securities trader said, tipping index resistance at 126.5 this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai Securities News cited Fan Jianping, a senior economist at the State Information Center, as saying he expects the country to face strong inflationary pressure in the next two years and the government to introduce further tightening measures in the property market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds ended lower Friday, as players adjusted their positions ahead of a closely watched U.S. monetary policy decision next week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But once the results of the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee are out Nov. 3, those who had held back will likely become active buyers again, possibly pushing the 10-year yield to as low as 0.9%, a trader at a Japanese bank said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also likely to support the JGB market next week is the decision taken Thursday by the Bank of Japan to move forward its next policy board meeting to Nov. 4-5 from Nov. 15-16. Since the new timing comes just after the FOMC meeting, market participants are speculating that Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may loosen policy further if the Federal Reserve announces aggressive quantitative easing measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa has denied such speculation, saying the change of date was aimed at starting its new asset-purchase program quickly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year yield is likely to trade in a 0.82%-0.97% range--levels seen since the BOJ adopted comprehensive easing earlier this month--until the end of this year, said Akito Fukunaga, chief strategist at RBS Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is unlikely to depart this range for purely monetary-policy reasons, and other factors (such as revived concerns over European financial firms) would have to be involved for it to break out (of the range)," Fukunaga said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data due Monday, Thai inflation likely rose slightly in October due to higher food prices following floods in many parts of country, though the baht's rise partly offset that impact. A Dow Jones poll of 8 economists puts the median CPI forecast at a rise of 3.1% on the year compared with a rise of 3.0% in September, and a rise of 0.3% on the month compared with a 0.07% decline. October core CPI likely rose 1.1% on the year and 0.1% on the month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US GDP Expands, Consumer Spending Up \par China Should Build More Public Houses To Avoid Supply Shortage - Xinhua \par China Starts Yuan Investment Settlement Trial--Xinhua \par Japan's Exporters Strive To Soften Rising Yen's Impact -Nikkei \par China Gives Assurances On Rare-Earth Minerals \par Rongsheng Raises As Much As US$2.28 Bln In HK IPO - Source \par China, Asean Target Two-Way Trade At $500 Bln A Year By 2015 \par Next Singapore Election Must Produce Future PM - Goh \par Singapore's Lim Makes S$513M Offer For \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thomson Medical Centre\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par BlackBerry Maker Says 'Pleased' With Progress Of Access Talks With India \par China Foreign Min Urges US To Be 'Cautious' On Diaoyu Islands \par US, UK Aid Yemen In Probe \par Greek PM: Early National Elections No Idle Bluff \par ECB's Mersch: New Capital Rules Hurt European Banks, Economy \par Portugal Govt, Opposition Reach Agreement On 2011 Budget \par Potash Corp. In Talks With 15 Potential Buyers \par AIG Gets $20.51B From AIA IPO \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chevron\f28 \uc2 \f2  Profit Down 1.6% After 2009 Gains \par Geithner, Barnier Vow To Cooperate On Reform \par Mitsubishi Near End Of China Venture Talks \par Merck Profit Falls 90%; Books $950m Vioxx Charge \par S Korea Banks' 3Q Total Net Profit KRW2.7 Trillion \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 0001  UK           CBI SME Trends Survey predicts growth in domestic demand in \par                    next three months \par 0001  UK           October Hometrack Monthly National House Prices Survey \par          