\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Up 0.7%; Financials, Miners Lead\b0\par\par\uc2 1,507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2010\par 00:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.7% at 4691.8, led by financials, miners in quiet trade before major events this week. Consolidation within 4660-4700 expected today, barring surprises from China manufacturing data at 0100 GMT. "There are obviously some bargains in financials after good results last week and miners are rising on the back of the U.S. dollar fall overnight," says RBS Morgans Investment Adviser Chris McDonald. "I think we will see the strength moderate as we get some nervousness about the outcome of the mid-term elections and the size of quantitative easing in the U.S." Says Wall Street factored in US$1 trillion QE, so smaller QE could cause "downside shock" in equities. But if Republicans win House of Representatives, Senate majorities, Wall Street should see a "massive rally" before year end amid expectations of business friendly conditions in the U.S. BHP (BHP.AU) up 0.9%, major banks up 1.0%-1.8%. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall in early trade vs 1,125.3 late Friday in Seoul tracking USD losses vs some majors late last week ahead of much-awaited announcement of possible extra stimulus from Fed at FOMC meeting later this week, traders say. "It's going to be a volatile week, with a series of big events coming," including FOMC meeting, as well as monetary policy meetings in some other countries, says local bank trader. Adds, "from how the pair performs this week, the market may be able to gauge what trading will be like for the rest of this year." Also says, data released earlier shows October CPI up a faster-than-expected 4.1% on-year, which may boost KRW on increased rate hike expectations. Tips pair in 1,115-1,130 range for day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 flat in subdued trade. Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Adrian Vance says Australian market has opened a touch higher, and "so hopefully some of the quality stocks will be able to hold onto their gains (from Friday)...I wouldn't expect there will be a lot of difference between where we opened and where we close." Adds AMP Office Trust (ANO.NZ) +1.3% at NZ0.79 as it resumes trading from a halt last week as company internalised property management contracts. Says \uc2 Pyne Gould Corp\uc2  (PGC.NZ) managing to hold Friday's NZ$0.01 gain after announcement of NZ$0.015 special dividend; "Shareholders will be pleased they are in a position to resume those dividends." PGC last flat at NZ$0.44. \uc2 Contact Energy\uc2  (CEN.NZ) +0.9% at NZ$5.90 after announcing received provisional support from Environmental Protection Agency's Board of Inquiry for new electricity plant. NZX-50 last 3304.80. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2336 GMT [Dow Jones] Prospects of an increase in BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) loan hedging activity in coming days on back of bid for Potash means it's time to go short 5-year CDS ahead of Canadian government's decision on the takeover, say \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Strategists. "We think the CDS can reach 120 bps on loan hedging. We would stop out at 80 bps." Last at 83-88 bps. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely to open higher on technical rebound after ending down 1.3% at 1882.95 Friday. "If China's purchasing manager's index (PMI) for this month comes out today as strong as last month's at above 50, the benchmark index will likely test the 1900 level again today," says Bae Sung-young at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 . Notes, investors in global markets now eying results of Fed meeting this week; as most large companies done reporting 3Q results, outside factors now in focus for domestic market, he says.(kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea's annual inflation spikes to 20-month high in October, defying economists expectations for slowdown from 17-month high in preceding month as fresh food index +49.4% on-year; marks 2nd month annual inflation well above mid-point of BOK's 2%-4% inflation target band, may revive expectations for further rate hike from 2.25% currently before year is out. Government data show CPI +4.1% on-year vs +3.4% forecast by economists, accelerating from +3.6% September, while +0.2% on-month, vs 0.5% contraction tipped by economists, following unusually strong +1.1% September. (KKA)\par \par \uc2 2326 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may test all-time low of 79.75 in global day as quantitative easing measures by Fed, expected to be announced Wednesday, will continue to weigh on pair, says senior dealer at major Japan trust bank. Adds, players remain cautious with speculators unwilling to pile up JPY long positions aggressively over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities; pair last at 80.35 after falling to 80.21 earlier in thin trade. EUR/USD may rise to 1.4050 in global day on expectations of Fed's quantitative easing measures; dealer also says players likely comfortable taking EUR/USD long positions after trimming some gains last week. Pair last at 1.3977, support tipped at 1.3850. EUR/JPY may trade in 111.50-113.00 range, last at 112.31. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2313 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 4.9 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket selling may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 14.4 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 9.5 million shares. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei may weaken as exporters likely impacted by lower USD/JPY (now 80.32); investors may stay on sidelines before U.S. FOMC meeting slated for this week; index may stay between 9100-9250 after ending down 1.8% at 9202.45 Friday, say market analysts. "Good corporate earnings may be offset by the strong yen," says Yukata Miura, senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities. Market analysts say such companies as \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) may outperform as it hiked its full-year earnings despite lower USD/JPY assumption for 2H. Also say companies like Daiwa Securities (8601.TO) may also outperform on share buyback plan. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Friday's Chicago trading at 9170 vs Osaka close of 9190. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely quiet after flat night on Wall Street and major events, including RBA meeting, U.S. mid-term elections and FOMC meeting looming. U.S. economic data including ISM manufacturing and non-farm payrolls also due this week. Index may bounce off the 20-day moving average near 4662.0 today, since Wall Street recovered from profit taking as U.S. dollar fell on weak U.S. GDP data. China manufacturing data, due at 0100 GMT, could also have some impact. However, with the crucial FOMC meeting looming, investors are likely to stay sidelined and major Fibonacci resistance at 4700 will probably continue to hold. Index last 4661.6. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesia's trade surplus likely narrows in September to $998.5 million vs $1.49 billion in August, according to median forecasts of 10 regional economists polled by Dow Jones. Indonesia has seen volatile trade balance this year as imports growth occasionally outpaced exports gains due to increasing investment activities with companies importing more machinery. Meanwhile, demand in main Indonesia's exports destinations such as U.S. and Japan remain weak, but large capital inflows likely to help mitigate impact of shrinking trade surplus on balance of payments. Trade data due around 0400 GMT Monday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesian inflation likely to edge tad higher in October, but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to stand pat on rates to avoid further inflows of short-term funds. Median forecast of 10 regional economists polled by Dow Jones is CPI +5.81% on-year in October vs +5.80% in September on strong consumer demand, lower supply of agricultural goods due to prolonged wet season. Median forecast of 7 economists who came up with on-month comparisons is for index to gain 0.20% vs 0.44% month earlier. Data due around 0400 GMT Monday. "The market will likely be unmoved by CPI data even if the on-year inflation is slightly higher than forecast as Bank Indonesia most likely won't hike interest rates," says \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com) [ 10-31-10 1949ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101031e6av0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
