ooming. U.S. economic data including ISM manufacturing and non-farm payrolls also due this week. Index may bounce off the 20-day moving average near 4662.0 today, since Wall Street recovered from profit taking as U.S. dollar fell on weak U.S. GDP data. China manufacturing data, due at 0100 GMT, could also have some impact. However, with the crucial FOMC meeting looming, investors are likely to stay sidelined and major Fibonacci resistance at 4700 will probably continue to hold. Index last 4661.6. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesia's trade surplus likely narrows in September to $998.5 million vs $1.49 billion in August, according to median forecasts of 10 regional economists polled by Dow Jones. Indonesia has seen volatile trade balance this year as imports growth occasionally outpaced exports gains due to increasing investment activities with companies importing more machinery. Meanwhile, demand in main Indonesia's exports destinations such as U.S. and Japan remain weak, but large capital inflows likely to help mitigate impact of shrinking trade surplus on balance of payments. Trade data due around 0400 GMT Monday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesian inflation likely to edge tad higher in October, but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to stand pat on rates to avoid further inflows of short-term funds. Median forecast of 10 regional economists polled by Dow Jones is CPI +5.81% on-year in October vs +5.80% in September on strong consumer demand, lower supply of agricultural goods due to prolonged wet season. Median forecast of 7 economists who came up with on-month comparisons is for index to gain 0.20% vs 0.44% month earlier. Data due around 0400 GMT Monday. "The market will likely be unmoved by CPI data even if the on-year inflation is slightly higher than forecast as Bank Indonesia most likely won't hike interest rates," says \b \highlight22\u