\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: S Korea Oct Inflation Fastest In 20 Months\b0\par\par\uc2 1,524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2010\par 00:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea's annual inflation spikes to 20-month high in October, defying economists expectations for slowdown from 17-month high in preceding month as fresh food index +49.4% on-year; marks 2nd month annual inflation well above mid-point of BOK's 2%-4% inflation target band, may revive expectations for further rate hike from 2.25% currently before year is out. Government data show CPI +4.1% on-year vs +3.4% forecast by economists, accelerating from +3.6% September, while +0.2% on-month, vs 0.5% contraction tipped by economists, following unusually strong +1.1% September. (KKA)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2326 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may test all-time low of 79.75 in global day as quantitative easing measures by Fed, expected to be announced Wednesday, will continue to weigh on pair, says senior dealer at major Japan trust bank. Adds, players remain cautious with speculators unwilling to pile up JPY long positions aggressively over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities; pair last at 80.35 after falling to 80.21 earlier in thin trade. EUR/USD may rise to 1.4050 in global day on expectations of Fed's quantitative easing measures; dealer also says players likely comfortable taking EUR/USD long positions after trimming some gains last week. Pair last at 1.3977, support tipped at 1.3850. EUR/JPY may trade in 111.50-113.00 range, last at 112.31. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2313 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 4.9 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket selling may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 14.4 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 9.5 million shares. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei may weaken as exporters likely impacted by lower USD/JPY (now 80.32); investors may stay on sidelines before U.S. FOMC meeting slated for this week; index may stay between 9100-9250 after ending down 1.8% at 9202.45 Friday, say market analysts. "Good corporate earnings may be offset by the strong yen," says Yukata Miura, senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities. Market analysts say such companies as \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) may outperform as it hiked its full-year earnings despite lower USD/JPY assumption for 2H. Also say companies like Daiwa Securities (8601.TO) may also outperform on share buyback plan. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Friday's Chicago trading at 9170 vs Osaka close of 9190. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely quiet after flat night on Wall Street and major events, including RBA meeting, U.S. mid-term elections and FOMC meeting looming. U.S. economic data including ISM manufacturing and non-farm payrolls also due this week. Index may bounce off the 20-day moving average near 4662.0 today, since Wall Street recovered from profit taking as U.S. dollar fell on weak U.S. GDP data. China manufacturing data, due at 0100 GMT, could also have some impact. However, with the crucial FOMC meeting looming, investors are likely to stay sidelined and major Fibonacci resistance at 4700 will probably continue to hold. Index last 4661.6. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesia's trade surplus likely narrows in September to $998.5 million vs $1.49 billion in August, according to median forecasts of 10 regional economists polled by Dow Jones. Indonesia has seen volatile trade balance this year as imports growth occasionally outpaced exports gains due to increasing investment activities with companies importing more machinery. Meanwhile, demand in main Indonesia's exports destinations such as U.S. and Japan remain weak, but large capital inflows likely to help mitigate impact of shrinking trade surplus on balance of payments. Trade data due around 0400 GMT Monday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesian inflation likely to edge tad higher in October, but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to stand pat on rates to avoid further inflows of short-term funds. Median forecast of 10 regional economists polled by Dow Jones is CPI +5.81% on-year in October vs +5.80% in September on strong consumer demand, lower supply of agricultural goods due to prolonged wet season. Median forecast of 7 economists who came up with on-month comparisons is for index to gain 0.20% vs 0.44% month earlier. Data due around 0400 GMT Monday. "The market will likely be unmoved by CPI data even if the on-year inflation is slightly higher than forecast as Bank Indonesia most likely won't hike interest rates," says \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Thai October inflation likely tad up on higher food prices due to floods in many parts of country, though THB's rise partly offset impact. Dow Jones poll of 8 economists put median CPI forecast at +3.1% on-year vs +3.0% in September, +0.3% on month vs 0.07% decline; October core CPI likely +1.1% on-year, +0.1% on-month. Given loud noises from exporters hurt by baht strength, "we believe the BOT will have no choice but to delay a rate hike of another 0.25 percentage point at the next MPC meeting. On the other hand, the BOT cannot maintain the current low interest rate level for much longer due to accelerating inflationary pressures on the resilient domestic economy and a possible asset bubble caused by speculation in the property sector and in the stock market," says Sunthorn Thongthip, \uc2 Kim Eng Securities\uc2  economist. CPI data due Monday.(phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2252 GMT Dow Jones] 10-year cash JGBs may weaken slightly, as market looks for higher coupon at tomorrow's auction in the zone, says \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano; Japan MOF slated to sell Y2.2 trillion 10-year notes tomorrow. But expects lead December JGB futures to trade in similar range to Friday's due to caution ahead of closely-watched FOMC outcome on Wednesday. Lead JGB futures tipped in 143.25-143.54 band vs 143.20 last close on Friday, 10-year cash JGB yield in 0.905%-0.935% vs 0.920%.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2248 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely in tight ranges, trade on local cues with investors cautious ahead of FOMC decision this week; earnings season still underway in many countries, likely to be focus; Japanese exporters could be weighed as USD/JPY hits new 15-year low Friday. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets likely to take cue from stocks, otherwise likely to trade in range ahead of FOMC. USD/JPY at 80.31 vs 80.40 in late NY trade Friday, EUR/JPY at 112.25 vs 111.99, EUR/USD at 1.3977 vs 1.3947. Philippines markets shut. On data slate: Korea CPI at 2300 GMT, Australia TD Securities inflation gauge at 0000 GMT, Korea manufacturing PMI at 0000 GMT, Australia house price index at 0030 GMT, China CFLP manufacturing PMI at 0100 GMT, Korea trade at 0100 GMT, Taiwan manufacturing PMI at 0200 GMT, China \uc2 HSBC\uc2  manufacturing PMI at 0230 GMT, Indonesia CPI, trade at 0400 GMT, India manufacturing PMI at 0500 GMT, Australia commodity price index at 0530 GMT, Thailand CPI at 0700 GMT, Hong Kong retail sales at 0830 GMT, RBI mid-term review at 1130 GMT. Later there's U.K. CIPS manufacturing PMI, U.S. ISM manufacturing PMI, construction spending. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2238 GMT [Dow Jones] Odds heavily favor renewed weakening in USD of sufficient magnitude to support front-month crude oil futures around $80/bbl area, so maintain bullish stance, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. Markets currently cautious ahead of meeting of U.S. Federal Reserve policy-making committee; Fed expected to announce plans to stimulate economy at its conclusion. "We still expect the Fed to provide some additional clarity that will likely reveal more quantitative easing than currently priced into the financial markets," Ritterbusch says. Nymex crude for December delivery now up 17 cents at $81.60/bbl on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2231 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  advises against aggressively buying into AUD/USD weakness but recommends going long pair before RBA meeting as market is underpricing the chances of a hike, which would drive AUD higher. For AUD/JPY, says key will be if pair close below 79.30. "With Japanese investors one of the largest players in the AUD carry trade, a close today below this level would signal further weakness in the cross." AUD/JPY last 79.14. (enda.curran@dowjones.com) [ 10-31-10 1932ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101031e6av0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
