ilar range to Friday's due to caution ahead of closely-watched FOMC outcome on Wednesday. Lead JGB futures tipped in 143.25-143.54 band vs 143.20 last close on Friday, 10-year cash JGB yield in 0.905%-0.935% vs 0.920%.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2248 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely in tight ranges, trade on local cues with investors cautious ahead of FOMC decision this week; earnings season still underway in many countries, likely to be focus; Japanese exporters could be weighed as USD/JPY hits new 15-year low Friday. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets likely to take cue from stocks, otherwise likely to trade in range ahead of FOMC. USD/JPY at 80.31 vs 80.40 in late NY trade Friday, EUR/JPY at 112.25 vs 111.99, EUR/USD at 1.3977 vs 1.3947. Philippines markets shut. On data slate: Korea CPI at 2300 GMT, Australia TD Securities inflation gauge at 0000 GMT, Korea manufacturing PMI at 0000 GMT, Australia house price index at 0030 GMT, China CFLP manufacturing PMI at 0100 GMT, Korea trade at 0100 GMT, Taiwan manufacturing PMI at 0200 GMT, China \uc2 HSBC\uc2  manufacturing PMI at 0230 GMT, Indonesia CPI, trade at 0400 GMT, India manufacturing PMI at 0500 GMT, Australia commodity price index at 0530 GMT, Thailand CPI at 0700 GMT, Hong Kong retail sales at 0830 GMT, RBI mid-term review at 1130 GMT. Later there's U.K. CIPS manufacturing PMI, U.S. ISM manufacturing PMI, construction spending. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2238 GMT [Dow Jones] Odds heavily favor renewed weakening in USD of sufficient magnitude to support front-month crude oil futures around $80/bbl area, so maintain bullish stance, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. Markets currently cautious ahead of meeting of U.S. Federal Reserve policy-making committee; Fed expected to announce plans to stimulate economy at its conclusion. "We still expect the Fed to provide some additional clarity that will likely reveal more quantitative easing than currently priced into the financial markets," Ritterbusch says. Nymex crude for December delivery now up 17 cents at $81.60/bbl on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2231 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  advises against aggressively buying into AUD/USD weakness but recommends going long pair before RBA meeting as market is underpricing the chances of a hike, which would drive AUD higher. For AUD/JPY, says key will be if pair close below 79.30. "With Japanese investors one of the largest players in the AUD carry trade, a close today below this level would signal further weakness in the cross." AUD/JPY last 79.14. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2228 GMT [Dow Jones] Participants in base metals and other markets continue to anticipate U.S. Fed meeting this week on extent, timing of it's next phase of quantitative easing, says \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 . Indeed, says Triland Metals, "all eyes on Wednesday's Fed announcement" during what is seen as a test week for the whole complex. Triland said copper's action Friday reflected view that Federal Reserve monetary stimulus won't be as large as once thought, "thus in that perverse way, good news on the economy meant a fall for copper," Triland said in note Saturday; it expects $8,150 to be tested again, and to see more buying on dips, in coming sessions. On Friday, LME 3-month copper lost $140 on day at $8,200/metric ton, aluminum eased $2.50 at $2,344, tin slumped $650 at $25,600, nickel slipped $110 to $22,990, zinc sank $71 at $2,422, while lead lost $56 at $2,449. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 10-31-10 1926ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101031e6av0001o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
