\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,264 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2010\par 13:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF: DIDN'T \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET IN OCTOBER \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  refrained from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in October, data from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  showed Friday, with the authorities sitting on the sidelines despite the yen's continued rise to near-record levels. (published 1008 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU CALLS ON G-20 TO ACT TO AVOID \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BRUSSELS --European Union leaders urged their Group of 20 counterparts Friday to avoid a damaging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war so as to prevent any return of damaging 1930s style trade protectionism. (published 103 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN HIGH YIELD BOND SUPPLY HITS RECORD IN OCTOBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro-denominated high yield bond market saw record monthly supply in October, with over EUR7 billion of bonds sold, as investors continue to seek out higher yields than are on offer in the high grade market, according to data from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (published 100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO-ZONE INFLATION RATE HIGHEST SINCE NOV 2008 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer prices in the 16 countries that use the euro rose at the fastest rate in two years during October. (published 1000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO-ZONE JOBLESS RATE ROSE TO 10.1% IN SEPTEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The rate of unemployment in the 16 countries that use the euro rose in September, despite a fall in the rate of joblessness in Germany as many firms around the region continue to cut costs amid an uncertain economic recovery, data showed Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK CONSUMER LENDING SLOWS, MORTGAGE APPROVALS DOWN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. net consumer lending rose at a slower rate than expected in September as mortgage lending remained subdued and the more forward-looking mortgage approval data fell for the fifth consecutive month. (published 081 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK BROAD MONEY SUPPLY PICKS UP IN 3Q,STILL BELOW TREND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of England's preferred measure of broad money supply picked up in September, but remained well below its long-term trend, highlighting the continuing hurdles to recovery in the U.K. economy. (published 1021 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO-ZONE ECONOMY ACCELERATED IN OCT: EUROCOIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone's economic recovery accelerated for the first time in seven months in October, according to a measure of activity compiled by the Centre for Economic Policy Research and the Bank of Italy. (published 0730 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN RETAIL SALES FALL SURPRISINGLY IN SEPTEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Retail sales in Germany fell short of expectations in September, falling 2.3% in inflation- and seasonally-adjusted terms from August, the Federal Statistics Office Destatis said Friday. (published 0705 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ITALY SEP PRELIM JOBLESS RATE 8.3% VS 8.2% YR-AGO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy's unemployment rate in September rose after the previous month's one-year low, preliminary data showed Friday. (published 1013 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PORTUGAL GOVERNMENT PREPARING NEW BUDGET COMPROMISE - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Portuguese government is preparing a new compromise budget proposal for 2011 after the biggest opposition party rejected the previous proposal and broke off negotiations, Publico newspaper reports Friday, without saying where it got the information. (published 087 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN OFFICIAL: WON'T RAISE YUAN CONCERNS IF CHINA, JAPAN PMS MEET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan won't raise \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issues with his Chinese counterpart Wen Jiabao if they were to meet on the sidelines of a regional summit, a Japanese official told Dow Jones Newswires Friday. (published 080 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC OFFICIAL: PBOC DOESN'T NOW FACTOR ASSET PRICES INTO MONETARY POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  doesn't currently factor asset prices into monetary policy and China prefers "tailored" policies to address property and stock prices, PBOC research bureau head Zhang Jianhua said at a forum Friday. (published 0727 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC ADVISER: CHINA WAS 'DISAPPOINTED' BY US CREDIT RATING AGENCIES BEHAVIOR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has been "disappointed" by the behavior of U.S. credit rating agencies and with the quality of U.S. financial securities, Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adviser Li Daokui said Friday. (published 0758 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER: WATCHING \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WITH GREAT INTEREST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is no change in our stance that the government will take decisive action when necessary," Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said at a regular press conference. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPANESE PRODUCTION FALLS AS DEFLATION TIGHTENS ITS GRIP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's economy suffered another setback in September with prices continuing to tumble as the yen soared and output at factories dropping as government purchasing incentives wind down, adding to Tokyo's already difficult balancing act as it tries to underpin growth while cutting the country's massive debt. (published 0340 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RUSSIAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LEAVES KEY RATES UNCHANGED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left interest rates unchanged at its monthly meeting Friday, saying inflation has slowed and the economy remains in need of further monetary stimulus. (published 0707 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPAIN UNEMPLOYMENT FALLS FOR 1ST TIME IN 3 YEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spain's high unemployment rate edged lower in the third quarter, its first decline in over three years, thanks to summer job creation in the country's tourism industry, data from the National Statistics Institute showed Friday. (published 1000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA ECON PLANNER: US DOLLAR DEPRECIATION TO BOOST CHINA MANUFACTURING COSTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A depreciating U.S. dollar will boost global commodity prices, putting upward pressure on production costs in the Chinese manufacturing sector in the fourth quarter, China's top economic planning agency said in a statement Friday. (published 0217 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC OFFICIAL: US QUANTITATIVE EASING WILL PRESSURE EMERGING MARKETS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging markets would face fund inflow pressure and volatility from further quantitative easing by the Federal Reserve, Zhang Jianhua, head of the research bureau at the People's Bank of China, said Friday. (published 0732 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC ADVISER: 3%-5% YUAN APPRECIATION PER YEAR CONTROLLABLE PACE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A yuan appreciation rate of 3%-5% per year is a controllable pace for China, historically speaking, Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adviser Li Daokui said Friday. (published 0339 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MOODY'S: EMERGING MARKET NATIONS' EFFORTS TO FIGHT SOARING \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WON'T HIT RATINGS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Efforts by emerging-market nations to cope with the surge in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  driven by huge capital inflows won't dent their credit ratings in the near term but could have longer-term implications that need watching, Moody's Investors Service said Friday. (published 0503 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par TAKING STOCK: DOLLAR, STOCKS BOTH LET FED DRIVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 'Dollar down stocks up, dollar up stocks down'; a familiar little jig to anyone who's had at least half an eye on the markets this year. Indeed, we might call it the post-crunch two-step. (published 101 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HEARD ON THE STREET: WATCH OUT FOR EMERGING-MARKET INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the U.S. Federal Reserve frets about deflation, in some parts of the world inflation may now be the bigger danger. Although the IMF expects headline inflation in emerging markets to fall to 5% in 2011, from around 5.75% now, pressures are building in some developing countries. India's inflation rate hit double digits earlier this year. In Brazil, economists have increased their 2010 inflation forecasts for six straight weeks, according to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Investors may want to consider some protection. (published 080 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BONDS TO EXTEND RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Keeping Wednesday's low at 128.52 intact is likely to attract strength to Thursday's 129.14 high. A break through 129.14 is expected, but congestion in the 129.19/129.40 area will prove more difficult to crack, which protects the 1.618 extension target at 129.60. Only a break below 128.60 would put bears back in control, exposing 128.52 and threatening weakness to the 128.30 area. (published 0705 GMT) [ 29-10-10 1104GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101029e6at001p8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
