\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Up Broadly As Nervous Investors Await GDP Data\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew J. Johnson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2010\par 14:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar rose broadly Friday as German and Japanese data signaled slowing economic growth and led to flight from riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 The disappointing German retail sales and soft Japanese industrial production heightened anxiety about the upcoming U.S. GDP release.\par \par \uc2 But the dollar weakened against the safe-harbor yen, grinding down near its 15-year low of Y80.41, reached last Monday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also continued to benefit from recent skittishness and a shedding of aggressive positions against it. A downsizing of estimates of the upcoming Federal Reserve stimulus plan--which will weaken the greenback by flooding the market with dollars--has ended weeks of substantially driving down the dollar in anticipation that the plan would have "shock and awe" dismensions.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's Federal Open Market Committee meets next Tuesday and Wednesday, when members will likely make a formal decision on the stimulus plan, known as quantitative easing, or QE.\par \par \uc2 Slight fears persist that U.S. economic data could prove resilient enough to challenge conventional wisdom that a large Fed plan is imminent, said analysts.\par \par \uc2 That being said, closely watched Friday will be U.S. third-quarter gross domestic product data and an employment cost index reported at 8:30 a.m. EDT.\par \par \uc2 "The market will likely have a stronger reaction to softer-than-expected GDP than it would to firmer-than-expected GDP," said David Ader, fixed-income strategist with CRT Capital in Stamford, Conn. "The logic is that QE is coming and that news that conforms to the rationale behind it will prove more convincing to those who had on QE trades -- but have since exited."\par \par \uc2 Early Friday morning, the euro was at $1.3856 from $1.3930 from late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y80.78 from Y81.02, while the euro was at Y111.91 from Y112.86. The U.K. pound was at $1.5925 from $1.5936. The dollar was at CHF0.9889 from CHF0.9832.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 77.530 from 77.294.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, retail sales in Germany fell short of expectations in September, falling 2.3% in inflation- and seasonally-adjusted terms from August, the Federal Statistics Office Destatis said Friday.\par \par \uc2 That is well below expectations of a 0.4% rise among economists polled ahead of time by Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Japanese industrial output fell 1.9% in September from a month earlier, marking the fourth straight month of decline in the face of abating exports.\par \par \uc2 Japan's minister of state for economic and fiscal policy Banri Kaieda Friday called last month's fall in industrial production "very serious."\par \par \uc2 With the dollar continuing its broad decline against the yen, Japan has nevertheless continued to remain on the sidelines with respect to more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in October, data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed Friday.\par \par \uc2 The MOF data covers Sept. 29 to Oct. 27.\par \par \uc2 The inaction was in sharp contrast to the previous month when Japan surprised the market by selling Y2.125 trillion in the market.\par \par \uc2 -By Andrew J. Johnson, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-3092; andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Kosaku Narioka in Tokyo and Geoffrey T. Smith in Frankfurt contributed to this article.) [ 10-29-10 0818ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101029e6at0007p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
