\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan Stocks May Benefit From BOJ Policy\b0\par\par\uc2 1,274 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2010\par 03:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Nomura Securities sees "a high likelihood" that BOJ policy board meeting will further widen the gap between 10-yr yields in U.S. and Japan. "USD/JPY could head toward 83-84 after exporters' USD selling, typical at month-end, is absorbed." Nevertheless, house does not anticipate sustained rise in U.S. Treasury yields, says possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has also receded. Recommends investors take advantage of USD/JPY rally to sell on any rebound. While rise in USD/JPY--even incremental or short-term--tends to benefit Japan stocks, less than sustained rally not likely to result in broad-market equity price appreciation. Investor concerns over \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels becoming more tempered, however, on strength in Japanese corporate earnings, with 1H FY reporting season just underway. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD lower at 7.7583 vs 7.7592 yesterday as a Singapore bank sells, says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in 7.7570-7.7620 band near term. "I expect the pair to maintain its downward bias as the upcoming IPOs are likely to attract more inflows," trader says. Notes IPOs in pipeline include Chinese lead-acid batteries manufacturer Leoch International Technology (0842.HK), which plans to raise up to US$229.6 million ahead of its HK listing, scheduled for November 16, according to term sheet viewed by Dow Jones. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares may extend recent gains as positive earnings news continue to lift sentiment, more companies expected to report solid results later this week; benchmark PSEi may test 4300 psychological resistance after closing 0.1% higher Wednesday at 4285.07. Support tipped at 4250. Technical indicators suggest market still needs to pullback, but "initial earnings report may provide some support," says Astro del Castillo, managing director at First Grade Holdings. Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH) expected to lead gains after its units reported strong 9-month earnings, while Philex may rise after posting strong 3Q results. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 12-month fair value target 5387, giving total shareholder return 19%, comprising 14.4% capital return, 4.6% dividend yield, says Macquarie. "This highlights the market's strong EPS growth profile, which is supporting positive returns as this growth rolls forward," says broker. "Resources continue to be the primary driver of the forecast returns given their strong earnings profile, attractive valuations and supportive macro conditions." Macquarie estimates S&P/ASX 200 PE ratios of 12.6x for 1 year forward, 13.1x for FY11 and 12.0x for FY12. Among sectors, estimates banks 1 year forward PE of 11.2x and resources 1 year forward PE of 13x. S&P/ASX 200 up 1.1% at 4697.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI likely to stabilize after yesterday's fall, as USD weakens, which likely to help commodity names recoup some of their sharp falls yesterday, says Ben Kwong at KGI. Tips index in 23,000-23,300 range. "I think stocks will move on their specific situations, given the slew of corporate earnings for the third quarter. Cautious trading is expected ahead of the current month futures expiry today." Among blue chips to watch for post-results are Bank of China (3988.HK), \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK), Ping An (2318.HK). HSI ends down 1.9% at 23,164.58 yesterday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR tad higher at 8,940 vs 8,935 late yesterday, tracking broad USD strength vs majors, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers say; "Month-end demand from importers also keeps the dollar-rupiah well bid," dealer says. Adds, Bank Indonesia may sell greenback around 8,950 to slow pair's rise. 8,930-8,950 range tipped today. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0118 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares +0.2% at 8305.74, likely to stay in range following lackluster performance on Wall Street overnight, while tech companies' 3Q earnings results mixed so far, keeping investors edgy about building long positions. "Tech shares should be mixed but could gain if the Taiwan dollar does not appreciate," says KGI Securities analyst Randy Chang. Tips benchmark index in 8250-8350 range. TSMC (2330.TW) +0.8% at NT$62.50, Asustek (2357.TW) off 0.4% at NT$239.00 ahead of 3Q earnings later today. UMC (2303.TW) +4.0% at NT$14.35 after reporting strong 3Q results yesterday. Hu An Cable (911612.TO) higher on debut, +2.2% at NT$13.90, thanks to recent strong demand for China plays; IPO price at NT$13.60. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB edges higher to 29.99 vs 29.985 late yesterday, off overnight high at 30.00. Month-end USD demand for trade settlement lends support. "The baht is likely to move in a narrow range as most players are reluctant to take any major positions ahead of the FOMC meeting," says Bangkok-based dealer. Pair upside likely capped in near term as exporters actively place USD sale orders around 30.00-30.05. Resistance tipped at 30.05, support in 29.92-29.93 band. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to rise, tracking broad USD strength overnight, though upside likely limited, says Shanghai-based trader at European bank. USD/CNY fixing tipped around 6.6950 vs 6.6912 yesterday. On OTC trade, USC/CNY tipped in 6.6750-6.6900 range vs 6.6806 last close, highest settlement level since Sept. 30. "Since the rate hike last week, the dollar-yuan has been rising," trader says. "But we think it will be difficult for the pair to rise above CNY6.7000." Expectations of long-term CNY strength still prevailing in market, she adds. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may trade lower on falls in commodity prices; Shanghai Composite Index tipped to test 2950 support after ending down 1.5% at 2997.05. Wei Daoke at Shenyin Wanguo Securities notes technical indicators suggest stocks overbought but adds didn't see any panic selling in yesterday's falls. Oil, metal names may lead falls. \uc2 Ping An Insurance\uc2  (601318.SH) may also fall, after saying 3Q net profit down 26%. Shenzhen index ends down 1.0% at 1289.19. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped lower, likely tracking USD slide vs EUR in early Asian trade; pair may slide to 43.15 support after settling at 43.26 late Wednesday, with next stop at 43.00 and resistance at 43.50; "We'll likely look at the euro for cues. We expect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar slides below PHP43.15," says a local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to fall to trade in 30.600-30.850 band vs 30.900 yesterday, weighed by exporters' month-end settlement selling, but losses likely limited as traders suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step in slow pair's drop, says local bank trader. Notes \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of buying USD mildly in late session every day this week. Still, "the selling pressure remains strong," he says. Expects pair to try 30.500 in near term. (mason.wu@dowjones.com) 