\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:AUD Falls Sharply On Below-View Aussie CPI -Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,433 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2010\par 02:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD falls sharply as Australian CPI data come in weaker than expected, investors with bullish expectations sell on disappointment, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Adds, impact magnified as trade volume still thin in Asia. Australia's 3Q CPI +0.7% on quarter vs +0.8% expected. AUD/JPY falls to 79.78 from 80.38 before data, may fall to 79.50. AUD/USD falls to 0.9787 from 0.9860 before data, may fall to 0.9750. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0041 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD higher on concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow yen rise, also a smaller than expected Fed stimulus, trader with international bank says; tips USD to trade "heavy" today but medium-term weakness for greenback to persist. "There was a feeling in the market that perhaps the dollar was oversold. This is just a correction, prompted by talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and speculation about the likely steps by the Fed in coming days." Tips 1.3030 resistance, 1.2930 support. Pair last 1.2979 vs 1.2928 late Asia yesterday. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian CPI weaker than expected at first glance, but housing and import components indicate an interest rate hike from RBA could still be on cards next week, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Paul Bloxham. "It doesn't rule it out." Says AUD wasn't a factor as hadn't strengthened enough through the quarter. Australian 3Q CPI +0.7% on-quarter; +2.8% on-year vs expectations for +0.8%, +2.9% respectively. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures down on weak U.S. Treasurys overnight, some hawkish contents from September BOK monetary policy meeting minutes released late yesterday; 5-year yield quoted +4 bps at 3.85%, December futures down 14 ticks at 112.51. Minutes show 2 out of 6 policy board members called for 25-bp rate hike, citing growing inflation pressures, with one calling for policy rate to be raised to 2.75% by year-end vs current 2.25%. "Judging from these minutes, I think the BOK could opt for another key policy rate hike as fast as November," says \uc2 SK Securities\uc2  analyst Yum Sang hoon. Following July's 25-bp hike, BOK has kept rates on hold at 2.25%. Bonds seemingly brush off 3Q GDP data which largely in line with market expectations. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] After recent rally to year-high, Kospi in mild correction Wednesday, with foreigners selling stocks, analysts say; last down 0.3% at 1913.95, with investors awaiting major events next week, including possible Fed quantitative easing, U.S. election. Major corporate earnings, such as Hynix (000660.SE), \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE), \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE), also eyed, capping upside. "A further rise may be possible, but it's not betting time, with heightened investor cautiousness," says \uc2 Woori Investment & Securities\uc2 ' Lee Kyoung-min; expects "short-term shock" for Kospi after Fed outcome, may slide as low as 1800 in November. Market watchers expect Fed QE of around $500 billion-$2 trillion. Kospi tipped in 1915-1930 range today. Techs, autos mostly down, with \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 0.7% at KRW758,000, \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  down 0.5% at KRW101,500, Hynix down 1.7% at KRW23,300. \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  down 0.6% at KRW170,500, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) down 0.4% at KRW42,900. Still, analysts tip chemical, refinery, auto, shipbuilders on strong 3Q earnings hopes. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] China's production and trade data for September indicates that "Chinese steel mills and utilities are filling their boots with imports" of bulk commodities in time for winter, says Macquarie. "At 646 million tons annualised, (iron ore) imports were at the highest level seen since April"; combined with weak steel production, figure "implies a heavy restock", suggesting October imports could hit new all-time record. Figures highlighted Australia's importance as exporter too, with country's iron ore accounting for 49.6% of China's imports; same picture with coking coal, suggesting that "steel mills are readying for a production push", while thermal coal imports rose to highest levels since January despite 5% fall in electricity generation. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0031 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.6% at 9434.07, as weaker yen helps exporters rise; \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 1.5% at Y2,908, Advantest (6857.TO) up 1.3% at Y1,728; index may trade between 9350-9500 today, say market analysts. "Even though the yen's weakness may be temporary, the market welcomes it for now," says Yoshinori Nagano, senior strategist at \uc2 Daiwa Asset Management\uc2 . Says sentiment good as Japanese companies likely to report strong 1H earnings later this week; Nikkei may see further upside in coming days if USD/JPY holds above 81 (now 81.44), with Nikkei resistance pegged around 9700 this week. 22/33 Topix subindexes higher; Fuji Heavy (7270.TO) up 5.3% at Y555 on brisk 1H earnings hopes. Kao (4452.TO) up 3.5% at Y2,075 on share buy back announcement. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0028 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may rise for 3rd consecutive session on follow-through buying after key index closed above key technical resistance yesterday; underlying sentiment also positive amid upbeat outlook for domestic economy. KLCI ended +0.4% at 1496.94 yesterday. "Having settled above the 1495 resistance, the KLCI will ride on this positive momentum to go higher. We could see it reaching a new 2010 high soon," dealer says. Current year-to-date high is 1503.82, hit on Oct. 14; this also highest level since January 1998. Banks such as Maybank (1155.KU), property developers like UEM Land (5148.KU), deemed as proxies to domestic economic growth, may lead gains. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0022 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/JPY may rise above 81.00 if Australian 3Q CPI data due 0030 GMT comes in strong as will likely fuel speculation for additional rate hikes, says Kenichiro Ikezawa, \uc2 Daiwa SB Investments\uc2 . Says market has somewhat priced in November RBA rate hike, but if CPI reads +1.0%, may raise speculation for additional rate hikes. Australian 3Q CPI tipped +0.8% on-quarter vs 2Q's +0.6% rise, according to 17 economists surveyed by Dow Jones. AUD/JPY last 80.19. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0019 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead 10-year JGB futures open lower as expected on weak U.S. Treasurys, Nikkei gains; contract currently down 0.25 at 143.17. Once early selling runs its course, some players expected to see 10-year cash yield above 0.9% as good bargain-hunting level, helping market trim falls later in day, says Mitsubishi UFJ \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  analyst Katsutoshi Inadome. Benchmark 10-year cash bond yield now up 2.5 bps at 0.925%. (kazuhiro.shimamura@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0014 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher at 3.1070 vs 3.0950 late yesterday, following USD recovery against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dealer says, in view of renewed speculation about possible Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank Negara Governor Zeti's remarks that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if market turns disorderly, participants may opt to unwind short USD positions near-term, wait for stronger cues. Pegs support for pair at 3.0900, resistance at 3.1150. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0008 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW takes out 1,120 resistance mark at market open to trade up at 1,122.7 vs 1,116.8 late yesterday in Seoul, tracking overnight USD gains vs some majors, with traders tipping next initial resistance at 1,125. Local bank trader tips pair in 1,118-1,132 range for day, with upside likely capped on exporter selling. She notes, for past 2 days, some players shorted USD on news G-20 reached agreement over weekend on need to avoid competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation, "but the pair didn't even go close to testing below the year's low (at 1,102.6)," adding, "pair hasn't been able to break out of the recent 1,110-1,140 range...so taking a range-bound approach still seems appropriate." (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 10-26-10 2044ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101027e6ar0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
