\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: AUD/JPY May Rise To 81 If Aussie CPI Strong-Daiwa\b0\par\par\uc2 1,469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2010\par 02:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0022 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/JPY may rise above 81.00 if Australian 3Q CPI data due 0030 GMT comes in strong as will likely fuel speculation for additional rate hikes, says Kenichiro Ikezawa, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daiwa SB Investments\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Says market has somewhat priced in November RBA rate hike, but if CPI reads +1.0%, may raise speculation for additional rate hikes. Australian 3Q CPI tipped +0.8% on-quarter vs 2Q's +0.6% rise, according to 17 economists surveyed by Dow Jones. AUD/JPY last 80.19. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0019 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead 10-year JGB futures open lower as expected on weak U.S. Treasurys, Nikkei gains; contract currently down 0.25 at 143.17. Once early selling runs its course, some players expected to see 10-year cash yield above 0.9% as good bargain-hunting level, helping market trim falls later in day, says Mitsubishi UFJ \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  analyst Katsutoshi Inadome. Benchmark 10-year cash bond yield now up 2.5 bps at 0.925%. (kazuhiro.shimamura@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0014 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher at 3.1070 vs 3.0950 late yesterday, following USD recovery against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dealer says, in view of renewed speculation about possible Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank Negara Governor Zeti's remarks that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if market turns disorderly, participants may opt to unwind short USD positions near-term, wait for stronger cues. Pegs support for pair at 3.0900, resistance at 3.1150. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0008 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW takes out 1,120 resistance mark at market open to trade up at 1,122.7 vs 1,116.8 late yesterday in Seoul, tracking overnight USD gains vs some majors, with traders tipping next initial resistance at 1,125. Local bank trader tips pair in 1,118-1,132 range for day, with upside likely capped on exporter selling. She notes, for past 2 days, some players shorted USD on news G-20 reached agreement over weekend on need to avoid competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation, "but the pair didn't even go close to testing below the year's low (at 1,102.6)," adding, "pair hasn't been able to break out of the recent 1,110-1,140 range...so taking a range-bound approach still seems appropriate." (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0001 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely rangebound in thin volume as investors tread with caution before release of results of benchmark 3-year MGS auction tomorrow. Still, expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would pause on rate hikes for at least rest of year would lend some support in near term, dealers say; this after Bank Negara Governor Zeti reiterated yesterday interest rates at appropriate levels, no inflationary pressures on economy. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGSs ended mixed; May 2013 +2 bps at 3.20%, August 2015 unchanged at 3.32%, September 2017 unchanged at 3.62%, November 2019 +1 bp at 3.82%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2359 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.2% at 4695.5, hits 4697.4 despite soggy leads from Wall Street, as NAB (NAB.AU) leads banks with 3.2% rise on above-consensus FY10 results. But NAB results are of poor quality, says \uc2 MF Global\uc2  Institutional Trader Nick Burmester. "The reported profit and cash profit were above market, as was the dividend. But provisions are down and there are one-offs from CDOs and they've also taken some costs below the line. Revenue is going backwards." Says he prefers selling banks ahead of potential ANZ (ANZ.AU) entitlement issue when its results are announced tomorrow. Also expects broader Australian, U.S. markets to struggle to make further gains before the RBA and FOMC meetings next week. "We expect more profit taking at these levels," says Burmester. "QE2 is factored in to a degree." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2350 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely up on strong foreign inflows, despite recent continued rally. "The market is still focused on how the Fed will carry out its quantitative easing measures," \uc2 Kyobo Securities\uc2  says; but adds investors will stay "cautious" ahead of major events next week as market may face a correction if Fed's anticipated QE moves, which largely priced into local stocks already, come below expectations of around "$1 trillion-$2 trillion". Still, analysts expect foreigners to remain active in dollar-carry trade to buy emerging market stocks, including Korea, as bearish USD trend eyed unlikely to reverse any time soon. Kospi tipped in 1915-1930 range after ending +0.2% at new 2010 high 1919.41 yesterday. Chemical, refinery, auto, shipbuilding stocks in focus on strong 3Q earnings hopes. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2346 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +3.3% at HK$4.292 billion Tuesday, 5.2% of market total, slightly higher vs 4.8% Monday, suggests HSI may remain in range-trading after 0.1% fall Tuesday, 0.5% rise Monday. A50 (2823.HK) leads with HK$679.36 million, \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) with HK$243.15 million, China Life (2628.HK) with HK$228.84 million, BYD (1211.HK) with HK$200.72 million and Tracker Fund (2800.HK) with HK$174.60 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude oil futures lower as market awaits weekly U.S. oil inventory data Wednesday from DOE and after industry group API releases own data sh