.S. could be smaller-than-expected, traders say. Local bank trader pegs cap at 1,125. "Foreigners are chalking up massive amounts of local stocks, while dollar-selling by exporters are likely to come in bigger volumes as the end of the month approaches. And the view that the longer-term trend is for the dollar/won to fall, will likely lead offshore players to opt for selling the dollar on any rebound," says another trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold up marginally vs late New York; at 2315 GMT, up $2.50 at $1,342.80/oz, but little momentum despite recovery from previous week's sell-off. Jon Nadler, analyst at bullion dealer Kitco, pointed to seventh consecutive decline in ETF gold holdings Tuesday while silver ETF balances slipped for first time in three weeks: "CFTC reports indicate changes in speculative attitude underway." But bullion dealer GoldCore says physical demand still robust due to jitters ahead of U.S. Federal Reserve decision next week on possible quantitative easing, with asset purchases tipped as high as $4 trillion. ANZ also points to jewellery demand for possible support: "The falling gold price was encouraging for key Indian buyers, attracting some physical gold interest back to the market ahead of the key Hindu festival of Diwali next week." Comex December gold at only slight premium to spot, also $1,343.10/oz. (david.fickling@dowjones.com) [ 10-26-10 2019ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101027e6ar00009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
