\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Cabinet Approves Y4.851 Trillion Extra Budget For Econ Package\b0\par\par\uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2010\par 09:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details of stimulus measures, comment by finance minister, background.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Kazuhiro Shimamura \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The Japanese government Tuesday approved a Y4.851 trillion extra budget to finance an economic stimulus package aimed at re-igniting the country's stalling economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 The spending plan--the first in 11 years to be funded without the use of deficit-financing government bonds--is the latest step by the ruling Democratic Party of Japan to cope with the twin threats of a soaring yen and deflation, which threaten to detail the country's fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Including Y238.8 billion worth of public works contracts to be brought forward, the latest package by the government of Prime Minister Naoto Kan amounts to Y5.1 trillion.\par \par \uc2 To put this figure into context, it is smaller than former premier Yukio Hatoyama's Y7.2 trillion package in December 2009 and well below a massive extra budget totalling Y15.4 trillion compiled by the rival Liberal Democratic Party in April 2009 to pull the economy out of recession when they were in power.\par \par \uc2 Already given the green light by the Cabinet earlier this month, the stimulus measures include a Y319.9 billion outlay to create and protect jobs, Y1.124 trillion for childcare, medical and nursing services, and Y3.071 trillion to help small businesses, revitalize regional economies and improve infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 The government will allocate Y336.9 billion to support growing business areas and technologies, including Y87.2 billion to help secure more sources of rare earth and other natural resources.\par \par \uc2 The extra spending comes as the dollar tumbled to a fresh 15-year low of Y80.41 in Tokyo Monday, further reducing the competitiveness and profitability of Japan's exports, the major driver of the economic recovery in the aftermath of the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Japan's finance ministry has had little success in stemming the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surge despite engaging in Y2.125 trillion worth of yen selling last month in its first \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than six years.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't had much luck in its fight against entrenched deflation either. Earlier this month the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  loosened its already easy monetary policy by bringing back its near zero-interest rate policy and setting up a Y5 trillion fund for private- and public-sector asset purchasing.\par \par \uc2 The stimulus package incorporates some of the proposals floated by opposition parties such as allocating funds to local governments for public works as Kan tries to pass the budget bill through the parliament, where his party has a majority in only the lower of the two houses.\par \par \uc2 "We put (the package) together by incorporating opinions and proposals from not only the ruling party but opposition parties," Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said at a regular press conference after a Cabinet meeting.\par \par \uc2 "I believe the size and the measures (of the package) can bring us to an agreement," he told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Along with public works projects, the well-worn weapons of past governments to keep the economy afloat, the new spending plan is more aggressive in welfare and medical services.\par \par \uc2 The package also comes with government financial support for corporations investing overseas to take advantage of the strong yen.\par \par \uc2 Backed by government funding, including \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings, the state-run Japan Bank for International Cooperation will provide loans to private companies, when needed, for business activities in developed countries. Such activities may include mergers, acquisitions and investment projects such as power-plant and high-speed train construction.\par \par \uc2 The government's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings are available to back a credit line of Y1.5 trillion through the JBIC.\par \par \uc2 The Cabinet expects the stimulus steps to push up the real gross domestic product by 0.6 percentage point and create and protect 450,000-500,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 -By Kazuhiro Shimamura, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2790; kazuhiro.shimamura@dowjones.com [ 10-26-10 0309ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020101026e6aq0001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
