\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Falls After G-20 As Old Trends Return\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew J. Johnson Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 799 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2010\par 02:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell broadly Monday as the statement from the Group of 20 nations cleared the way for investors to focus on another round of anticipated quantitative easing from the Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 A nonbinding G-20 agreement to avoid "competitive devaluation" eased fears that governments would aggressively push their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower against the dollar. That allowed investors to return to global growth scenarios and punishing the greenback for expected Federal Reserve stimulus.\par \par \uc2 "Nothing very surprising came out the G-20, so what was reverted to was the overwhelming assumption in the market, and that's [quantitative easing]," the widely expected Fed stimulus program that would flood the market with dollars, said John McCarthy, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at ING Capital Markets in New York.\par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a new 15-year low against the yen at Y80.41 overnight. The Australian dollar led the march forward with a 1.4% rise overnight.\par \par \uc2 To see the Australian dollar's performance against the U.S. dollar, please see:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4818"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4818}}}\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar erased some of its losses against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York trading after positive U.S. housing data helped stem the negative tide.\par \par \uc2 In choosing to flee the low-yielding dollar, investors are attracted to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tied to the strongest global growth, mostly based in Asia, McCarthy said. "People know Asian market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to outperform the West until the mortgage mess can be worked out not just here, but in Europe," he said.\par \par \uc2 Late Monday afternoon, the euro was at $1.3969, up from $1.3923 from late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y80.79, down from Y81.41, while the euro was at Y112.86, down from Y113.46. The U.K. pound was at $1.5732, up from $1.5667. The dollar was at CHF0.9708, from CHF0.9787.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traded at 77.145, down from 77.494.\par \par \uc2 The final G-20 communique said the participating nations will "move towards more market determined exchange rate systems that reflect underlying economic fundamentals and refrain from competitive devaluation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 That said, a march against the low-yielding dollar--pumped up for weeks by the expected return of QE--resumed overnight Monday.\par \par \uc2 It will be hard for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 --to directly lower the value of its exchange rate, to help its suffering exporters--because of the new agreement, but chances are \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't view \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  as egregious market manipulation, said Steven Barrow, head of G-10 strategy at Standard Bank in London. On that basis, further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot be ruled out if the yen strengthens further, he said.\par \par \uc2 Monday's pullback in New York trading represented some nervousness at the immediate event risks coming in November, said Jessica Hoverson, fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 MF Global\uc2  in Chicago. "There is still a risk we could see a dollar rally," she said. ""Republicans could take over a majority in Congress Nov. 2," a view that is widely seen as a dollar positive with less overall political support for stimulus measures, she said. "The risk of the [Federal Reserve] disappointing is high," in terms of the size of a new stimulus package when it meets Nov. 2-3 and decides on further action, which most have already priced into the dollar and believe is a foregone conclusion, she said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was also hurt by research from \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  suggesting the Fed may need to provide another $2 trillion in additional quantitative easing--maybe double that amount, but only in a worst-case scenario.\par \par \uc2 Separately, a key member of European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governing council Monday said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be freely determined by the market and protectionist tendencies must be avoided.\par \par \uc2 "I think a verbal disarmament is absolutely necessary" when talking about global exchange rates, said Axel Weber, who is also president of Germany's Bundesbank. Prior the G-20 meeting, government officials had frequently used heated rhetoric in complaining about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.\par \par \uc2 With the ICE Dollar Index declining, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's PowerShares U.S. Dollar Index Bearish exchange-traded fund was up 0.44% from late Friday, while its PowerShares U.S. Dollar Index Bullish was down 0.45%. The two exchange-traded funds are based on \uc2 Deutsche Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -futures indexes, whose composition mirrors that of the ICE's Dollar Index. -By Andrew J. Johnson, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-3092; andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com\par 