rnational passenger numbers in September +5.8% on year. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may rise in early trade vs 1,116.8 late yesterday in Seoul, tracking USD gains vs JPY, EUR overnight as investors remain cautious over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , plus on views any extra stimulus by U.S. could be smaller-than-expected, traders say. Local bank trader pegs cap at 1,125. "Foreigners are chalking up massive amounts of local stocks, while dollar-selling by exporters are likely to come in bigger volumes as the end of the month approaches. And the view that the longer-term trend is for the dollar/won to fall, will likely lead offshore players to opt for selling the dollar on any rebound," says another trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold up marginally vs late New York; at 2315 GMT, up $2.50 at $1,342.80/oz, but little momentum despite recovery from previous week's sell-off. Jon Nadler, analyst at bullion dealer Kitco, pointed to seventh consecutive decline in ETF gold holdings Tuesday while silver ETF balances slipped for first time in three weeks: "CFTC reports indicate changes in speculative attitude underway." But bullion dealer GoldCore says physical demand still robust due to jitters ahead of U.S. Federal Reserve decision next week on possible quantitative easing, with asset purchases tipped as high as $4 trillion. ANZ also points to jewellery demand for possible support: "The falling gold price was encouraging for key Indian buyers, attracting some physical gold interest back to the market ahead of the key Hindu festival of Diwali next week." Comex December gold at only slight premium to spot, also $1,343.10/oz. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs likely to open lower on weaker U.S. Treasurys overnight, with mid-zone sectors possibly leading decline. RBS chief strategist Akito Fukunaga says 10-year JGBs look overpriced compared to other notes, further selling pressure on this sector expected. "The underlying market climate is that trading is prone to adjustment moves after interest rates have been overly on the downward path" on expectations of further monetary policy easing from BOJ, he adds. Lead December 10-year futures tipped in 143.20-143.70 band vs 143.42 last close. Analysts say impact on market of 2-year auction due later today likely limited. (kazuhiro.shimamura@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2319 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 1.7 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 3rd straight day of premarket selling may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 11.7 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 10.0 million shares. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2318 GMT [Dow Jones] USD will likely continue to rise as investors uncertain about magnitude of possible quantitative easing measures expected to be decided at FOMC meeting next week, says \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer Takayuki Asahara. Investors piled up massive USD short positions against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks on expectation of potential QE measures by Fed. Says USD/JPY may rise to 82.00, but further rises unlikely as Japan exporters will likely to increase their sales at month-end. Pair last 81.33. Says pair's downside limited at 80.50 as investors still cautious about potential JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. As investors reduce USD short positions, EUR/USD biased down, may trade in 1.3700-1.3950 range. Pair last 1.3856. Says EUR/JPY may trade in 112.40-113.30 band, roughly within yesterday's range. Pair last 112.68. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea 3Q GDP +0.7% seasonally adjusted from 2Q, decelerating from +1.4% on quarter in 2Q. On-year, GDP +4.5% vs 2Q's +7.2%. 3Q performance beats forecast for +0.6% on-quarter, +4.4% on-year in Dow Jones poll. Economists say growth likely to continue decelerating in 4Q, dragged by slowing exports, but stronger-than-expected performance in 1H, rising domestic demand, improvement in construction sector may prompt BOK to resume tightening before year-end as inflation picks up; "the pace of recovery has slowed, but the local economy will continue to improve, led by domestic consumption and facility investment," says \uc2 Woori Investment & Securities\uc2  economist June Park; "If the current low interest rates continue, this will cause an asset bubble, hurting the sustainability of the domestic economy." (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2313 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to rise as stronger USD/JPY expected to help exporters; Nikkei may trade between 9350-9500 after ending down 0.3% at 9377.38 Tuesday, market analysts say. "The index may stay in a narrow range, but it is likely to be in positive territory most of the time," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Also says before (official) Japanese corporate earnings announcements later this week, pre-earnings figures may move stocks such as \uc2 Fuji Heavy Industries\uc2  (7270.TO), which upped its 1H net profit view. Nikkei 225 December futures ended yesterday's Chicago trading at 9430 vs Osaka close of 9360. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] With market on razor thin edge ahead of 3Q Australian CPI data, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  sees a myriad of outcomes possible for bonds given data's potential impact on RBA's November rate decision. Ahead of data, Aussie 3-year down 4 ticks at 94.96. Should CPI hit +0.6% on quarter, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  expects 3-year bond futures up slightly to 95.07; +0.7% reading would see futures at 95.00; +0.8% would see futures at 94.91 and +0.9% would see futures at 94.88. 3Q CPI expected +0.8% on-quarter, according to 17 economists surveyed by Dow Jones, with economists viewing any number above +0.7% as tipping point for RBA to hike interest rates to 4.75% from current 4.5% level. Data due at 0030 GMT. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2252 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely in relatively tight ranges, trade on local cues; not much lead from U.S. markets with Wall Street managing marginal rise supported by better-than-expected consumer confidence data; investors likely to start pulling to sidelines ahead of U.S. data this week, Fed meeting next week. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, USD could rise on concerns that greenback's been oversold on expectations of more Fed quantitative easing; EUR/USD at 1.3854 vs 1.3855 late NY, EUR/JPY at 112.71 vs 112.90, USD/JPY at 81.34 vs 81.47. On data slate: Korea advance GDP at 2300 GMT, Australia CPI at 0030 GMT, New Zealand NBNZ business outlook at 0200 GMT, Taiwan business indicators at 0800 GMT. Later there's U.S. MBA mortgage applications, advance report on durable goods, new residential sales, EIA oil report, Chicago Fed Midwest manufacturing index, NY Fed president Dudley speaks, German provisional CPI. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2245 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely stronger this morning, despite flat leads from Wall Street, as above-consensus FY10 earnings and dividend from \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  (NAB.AU) could trigger gains in major banks. 3Q CPI data, due 0030 GMT, is the other major focus today. While expectations of RBA interest rate hike on Tuesday will grow if CPI is stronger than 0.8% consensus, any knee-jerk sell off in equities may prove to be a buying opportunity, as economic growth outlook remains strong. Moreover, any renewed AUD/USD strength could trigger renewed interest in carry trade related buying of Australian equities, particularly with the market seeing a good chance of quantitative easing from the Fed next week. Expect intraday support at 4680, resistance at 4725, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. Index last 4687.8. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 10-26-10 1941ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101026e6aq000en\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
