\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Speculative Gold Demand May Falter; Fed Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,670 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2010\par 01:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold up marginally vs late New York; at 2315 GMT, up $2.50 at $1,342.80/oz, but little momentum despite recovery from previous week's sell-off. Jon Nadler, analyst at bullion dealer Kitco, pointed to seventh consecutive decline in ETF gold holdings Tuesday while silver ETF balances slipped for first time in three weeks: "CFTC reports indicate changes in speculative attitude underway." But bullion dealer GoldCore says physical demand still robust due to jitters ahead of U.S. Federal Reserve decision next week on possible quantitative easing, with asset purchases tipped as high as $4 trillion. ANZ also points to jewellery demand for possible support: "The falling gold price was encouraging for key Indian buyers, attracting some physical gold interest back to the market ahead of the key Hindu festival of Diwali next week." Comex December gold at only slight premium to spot, also $1,343.10/oz. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs likely to open lower on weaker U.S. Treasurys overnight, with mid-zone sectors possibly leading decline. RBS chief strategist Akito Fukunaga says 10-year JGBs look overpriced compared to other notes, further selling pressure on this sector expected. "The underlying market climate is that trading is prone to adjustment moves after interest rates have been overly on the downward path" on expectations of further monetary policy easing from BOJ, he adds. Lead December 10-year futures tipped in 143.20-143.70 band vs 143.42 last close. Analysts say impact on market of 2-year auction due later today likely limited. (kazuhiro.shimamura@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2319 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 1.7 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 3rd straight day of premarket selling may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 11.7 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 10.0 million shares. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2318 GMT [Dow Jones] USD will likely continue to rise as investors uncertain about magnitude of possible quantitative easing measures expected to be decided at FOMC meeting next week, says \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer Takayuki Asahara. Investors piled up massive USD short positions against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks on expectation of potential QE measures by Fed. Says USD/JPY may rise to 82.00, but further rises unlikely as Japan exporters will likely to increase their sales at month-end. Pair last 81.33. Says pair's downside limited at 80.50 as investors still cautious about potential JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. As investors reduce USD short positions, EUR/USD biased down, may trade in 1.3700-1.3950 range. Pair last 1.3856. Says EUR/JPY may trade in 112.40-113.30 band, roughly within yesterday's range. Pair last 112.68. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea 3Q GDP +0.7% seasonally adjusted from 2Q, decelerating from +1.4% on quarter in 2Q. On-year, GDP +4.5% vs 2Q's +7.2%. 3Q performance beats forecast for +0.6% on-quarter, +4.4% on-year in Dow Jones poll. Economists say growth likely to continue decelerating in 4Q, dragged by slowing exports, but stronger-than-expected performance in 1H, rising domestic demand, improvement in construction sector may prompt BOK to resume tightening before year-end