\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:USD May Rise On Uncertainty On Possible QE -Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,730 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2010\par 01:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2318 GMT [Dow Jones] USD will likely continue to rise as investors uncertain about magnitude of possible quantitative easing measures expected to be decided at FOMC meeting next week, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinkin \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer Takayuki Asahara. Investors piled up massive USD short positions against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in recent weeks on expectation of potential QE measures by Fed. Says USD/JPY may rise to 82.00, but further rises unlikely as Japan exporters will likely to increase their sales at month-end. Pair last 81.33. Says pair's downside limited at 80.50 as investors still cautious about potential JPY-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities. As investors reduce USD short positions, EUR/USD biased down, may trade in 1.3700-1.3950 range. Pair last 1.3856. Says EUR/JPY may trade in 112.40-113.30 band, roughly within yesterday's range. Pair last 112.68. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea 3Q GDP +0.7% seasonally adjusted from 2Q, decelerating from +1.4% on quarter in 2Q. On-year, GDP +4.5% vs 2Q's +7.2%. 3Q performance beats forecast for +0.6% on-quarter, +4.4% on-year in Dow Jones poll. Economists say growth likely to continue decelerating in 4Q, dragged by slowing exports, but stronger-than-expected performance in 1H, rising domestic demand, improvement in construction sector may prompt BOK to resume tightening before year-end as inflation picks up; "the pace of recovery has slowed, but the local economy will continue to improve, led by domestic consumption and facility investment," says \uc2 Woori Investment & Securities\uc2  economist June Park; "If the current low interest rates continue, this will cause an asset bubble, hurting the sustainability of the domestic economy." (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2313 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to rise as stronger USD/JPY expected to help exporters; Nikkei may trade between 9350-9500 after ending down 0.3% at 9377.38 Tuesday, market analysts say. "The index may stay in a narrow range, but it is likely to be in positive territory most of the time," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Also says before (official) Japanese corporate earnings announcements later this week, pre-earnings figures may move stocks such as \uc2 Fuji Heavy Industries\uc2  (7270.TO), which upped its 1H net profit view. Nikkei 225 December futures ended yesterday's Chicago trading at 9430 vs Osaka close of 9360. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] With market on razor thin edge ahead of 3Q Australian CPI data, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  sees a myriad of outcomes possible for bonds given data's potential impact on RBA's November rate decision. Ahead of data, Aussie 3-year down 4 ticks at 94.96. Should CPI hit +0.6% on quarter, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  expects 3-year bond futures up slightly to 95.07; +0.7% reading would see futures at 95.00; +0.8% would see futures at 94.91 and +0.9% would see futures at 94.88. 3Q CPI expected +0.8% on-quarter, according to 17 economists surveyed by Dow Jones, with economists viewing any number above +0.7% as tipping point for RBA to hike interest rates to 4.75% from current 4.5% level. Data due at 0030 GMT. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2252 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely in relatively tight ranges, trade on local cues; not much lead from U.S. markets with Wall Street managing marginal rise supported by better-than-expected consumer confidence data; investors likely to start pulling to sidelines ahead of U.S. data this week, Fed meeting next week. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, USD could rise on concerns that greenback's been oversold on expectations of more Fed quantitative easing; EUR/USD at 1.3854 vs 1.3855 late NY, EUR/JPY at 112.71 vs 112.90, USD/JPY at 81.34 vs 81.47. On data slate: Korea advance GDP at 2300 GMT, Australia CPI at 0030 GMT, New Zealand NBNZ business outlook at 0200 GMT, Taiwan business indicators at 0800 GMT. Later there's U.S. MBA mortgage applications, advance report on durable goods, new residential sales, EIA oil report, Chicago Fed Midwest manufacturing index, NY Fed president Dudley speaks, German provisional CPI. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2245 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely stronger this morning, despite flat leads from Wall Street, as above-consensus FY10 earnings and dividend from \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  (NAB.AU) could trigger gains in major banks. 3Q CPI data, due 0030 GMT, is the other major focus today. While expectations of RBA interest rate hike on Tuesday will grow if CPI is stronger than 0.8% consensus, any knee-jerk sell off in equities may prove to be a buying opportunity, as economic growth outlook remains strong. Moreover, any renewed AUD/USD strength could trigger renewed interest in carry trade related buying of Australian equities, particularly with the market seeing a good chance of quantitative easing from the Fed next week. Expect intraday support at 4680, resistance at 4725, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. Index last 4687.8. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2237 GMT [Dow Jones] Comex copper futures pointing towards higher open for LME copper, with December contract at $3.8905/lb, $8,578.50/ton vs LME's $8,510/ton close yesterday. ANZ sees base metals as "toppy, but heightened expectations of mine strike activity in South America and declining exchange stockpiles are likely to see funds add to already high long positions". LME copper stocks declined 900 tons to 367,475 tons Tuesday while cancelled warrants trimmed 275 tons to 28,425 tons, 12.9 days' supply, and EUR/USD tad weaker at 1.3856. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2227 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat prices likely to rise after U.S. wheat hit one-week highs yesterday on poor crop ratings, stoking renewed fears over the direction of global wheat supplies. Most-active Chicago Board of Trade December wheat ended 18 cents, or 2.7%, higher at US$6.92 a bushel. In CBOT trades, speculative fund buying was estimated at 4,000 lots. AUD/USD tad weaker at 0.9854 from 0.9906 late yesterday. On Tuesday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in New South Wales lost A$2.70 to A$256.80/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in Western Australia lost A$1.50 at A$288.50. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2222 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Metals searched for direction Tuesday as mixed U.S. economic data and a strengthening dollar took the wind out of recent rises across the complex. Traders say base metals lacked strong upward momentum, driving copper, nickel and tin lower, while the rest of the industrial metals rose. LME 3-month copper fell $7 against Monday's PM kerb trade to $8,510 a metric ton, while aluminum rose $17.50 to $2,389.50 and zinc rose $50 to $2,615. Lead put on $9 to hit $2,593, but nickel was down $255 to $23,295/ton and tin $225 to $26,650. Precious metals were little changed, as physical demand from India and the Middle East steadied gold's sharp sell-off last week. Spot gold rose 30 cents to $1,340.60 a troy ounce, while silver rose 26 cents to $24.12, platinum $4 to $1,705 and palladium $1 to $629. The USD Index edged up to 77.641, up 0.93% from Monday's 76.709 low, while EUR/USD was marginally down at 1.3856. Nymex crude rose 3 cents to $82.55 a barrel. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2208 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads flat, with overnight SPI 200 futures down 0.1% at 4685.0 after Wall Street was balanced by mixed company earning reports and economic data. DJIA up 0.1% at 11169.46, S&P 500 flat at 1185.64, Nasdaq up 0.3% at 2497.29. In Europe, Stoxx 600 fell 0.2% and FTSE 100 fell 0.8% on disappointing earnings from \uc2 UBS\uc2  and cautious outlook from \uc2 Arcelor Mittal\uc2 , both stocks declining 5.0%. U.S. October consumer confidence rose to 50.2 vs 50.0 expected, but Case-Shiller August home price index fell for the first time in 4 months). S&P 500 Financials up 0.1%, with \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  up 1.3% after recent falls on U.S. mortgage foreclosure crisis. S&P 500 Materials down 0.2%, with DuPont down 1.7% on 10% fall in 3Q profit, Vale up 1.2%, Freeport up 2.3%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 3.4% on 3Q EPS loss of 35 cents vs 22 cents expected. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs at equivalent A$41.72 vs local close at A$41.83. London-listed BHP down 2.5%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) down 1.2%. S&P 500 Industrials down 0.3%. S&P 500 Energy up 0.3%. S&P/ASX 200 last 4687.8. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2154 GMT [Dow Jones] If 3Q Australian CPI is weaker than expected 0.7% on quarter, AUD/USD could test below 0.9662, says NAB \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist John Kyriakopoulos. "A 0.9% on-quarter rise in the underlying CPI would all but guarantee an RBA rate hike next month, which traders currently price just below a 50% chance," in turn boosting AUD/USD. Pair last 0.9867. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2129 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD would need a major upward surprise on 3Q CPI due 0030 GMT to breach 0.9950, says CBA \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Joseph Capurso. Pair 0.9854 early. Market consensus is core inflation of 0.8% on quarter; Capurso says 0.9% reading would up bets of a rate hike by RBA next week, pricing now around 47%. "It would have to be a hell of a CPI number" to significantly up pricing. (enda.curran@dowjones.com) [ 10-26-10 1918ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101026e6aq000eh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
