\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Dollar Slide Stalls, Japan Ramps Up JPY Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2010\par 09:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0700 GMT [Dow Jones] The post G20 dollar selloff stalled overnight helped in part by an unexpected strong gain for US existing home sales. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ramped up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  talk with Finance Minister Noda saying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves look a bit one sided and bold action would be taken if necessary, while his deputy Igarashi warned \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was very much an option and a surprise move would probably be effective. For Tuesday the data highlight is the first reading of UK 3Q GDP where a 0.4% gain is expected, anything lower will encourage thoughts of more QE while anything approaching 1% will have the opposite effect. For the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , all eyes are on USD/JPY with Monday's fresh 15-year low of 80.41 the obvious target on the downside while for GBP/USD which has already enjoyed a short covering rally in late Asia trade the jury is out until 0830GMT. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0655 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CHF is currently caught in a neutral trend, after snapping a 4-month downtrend, and is set to consolidate around current levels in the near term, according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  technical analysts. Downside support for the pair is pegged at 0.9635, above the Oct. 7 low of 0.9555, while upside resistance is seen at last week's high of 0.9806, they say. USD/CHF at 0.9706. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.2% at 23,578.14, with index staying in narrow range of less than 200 points (23,562.03-23,727.63) so far, amid caution ahead of FOMC meeting in U.S. next week, says Linus Yip, strategist at First Shanghai. Tips index in 23,000-23,800 range near-term. "It would be key to watch for any details regarding further quantitative easing in the U.S., which has been one of the key drivers for the market's recent rally." Cheung tips Chinese airline, auto, insurance plays on their strong industry outlook. Sinopec (0386.HK) +2.3% at HK$7.59 after China fuel price hike news, but China property stocks lower on concern of further tightening in China, Cheung says; China Overseas (0688.HK) down 1.4% at HK$16.10, \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) down 2.2% at HK$15.40. Volume brisk at HK$55.33 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0648 GMT [Dow Jones] Troika Dialog recommends being long the ruble at current levels, entering at 36.35 versus the basket for a rally to the 34.50-34.70 range. "We are still in the middle of the tax payment period, with this week including mineral extraction and profit taxes. This may add some fuel to the fire and allow the ruble to continue rebounding," the investment bank says. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex down 0.1% at 20,274.40 as investors cautious ahead of derivative contracts expiry Thursday, says S.P. Tulsian, independent investment advisor. Adds, markets likely rangebound with more weakness later in session; also says Sensex may see upward trend from November, with trigger likely to be strong 2Q earnings from \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) due Saturday. Tips 19,700-20,500 range for week, with 19,400 as support level for session. \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  +0.3 at INR1,092.95, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) down 1.0% at INR1,131.10, \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  (500112.BY) off 1.2% at INR3,201.15. (suhita.poddar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are down Tuesday, with the curve flattening bias resuming ahead of ECB liquidity \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week. Safe haven bunds rose Monday, with the front-end of the curve outperforming after the recent cheapening. However, the direction of money market rates continues to be an important influence on schatz, and the market will be keeping an eye on the 7-day main refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday ahead of the 3-month liquidity tender on Wednesday. Eonia overnight fixed at 0.859%, up from 0.849% on Friday. Data already out show German consumer confidence is set to stabilize in November. December bunds are down 0.13 at 129.71 and the 10-year bund is down at 98.15, yielding 2.462%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] STI down 0.2% at 3175.05; index stuck in consolidation mode under 3200 mark, touched Oct. 14, 15, highest since May 2008; analysts say will be hard for STI to breach that level amid sideways trade, with upcoming FOMC meeting most likely candidate to give meaningful push to index either way. Last 1.8 decliners for every gainer, vs 1.9 to one midday. Volume moderate with 1.09 billion shares traded worth S$1.12 billion. SGX (S68.SG) down 1.7% at S$8.80 in active trade as investors fret over high premium bourse wants to pay for ASX (ASX.AU), also increasing opposition to deal in Australia; Transcu (E15.SG) +7.4% at S$0.145 after saying it gets in-principle nod for rights shares, warrants listing; Mapletree Industrial (ME8U.SG) down 0.9% at S$1.08, second most active today. Index trades in narrow range today, unlikely to move much either way before close; support at last week's low of 3148 looks safe. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0640 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +0.4% at 8343.23, trading in narrow range on strength in plastic, petrochemical shares, following yesterday's 1.7% gain. "The index was capped because most investors were sidelined ahead of investor conferences of major tech firms," says Grand Cathay Securities analyst Mars Hsu. Adds, most petrochemical shares boosted by positive outlook for Chinese domestic demand, weaker USD. Expects Taiex to trade in 8300-8400 range tomorrow. Formosa Plastics (1301.TW) +3.6% at NT$90.00. Formosa Chemicals and Fibre (1326.TW) +2.9% at NT$88.80. Tech shares mixed, \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) +2.5% at NT$30.75 on bargain hunting. TSMC (2330.TW) +0.5% at NT$62.30. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0639 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economist Piotr Kalisz expects Hungary to keep interest rates on hold at 5.25% until 3Q 2011, with rate hikes possible before that if risks to financial stability increased substantially. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to watch carefully the implementation of the government's fiscal package, and if that is accompanied by increased market volatility and strong HUF depreciation pressure, it could act earlier. However, he expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would choose first to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the HUF market and use rate hikes only as a last solution. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0637 GMT [Dow Jones] The tone of Hungary's Monetary Policy Council was far from constructive after Monday's interest rate decision, and, "unless risk rallies convincingly from here, we remain closer to a rate hike than a rate cut," says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  economist \uc2 Pasquale Diana\uc2 . MPC left the key rate unchanged at 5.25%. Diana notes the tone of the MPC changed quite significantly as it thinks the government's fiscal measures could boost upside risks to CPI and increase uncertainty in the investment environment. That's a change versus September, when it sounded more relaxed on the CPI outlook. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0634 GMT [Dow Jones] STOCK CALL: \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says China's latest fuel price hike in line with expectations. Reckons hikes may restore confidence in pricing mechanism, especially after PBOC's benchmark interest rate hike and 3.6% on-year rise in September CPI, which was highest in 23 months. Notes NDRC indicated it had taken CNY strength into consideration, and price hike would have been bigger if not for impact of stronger Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Says Sinopec's (0386.HK) October refining margin likely at US$8.3/bbl (similar to high level seen in April, when there was also round of price hikes), mainly due to low-cost crude inventory with time lag of about 1.5 months. Thinks margins could decline to around US$5/bbl in November. Reckons year-to-date domestic refining margin of about US$5/bbl could be maintained. Shares last +1.9% at HK$7.56. (yvonne.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0623 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mixed; Nikkei down 0.3%, S&P/ASX 200 down 0.5%, Kospi +0.2%, Shanghai Composite down 0.8%, HSI down 0.1%, STI 0.2% lower, KLCI +0.1%, NZX-50 off 0.1%, Taiex +0.4%, SET +0.6%, Sensex down 0.3%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, JPY remains near 15-year high vs USD despite Japanese authorities' latest effort to talk down strong JPY; "I think the moves yesterday were a bit one-sided," says Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda. Traders say investors have grown accustomed to such "verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " from Japanese authorities; USD/JPY at 80.71 vs 80.79 late Monday in NY. EUR/JPY at 112.70 vs 112.86, EUR/USD at 1.3965 vs 1.3939. Japan September corporate service price index down 1.1% on-year, 0.1% lower on-month at 96.7. National Australian Bank's business conditions index for Australia fell 1 point to +5 points in 3Q vs 2Q; business confidence index for nation up 6 points to +9 points over same period. Key index measuring consumer confidence in Korea fell for third straight month to 16-month low in October; BOK says consumer sentiment index was at 108 in October, down from 109 in September. Singapore manufacturing output +26.2% on-year in September vs revised +7.7% in August, +18.9% expected. Philippine imports in August rose 22% on-year to $4.41 billion. Spot gold at $1,338.30/oz, down $2.00 vs NY close. December Nymex crude oil futures down 30 cents at $82.22/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com) [ 10-26-10 0300ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101026e6aq00044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
