\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Taiwan Unlikely To Set New Rules On Bond Flows-JPM\b0\par\par\uc2 1,471 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2010\par 04:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0202 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan unlikely to carry out more measures to discourage foreign funds buying local bonds, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The key...is the government's sensitivity to effects on stocks as foreign ownership levels are above 30% and lower valuations would be problematic before this year's elections." Traders say foreign funds likely to enter Taiwan via bond market, deposits, then flow to stock market. Adds, Taiwan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been ahead of curve in fighting hot inflows; "the most likely outcome in the immediate near term is a continuation of bank inspections and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 )." (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] October HSI futures down 0.1% at 23,673, as cash market tipped to stay in tight range amid absence of fresh catalysts. Contract ended Monday at 79-point premium to cash, pointing to positive bias near-term. October open interest down 8,612 lots at 103,503, as investors likely rolling over positions with current month futures expiring Thursday.(susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets notes \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has shrugged off weekend meeting of G-20 ministers with USD/JPY fluctuating in tight range around Y1 over past 10 days or so, suggesting a strong move in either direction depending on how the market plays out. "Although the dollar tends to bottom at a 40-45 week cycle vs the yen (suggesting possible inflection point), its direction probably depends on which way volume breaks." Separately, also notes networking equipment maker heavyweight Ericsson beat profit expectations for recent quarter, thanks to increased capital spending at wireless service providers in North America and Japan. While some view firm as mobile phone handset maker, house says Ericsson is actually the global leader in terrestrial wireless infrastructure, thinks such developments may point to emergence of an upcycle in capital spending on wireless broadband infrastructure, ostensibly affecting \uc2 NTT Docomo\uc2  (9437.TO), \uc2 KDDI\uc2  (9433.TO) in Japan. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] STI down 0.1% at 3177.58 after briefly trading in positive territory at open; index likely consolidating with mixed regional cues, also after Monday's 0.3% rise took it close to psychological 3200 level, which analysts see as hard to overcome. Trader at local brokerage says there's "nothing much happening;" focus still on SGX (S68.SG) with analyst reports coming out after news yesterday of its merger bid with ASX. Says today's reaction likely "more considered," shares' 2.6% fall to S$8.72, shows view of deal not too favourable. DBS (D05.SG) off 0.7% at S$14.50, OCBC (039.SG) down 0.2% at S$9.07, UOB (U11.SG) +0.3% at S$18.72; trader says interest in banks to be strong ahead of 3Q results starting next week amid ongoing concerns over effect of low Sibor on profitability. 392 million shares traded, worth S$318 million; gainers, decliners fairly evenly matched; support at 3148, last week's low. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] Base, precious metals consolidating yesterday's gains in early Asia trade despite USD paring yesterday's declines overnight. EUR/USD at 1.3957 at similar level to where it ended last week, well off yesterday's 1.4080 intraday high. But persistent bullish metals sentiment obvious from early price action with gold, copper remaining well bid and yesterday's laggards like platinum playing catch-up. Spot platinum at $1,701/oz, up $9 since NY close, while palladium at $611/oz, up $3. Triland tips near-term targets of $1,724 for platinum, $620 for palladium. On LME, 3-month copper at $8,530.25/ton, up $12.25 with 606 lots done so far, helped by strong Chinese interest. SHFE February copper at CNY65,160/ton, up CNY530 and at discount to LME of around $200 compared to $300 at open yesterday. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0137 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares may rise; better-than-expected 3Q corporate earnings lifting sentiment so far, while ample global liquidity amid broad USD weakness, expectations Fed will pump more money into U.S. economy also lending decent support. Hopes of more quantitative easing will help foreign investors maintain interest in local stocks, says KT Zmico Securities' First VP Charoen Iamphattanatham. Tips firm resistance at 1000; without strong volume, says it's difficult to break that psychological resistance. Support put at 982. Thai market was closed Monday for public holiday. SET Index ended +0.4% at 992.24 Friday. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.4% at 4691.6, hits intraday high of 4685.8 as buying fades amid political resistance to ASX (ASX.AU) takeover. Some traders feel the market is still finding resistance near 4700.0 (61.8% retracement of April-May decline), despite Monday's close at 4710.0. But focus remains on buying dips before bank reporting season and expected U.S. quantitative easing next week. "Given the run we've had in the past 6 weeks and the events in the week ahead, there's probably some risk to take off the table but I still think the market will be higher by the end of the year," says IG Markets Strategist Ben Potter. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD lower at 7.7593 vs 7.7615 yesterday on equity-driven inflows, says senior trader at Chinese bank. HSI ends up 0.5% yesterday. Tips pair in 7.7570-7.7610 band. Adds "I spotted some foreign banks buying the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on corporate demand, putting downward pressure on the pair." (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil futures tad lower in early Asia trading amid mixed regional equities after closing 1.0% higher overnight. Nymex December contract recovers after falling to $82.05/bbl in early Asia trade, last down 8 cents at $82.44/bbl on Globex; oil futures likely to trade sideways rest of week as bulls, bears alike expected to be cautious ahead of Fed meeting next week, says Jim Ritterbusch at Ritterbusch and Associates; expects downward pressure on dollar to persist, supporting crude prices. ICE Brent down 5 cents at $83.49/bbl. (gurdeep.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] Marubeni (8002.TO) off 0.2% at Y524 after opening in positive territory. Analysts say overall bearish market outweighing positive sentiment from $650 million deal announced Monday to purchase interests in four mature deep water oil and gas field in U.S. Gulf of Mexico from BP (BP). Investors also uncertain for now about how much profit deal will generate, especially weighed against acquisition cost, trader at Japanese brokerage says. Operating officer at Japanese brokerage adds trading house shares in general facing profit-taking following recent sharp rise on back of strength in commodity markets. \uc2 Mitsubishi Corp\uc2  (8058.TO) down 0.8% at Y1,980. \uc2 Mitsui & Co\uc2  (8031.TO) off 0.8% at Y1,303. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  has Neutral rating on Marubeni with Y560 target.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] Steepened JGB yield curve good reflection of future interest rate rise, makes floating rate bonds with long current maturity attractive, says RBS chief strategist Akito Fukunaga. Notes when timing of expected interest rate climb hard to foresee, "the best investment strategy is buying floating rate bonds with the longest current maturity available." Adds possibility high for Japan government's eventual use of reflationary policies, which would bring sharp rise in long-term rates. (kazuhiro.shimamura@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0118 GMT [Dow Jones] Players will be unwilling to sell USD/JPY below 80.50 as caution about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  grows after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda expresses displeasure earlier today, about Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves, says Tomohiro Nishida, senior dealer at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking. Yesterday, USD/JPY marked fresh 15-year low at 80.41. Noda says "the moves yesterday were a bit one-sided," raising caution about another JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Personally, I think there might be an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the pair breaks 80.00, but if they do \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any time, the dollar will surge by at least Y1-Y2. That's why it's hard to sell the pair below 80.50." Pair last 80.69 down from 80.79 Monday late New York. Says pair's topside limited at 81.20 in Asia, as Japan exporters will likely sell pair if pair rises that much as end of month approaches.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com) [ 10-25-10 2202ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101026e6aq0001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
