s Sensex in 20,200-20,350 range. \uc2 Hindustan Unilever\uc2  (500696.BY) +1.1% at INR304.80, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) +0.9% at INR1,090.60. \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) +1.3% at INR1,167.50, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) +1.0% at INR1,142.80. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] Sell volatility in USD/JPY market with mildly bearish directional bias to year-end to exploit overpriced USD/JPY gamma and slow spot downtrend via ratio USD/JPY put spreads or, as limited-risk alternative, long reverse knockout USD/JPY puts, says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus senior analyst Russell Floyd; adds, short USD down one-touches would suit those who prefer to earn premium. Recommends selling $40 million 2-month 78.90 USD/JPY put, and buying $20 million 81.10 USD/JPY put for zero net premium; or buying Nov 25 81.10 USD/JPY put with 78.20 knockout for 0.25% USD; or selling Nov 25 78.20 one-touch for 28% USD. See =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEWS: Sell Pricey Front-End Dollar/Yen Volatility. (murugesan.suppayyan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds lower as supply from INR110 billion government bond auction weighs, but bonds likely to find support at current levels given intensity of correction over last few weeks, says trader with primary dealership; 8.13% 2022 bond at 100.07 vs 100.24 last close. "Bonds have probably priced in a lot of the negatives from stock-specific issues surrounding the benchmark, as well as tight liquidity," says trader; adds, with expectations from up to INR120 billion government bond buyback low, surprises, if any, may just be to upside. RBI to buyback 8.75% 2010, 12.32% 2011, 6.57% 2011 note at buyback auction later in session. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0404 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD down sharply at 30.645 vs 30.910 late Friday on foreign fund inflows, gains in local shares, says trader. Taiex last +1.5% at 8290.38. "The pair could break through support at NT$30.600, but I expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lend some support later today," he says. Adds, corporate demand also expected to lend support; tips pair in 30.60-30.70 range today.(paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0402 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 close relative to major Fibonacci retracement at 4700 (61.8% retracement of April-May fall) should determine if index is headed for rapid gains toward April 2010 peak at 5025.0 or further consolidation above major support at 4559.0. Index has received strong bullish impetus from Singapore Exchange's (S68.SG) takeover action on ASX (ASX.AU). Takeover interest supports bullish inverse head & shoulders pattern which targets 5025, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. Index last up 1.5% at 4717.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0354 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares turn higher midday, as banks rebound on ample liquidity. Shanghai Composite Index +1.3% at 3014.03, up from intraday low of 2959.30 earlier; with cap at 3041 recent high level. "When investors realize the stock index won't fall too much given the flush liquidity in the system, they will go for the low-valuation banking sector," says Wang Qunqing at Guosen Securities. \uc2 China Everbright Bank\uc2  (601818.SH) +1.1% at CNY4.52, Industrial Bank (601166.SH) +2.0% at CNY28.46. Securities firms also higher as investors bullish on long-term market performance, with Citic Securities (600030.SH) +7.4% at CNY16.15. Shenzhen index +2.0% at CNY1287.91. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR down, tracking global USD, but onset of month-end oil importer demand, talk of repatriation of funds brought in for almost $3.5 billion \uc2 Coal India Ltd\uc2 . IPO, helping support pair, says trader with private bank; pair at 44.40 vs 44.59 late Friday, trader tips 44.30-44.60 band in session. "Unless something really drives the euro up, I don't see much of a downside for the pair now," says trader. But notes, eventual rally to as high as 45.00 on cards likely within week as importer buying for month-end payments likely to be key focus as market wary of beginning of heavy outflows as foreign funds earmarked for \uc2 Coal India\uc2  IPO flow back out again once refunds realized. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0352 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  recommends shorting 3-month USD/INR NDF as "drivers of carry and inflow are expected to remain strong." Notes, in short-term, however, risks are that return of \uc2 Coal India\uc2  IPO subscription funds may engender some outflow risk for INR; also, with choppy USD conditions, house tightens stop level to 45.10 to guard against a blowout. "However, we reiterate this only as a short-term risk. Our fundamental view for INR appreciation remains unchanged," says house. Trade entered at 45.72 on Sept. 27, currently +148bps at 45.05; sets target at 44.00. Notes, there's very little evidence that RBI has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite outsized recent IPO inflows. "Only after large scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen, and failed, will capital curbs be considered and we believe that we are still some way from that point." (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0344 GMT [Dow Jones] Anticipated Fed quantitative easing, weaker USD already factored into crude oil futures, says Yingxi Yu at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . "In terms of the G-20 statement, we don't think there's anything that will significantly change the landscape for trading in oil." Says Nymex prompt contract well supported around $80/bbl, with upside resistance around $85/bbl. "We're not entirely convinced of an accelerated pace of price gains for the next three months." Crude futures up steadily Monday amid falls in US dollar against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Nymex December contract up 77 cents at $82.46/bbl on Globex; ICE Brent contract for December up 63 cents at $83.59/bbl. (gurdeep.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0340 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares last +0.6% at 447.72 led by foreigners on 3Q earnings hopes, though pessimism over high CPI remain. "Although high inflation continues to hurt profitability, many companies continue to report solid results for the Jan-Sept period, attracting more buyers, especially foreigners," says brokerage analyst. Volume low with 23 million shares valued at VND590 billion traded. Among advancers, \uc2 PetroVietnam\uc2  Fertilizer (DPM.VH) +1.5% at VND33,200, Masan (MSN.VH) +1.8% at VND55,000, Vincom (VIC.VH) +3.8% at VND68,500. Trader tips immediate resistance for key index at 450. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0332 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares up 0.5% at 3616.501 in moderate volume, tracking gains in many Asian markets, with firmer IDR providing support, traders say; expect main index to top 3650 if key 3620 level breached. "Buying in most banks and consumere-related stocks by foreign funds has driven the main index higher," says trader at Lautandhana Securities. Among gainers: Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) up 3.2% at IDR11,200, car maker Astra (ASII.JK) up 0.7% at IDR56,650, while retailer Matahari (MPPA.JK) up 2.9% at IDR1,410 on hopes for strong nine-month earnings. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com) [ 10-25-10 0016ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101025e6ap0002h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
