\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Down; May Test 15-Yr Low - Strategist\b0\par\par\uc2 1,357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2010\par 03:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may test 80.84, 15-year-low marked last Wednesday as post G-20 statement that says economic powers will "move towards more market determined exchange rate systems" might make it harder for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market again, says Yuji Kameoka, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daiwa Institute of Research\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Adds that statement may encourage short-term investors to increase USD/JPY short positions more aggressively than before. Pair last 81.10 down from 81.30 earlier. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may stay in range following Wall Street's mixed performance Friday, while investors expected to tread with caution ahead of FOMC meeting Nov. 2, says Mark To at Wing Fung. To tips index to be mostly in 23,500-23,700 range today. Reckons impact of China rate hike should have been well factored in, while trading volume may decrease amid absence of fresh leads with no real surprises from G-20 meeting at weekend. HKEx (0388.HK) unlikely to react too much to WSJ report Singapore Exchange (S68.SG) plans to offer US$8.2 billion for all of Australian Securities Exchange (ASX.AU), as deal would have to clear significant regulatory hurdles. HSI ended down 0.6% at 23,517.54 Friday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil futures rise in early Asia trading, supported by modest gains in regional equities; prices boosted by USD weakness as greenback selling resumes post G-20 meeting. ICE Dollar Index last at 77.290, down 0.395. Nymex December contract up 56 cents at $82.25/bbl on Globex; ICE Brent for December up 49 cents at $83.45/bbl. (gurdeep.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD continues heading south on inflows; pair last at 30.800 vs Friday's close at 30.910. Trader from local bank tips pair to test 30.600 today; "the overall trend of a strengthening Taiwan dollar remains intact, although the pace of flows appears more disciplined recently." Notes exporters may buy around 30.900 to help contain TWD gains. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0115 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD lower at 7.7620 vs 7.7657 Friday as broad USD weakness continues after G-20 commitment on need to avoid "competitive devaluation" of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says trader at U.K. bank. Tips 7.7600-7.7650 band in near term. "The G-20 didn't bring any surprise to the market and the U.S. dollar's weakness is once again the trading theme, putting downward pressure on the pair," trader says. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiex last +0.4% at 8204.01, likely to consolidate around 8100-8200 range today, says Yuanta Securities analyst Kerry Chen; "around NT$110 billion of funds are locked after subscribing for TPK's (3673.TW) IPO, plus many major tech companies are due to update their fourth-quarter outlook this week. I think people are likely to wait on the sidelines today and tomorrow." UMC (2303.TW), TSMC (2330.TW), \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW), Asustek (2357.TW), HTC (2498.TW), Acer (2353.TW) are due to announce 3Q earnings results, 4Q guidance from Wednesday; TSMC last flat at NT$61.80, HTC +0.4% at NT$680.00. TPK to debut Friday, Chen tips rival Wintek (2384.TW) may fall due to portfolio shifting by some fund managers. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD rises to 1.4022 in early Asian trade as broad USD weakness expected to continue; nothing much came out from G-20, investors likely to shift their focus back to expected additional quantitative easing by Fed next week, says senior dealer at major Japanese bank. Tips immediate resistance at 1.4030. If pair breaks that level, stop-loss buying orders will be triggered, sending pair to 1.4050. Pair last at 1.4020. EUR/JPY rises to 113.73 in tandem; says cross may rise to 114.80 vs last 113.70. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may continue its technical consolidation around key resistance at 3000, but downside remains capped as investors upbeat about market's performance in coming months on ample liquidity. Shanghai Composite Index last ends down 0.3% at 2975.04. "Though the stock index needs to take a breath after recent gains...the really limited losses (in the past 2 trading days) suggest the market is still strong," says Wei Daoke at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. Property developers may fall following \uc2 China Vanke\uc2 's (000002.SZ) lower-than-expected earnings; Vanke says 3Q net profit up 6.1% from year earlier. Shenzhen index last ends +1.1% at 1262.16. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last up 0.3% at 1902.48, led by auto, shipbuilding, machinery stocks; "as China's retail sales data in October turned out strong, stocks that have something to do with consumption in China are getting a boost from it," says Bae Sung-young at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 ; tips 1910 cap for day. Among leading gainers, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +1.2% at KRW170,000, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) +3.5% at KRW41,150, \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  (009540.SE) +2.9% at KRW352,500, \uc2 STX Offshore & Shipbuilding\uc2  (067250.SE) +5.7% at KRW24,100, Doosan Infracore (042670.SE) +4.2% at KRW27,050. But techs falling due to weaker-than-expected 3Q earnings forecasts in some companies; \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) down 2.9% at KRW102,000, \uc2 Samsung SDI\uc2  (006400.SE) off 2.0% at KRW151,000. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to fall moderately on USD weakness following G-20 commitment on need to avoid "competitive devaluation" of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , push toward market-based exchange rate systems. USD/CNY central parity eyed around 6.6650 vs 6.6759 Friday, pair tipped in 6.6450-6.6650 band for session vs 6.6567 at Friday's close. "The G-20's impact on the market will be subdued, with the dollar-yuan mostly following dollar's move globally," says Shanghai-based trader at European bank; adds CNY weakness over past few sessions, following surprise rate hike last week, mostly an attempt by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to discourage CNY appreciation speculation. "But we think the yuan is still on an appreciation trend, just it will be in the fashion of two steps forward, one step back." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW lower at 1,116.6 vs 1,123.0 late Friday in Seoul following G-20's commitment on need to avoid "competitive devaluation" of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , boosting KRW sentiment, while EUR gains in early Asian hours also helping, analysts say. Woori Futures analyst Byeon Ji-young pegs initial support for pair at 1,115. If euro breaches key $1.4000 mark - which firm resistance in recent days - KRW likely to see bigger gains. But notes "with the latest agreement between G-20 nations, the local authorities may now opt for using indirect ways to curb the won's gains, such as the implementation of stronger regulations" to curb foreign capital inflows. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,335.60/oz, up $7.90 (or 0.6%) since Friday's NY close, helped by EUR/USD which up at 1.3992 after closing last week at 1.3954. Vague G-20 commitment to avoid "competitive devaluation" of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  means it's back to business as usual for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, with weekend meeting of finance ministers, central bankers yielding no actual policy commitments. However, few traders expected anything substantive from meeting, so it remains to be seen how long effect will last; gold likely to keep tracking EUR/USD. (james.campbell@dowjones.com) [ 10-24-10 2133ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101025e6ap0000y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
