\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Malaysia Govt Bonds Biased Up; Benign Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 1,555 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2010\par 01:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2358 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds likely in range but biased up, bolstered by data indicating benign inflation in September; Malaysia's CPI rose at slower-than-expected 1.8% in September, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  likely to stand pat on interest rates for rest of year, analysts say. While CPI data may lend support, room for upside likely limited near-term due to lingering supply concerns amid expectations of increased bond issuances in 2011, dealers say. Yields on benchmark MGSs ended little changed Friday, with May 2013 unchanged at 3.18%, August 2015 unchanged at 3.32%, September 2017 down 3 bps at 3.62%, November 2019 unchanged at 3.81%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD lower at 1.2978 vs 1.3006 late Asia Friday as investors sentiment remains bearish for USD given that G-20 communique didn't enforce any commitment toward defending the USD, says trader with local bank. "The G-20 stopped short of establishing any targets...everyone was committed to no comparative devaluation (vs USD) which means that other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to appreciate against the (U.S.) dollar," trader says. Tips pair to test Oct. 14 low of 1.2886 later today. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.1% at 4653.4 in quiet trading after lack of movement on Wall Street on Friday. Equity traders generally unexcited about weekend G-20 finance ministers' meeting and consolidation is expected to prevail before major events this week including domestic CPI data, bank earnings, U.S. earnings and economic data, followed by the FOMC meeting next week. "I think the market will be more focused on things like domestic CPI data and U.S. GDP data this week," says Macquarie Private Wealth division director Martin Lakos. "We expect the RBA to stay on hold next month, while the Fed restarts quantitative easing, so the environment should be positive for equities." Also highlights potential for increased M&A activity in Australia. Adds that while S&P/ASX 200 currently lacks momentum, break above 4700 seems inevitable in the short term. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2354 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 23.2% at HK$3.985 billion Friday, 4.7% of market total volume, vs 5.5% Thursday, suggests HSI likely to resume uptrend after 0.6% fall Friday. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) leads with HK$446.73 million, then China Life (2628.HK) with HK$339.12 million, A50 (2823.HK) with HK$184.43 million, \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK) with HK$155.64 million and Ping An (2318.HK) with HK$141.23 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely to open higher as dollar-carry trade expected to continue after G-20 meeting of finance ministers, central bankers agreed last week to let exchange rates to be determined by market. As agreed at G-20, "weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of developed nations and strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of emerging economies will likely remain intact," says \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2 . Foreigners seeking for relatively higher returns may find strengthening KRW as good excuse to pick up local shares, analysts say; but adds, sharp gains unlikely, at least until Fed's formal announcement on expected easing momentary measures early next week. "If the size of the U.S.' easing monetary measure comes below the market's expectation of around $500 billion to as much as $1 trillion, the Kospi may even reverse (upward) trend," Daewoo adds. Kospi ended +1.2% at 1897.31 Friday, tipped to test 1900 today. Analysts expect techs, shipbuilders to lead gains. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2348 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall vs 1,123.0 late Friday in Seoul after G-20 nations agreed at weekend meeting on need to avoid "competitive devaluation" of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a deal which likely to spread view that local authorities will no longer be so quick to prevent KRW gains, traders say. But local bank trader expects pair's trade to remain volatile until G-20 summit in November; notes "we have to keep in mind that market expectation for further quantitative easing from the U.S. could considerably weaken" along the way depending on U.S. economic data, which would bolster USD. Tips pair in 1,115-1,130 range for day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore Exchange (S68.SG) to release announcement at 0100 GMT, hold media briefing at 0115 GMT, requests trade resumption at 0200 GMT, exchange says in filing without elaborating. Bourse requested share halt Friday, while its Australian counterpart ASX (ASX.AU) shares were also halted amid media reports both exchanges were in talks; Sunday, WSJ reported, citing 2 people familiar with deal, SGX plans to offer US$8.2 billion for all of ASX; one person says SGX will offer A$48 (US$47.11) per ASX share--A$22 in cash, A$26 in SGX shares. Combined entity could create around US$1.9 trillion market; serious rival to exchanges in Hong Kong, Tokyo as may lure away big fee-payers like high-frequency traders; deep, liquid market attractive for companies seeking to raise capital. However, deal would face significant regulatory hurdles, potential backlash in Australia, where foreign ownership of companies is sensitive issue. SGX shares halted Friday at S$9.54. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [Dow Jones] RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Greg Gibbs thinks week ahead could be parity time again for AUD/USD, though this time likely without the party. Big event risk is 3Q CPI which may add to case for RBA to hike, while Fed argues over more QE. "The diverging policy directions between Australia and the U.S. may again see the AUD testing parity in the coming week." Last 0.9845. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 5.2 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket selling after 1 day of buying may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 15.1 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 9.9 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2320 GMT [Dow Jones] Potential additional quantitative easing measures expected to be announced by Fed next week may continue to weigh on USD, despite U.S. Treasury Secretary Geithner's post G-20 comments affirming U.S.'s support for strong USD, says senior dealer at major Japan trust bank. Tips USD/JPY bias down, key level at 80.80, which market failed to break several times in recent sessions; last 81.30. Any rise in USD/JPY may be capped at 81.50 due to expected sales by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  exporters. Says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities may conduct JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any time if pair falls sharply. Expects another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if pair falls sharply below all-time low of 79.75. Also, EUR/USD may test 1.4161 marked mid October sometime this week on broad USD-bearish trend; last 1.3942. Tips support at 1.3900 for global day. Says EUR/JPY may trade in 113.00-114.00 range vs last 113.35. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2318 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals likely supported this week with no major impact from weekend's G-20 meeting, says Sydney based trader for major market participant. Even after G-20 meeting, trader still expects further depreciation of USD, which will help metals appreciate. Looked at another way, he says, "metals are well supported now" and nothing in G-20 disturbed that. Meanwhile, Triland Metals said Saturday LME 3-month copper tested $8,200/metric ton support twice last week, which therefore gained credibility, while "strong sentiment that still prevails seems to announce further potential gains" this week, it said. Copper closed last week at $8,334, "a strong signal" for this week, it said. On Friday, aluminum added $16 at $2,365, zinc gained $37.50 at $2,511.50, lead rose $45 at $2,529 but tin eased $50 at $26,350, nickel fell $350 to $23,200. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2311 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei may trade between 9350-9550 vs 9426.71 (+0.5%) Friday close with reaction to weekend G-20 meeting likely limited; G-20 results mostly in line with expectations after finance ministers and central bankers agreed on need to avoid "competitive devaluation." "Amid perceived absence of concrete criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market), another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be likely if USD/JPY hits its all-time low (of 79.75)," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Market focus this week on upcoming string of Japanese earnings results with attention on companies' \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  (repatriation) assumptions for FY 2H. Banking shares may outperform on bullish pre-earnings report in Yomiuri Shimbun on Sumitomo Mitsui FG (8316.TO). Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9445 vs Osaka close at 9420. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 10-24-10 1958ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2