\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ 2nd UPDATE: Japan Says Advanced Nations 'More Active' Against  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Volatility\b0\par\par\uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2010\par 13:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds more comments from Noda, background.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister said the statement issued by the Group of 20 industrial and developing nations on Saturday meant that advanced nations will have a "more active" stance against excessive moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.\par \par \uc2 But Yoshihiko Noda left it vague on whether the U.S. and European countries are now more willing to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar drops sharply against the yen.\par \par \uc2 He also said he can't tell whether Tokyo's policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when necessary has support from other nations.\par \par \uc2 Noda's remarks underline Japan's desire to see an end to the yen's meteoric ascent, but uncertainty is likely to remain over the degree of commitment by rich nations to prevent sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 In September \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  undertook a massive Y2 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the rising yen, but has since sat on the sidelines even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated even further to near a record high of Y79.75 against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 In their communique Saturday after a two-day meeting in South Korea, G-20 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors said: "Advanced economies, including those with reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will be vigilant against excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates."\par \par \uc2 Long dogged by relentless gains in the yen, Noda used his news conference to call attention to the language used by the G-20 on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I want the market to value the fact that we were able to forge a certain level of agreement," Noda said. "Until now, we haven't gone beyond acknowledging that excessive volatility or disorderly moves in exchange rates have an negative impact."\par \par \uc2 "Yet, 'vigilant' has a more active meaning, like we will monitor market movements and cooperate as appropriate. We can also interpret it to mean that the relationship between the dollar, the yen and the euro will become more stable," he said\par \par \uc2 Noda also renewed his pledge to take "decisive steps"--Japanese code for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --when he sees the need for such action.\par \par \uc2 But asked whether cooperation by advanced economies would include joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Noda said: "You are fixated on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but 'cooperate when necessary' means various things."\par \par \uc2 Also unclear is whether other G-20 countries endorse \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance.\par \par \uc2 While the G-20 statement spoke of vigilance against rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, it also called for a "move towards more market determined exchange rate systems that reflect underlying economic fundamentals and refrain from competitive devaluation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 "There were no discussions on individual countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy or \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates" during the G-20 meeting, Noda said.\par \par \uc2 "We didn't talk about how Japan or China is doing, so I can't answer the question" on whether other countries back Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, he said.\par \par \uc2 Noda said the Group of Seven nations probably understood his "explanations" on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, but whether traders will agree is unclear.\par \par \uc2 The September action appeared to come without approval of the U.S. or Europe, a person familiar with the matter has recently told Dow Jones Newswires. The move was largely tailored to a domestic audience critical of the inability of Prime Minister Naoto Kan's government to stop the yen's climb, the person said.\par \par \uc2 Noda had been reluctant to authorize \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but faced with intense pressure from Kan's office, particularly from Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshito Sengoku, the finance minister complied and ordered the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the person.\par \par \uc2 Turning to G-20 debates on the feasibility of setting numerical goals to cut current-account imbalances, Noda showed opposition to any fixed targets.\par \par \uc2 "If we set rigid, numerical targets, we would eventually face the question of whether we could carry out a kind of managed trade," Noda said. "I think it is good that we came to an agreement that we will create some sorts of reference figures" to gauge progress rather than establish strict goals, he said.\par \par \uc2 The G-20 promised to "pursue the full range of policies conducive to reducing excessive imbalances and maintaining current account imbalances at sustainable levels." But rather than setting any targets, the group said it would agree on "indicative guidelines."\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 23, 2010 07:43 ET (11:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101023e6an000d3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
