\par\par \b \uc2 MARKETWATCH VIEW: Fistful Of Dollars And Nowhere To Go In Asia\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Callaway   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES COLUMN   \par \uc2 1,304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2010\par 14:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The tasting menu on display outside the Japanese restaurant at the Westin Hotel in Tokyo had all the treats a foodie from San Francisco would expect from a Western business hotel in Japan's capital: fine rare tuna sashimi, fabulous beef and wine pairings for each of the handful of items. Then I saw the price of more than Y53,000, or more than $650.\par \par \uc2 I checked my total available cash for a 10-day business trip to Asia and wasn't even close for just that one dinner. That, and the fact that company policy provides for about $50 a day for meals, sent me over to the sushi bar instead, where five small pieces of Nigiri sushi and a beer blew through a day-and-a-half worth of sustenance, or Y6,400, about $80.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to the front line of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars, where quantitative easing, smothered interest rates and an absurd expectation that a weak dollar will lead to a U.S. economic recovery combine to make U.S. dollars a laughingstock abroad.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the Federal Reserve couldn't care less about tourists in Asia or Europe, especially business tourists, when it's trying to save the U.S. economy from sinking into a 1930s-style, double-dip depression. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war the Fed and the Treasury have started across the world--flooding emerging markets with dollars and berating China for essentially keeping the weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  they'd like to see themselves--couldn't do less for American economic leadership at a time when everybody is looking for a strong example to follow. No country has ever devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prosperity, and that's not going to happen now.\par \par \uc2 My trip to visit MarketWatch bureaus in Tokyo, Hong Kong and Sydney allowed me an opportune time to retest my self-patented, beer-to-taxi exchange-rate theory, which is like The Economist's famous Big Mac Index, but more fun and doesn't require a visit to \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 . Using New York or San Francisco as benchmarks, where the average price of a short taxi ride downtown is about equal to a $6 or $7 beer at an average bar, I always note how the ratio in cities I visit compares with those 1-1 examples.\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, where the ubiquitous red Toyota Crown cabs race the city's winding, elevated streets like a gigantic bobsled ride at a traveling carnival, the ratio is about 1-3. For the price of a beer, about HK$75 (just under $10), you can generally take three cab rides in the financial district.\par \par \uc2 In Sydney, it's the reverse. A taxi downtown costs about A$20 (about $19), or roughly the price of three pints of Victoria Bitter. In Tokyo, however, a 10- to 15-minute cab ride can easily cost about Y4,000, or $50. Even at extraordinary prices for beer of as much as Y1,000, or $11 to $12, that's still four beers per cab ride.\par \par \uc2 The only city close, at least that I've visited, is London, where a black cab can easily cost GBP12 to GBP15. That's about $20, so less than half the price of Tokyo. But given the cheap price of beer in the U.K., about right for a 4-1 ratio. The fact that the cities with the cheapest beer have the most cabs is probably just a coincidence.\par \par \uc2 So with fine dining or any type of shopping out of consideration for my fistful of American dollars, I spent a lot of time walking around talking to people when I wasn't at the bureaus, considering just how the U.S. could right itself from this self-imposed economic exile. A couple of local market strategists I spoke with offered some guidance.\par \par \uc2 John F. Vail, chief global strategist at \uc2 Nikko Asset Management Co\uc2 .'s investment strategy group in Tokyo, said he expects to see more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  maneuvers by countries hard hit by speculative U.S. dollars flooding in, including the possibility of another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Unlike some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts, who blasted the BOJ for what they said was a foolhardy attempt to fight global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows to try to weaken the yen, Vail said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent a strong message to both the markets and to the Japanese government that it won't be pushed around anymore. He didn't speculate on the level at which the BOJ might come back in. At just above Y81 on Friday, the yen is already back below the level the bank first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at, and not far from its historic low of Y79 and change.\par \par \uc2 Whether another move comes before the Group of 20 leaders' summit in Seoul in a few weeks, is anybody's guess. Vail also railed against South Korea, which has continually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  competitive yet attracted none of the attention that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did with its one \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That may change as the summit approaches, and indeed, has already stirred the ire of the Japanese government.\par \par \uc2 Francis Cheung, managing director of China-Hong Kong Strategy at \uc2 CLSA Research Ltd\uc2 . in Hong Kong, said he expects the Seoul summit to be important, but to have little overall effect on the impact on Hong Kong of a flood of Chinese yuan into the city.\par \par \uc2 Because Hong Kong's dollar is pegged to the U.S. dollar at a little less than 8-1, as the U.S. dollar weakens, so does the value of the Hong Kong dollar for anyone looking to spend it across the border in China or elsewhere. That's helped fuel the property boom in Hong Kong by attracting more yuan and raised questions about the viability of the 27-year-old peg.\par \par \uc2 Cheung expects the peg to hold, but he also expects a period of dual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Hong Kong, where soaring yuan deposits and new investment-product offerings in yuan will lead to more and more of a parallel \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system.\par \par \uc2 All of this leaves the U.S. continually in the cold. And in Australia it's no different. Last week's surge in the Aussie dollar to parity against the greenback for the first time since 1982 generated great pride in the commodity-rich country, even though its effect on the economy is somewhat neutral.\par \par \uc2 Gains for importers and for traveling Australians using Aussie dollars to go overseas are offset by more hardships for exporters, though few expect the sucking of Australian mineral assets by China to stop just because the Aussie dollar is a bit higher.\par \par \uc2 Each country feels differently about the impact of the weak U.S. dollar on its economy, but the common denominator is that just about everybody expects it to end badly, a la the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis of 1997. Indeed, the move by China to raise lending and deposit rates earlier in the week, even though a minor maneuver compared with really letting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate, stopped the global commodities rally in its tracks and sank equities around the world, if only for one day.\par \par \uc2 Next day, it was back to normal. But for investors scouring the globe for cheap assets in emerging markets with high yields, business executives looking for inexpensive takeover targets, or tourists searching for food with either weak U.S. dollars or strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it was perhaps a taste of something quite unpleasant yet to come. Courtesy of an unsustainable weak-dollar, low-interest-rate U.S. policy.\par \par \uc2 (David Callaway is editor in chief of MarketWatch. He can be reached at 415-439-6400 or via email at AskNewswires@dowjones.com.) [ 10-22-10 0804ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020101022e6am0004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
